ment","engineering"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000042","summary":"I draw by hand and enjoy artists demonstrating their process. I do not want tutorials or showcases centered on AI-generated images.","topics":["drawing","handmade art","creative process"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000043","summary":"I work in local government and want workable ways to broaden public participation. National reform debates can help if the lessons travel.","topics":["local government","participation","democracy"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000044","summary":"I work with young children with developmental delays. Show me ways families and schools can improve access to support.","topics":["early intervention","developmental needs","access"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000045","summary":"I manage a team and want research-informed ways to support health at work. I am skeptical of advice that treats burnout as poor personal planning.","topics":["workplace health","management","burnout"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000046","summary":"I train for distance races and want coaching methods, recovery, or training evidence. Pure inspiration and unrelated self-help are not enough.","topics":["running","training","recovery","coaching"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000047","summary":"I am curious about space science but need explanations grounded in observation. Please avoid turning physics into motivational advice.","topics":["cosmology","space science","observation"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000048","summary":"I want practical public-health approaches to addiction and prevention. I am especially interested in community conditions and access to care.","topics":["addiction","prevention","public health","community"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000049","summary":"I am planning a career change and want advice on finding work and building useful relationships. I do not need advice on maximizing social-media reach.","topics":["career change","networking","employment"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000050","summary":"I like thoughtful conversations about ethics and living with other people. I would rather hear a difficult question than a productivity formula.","topics":["ethics","relationships","philosophy"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000051","summary":"I collect records and help a local archive preserve music. Show me why ownership and preservation matter, not advice about performing.","topics":["record collecting","media preservation","archives"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000052","summary":"I'm learning paper engineering with my teenager. We want demonstrations we can discuss at the kitchen table, rather than software predictions.","topics":["paper engineering","hands-on projects","family making"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000053","summary":"I'm a nurse educator looking for ways to help patients disclose embarrassing information without feeling judged.","topics":["patient trust","clinical communication","nursing education"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000054","summary":"I manage a small office and want practical ways to support colleagues with chronic conditions. Please avoid telling them to just become more resilient.","topics":["workplace accommodations","chronic conditions","people management"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000055","summary":"I volunteer with a children's charity and want concrete interventions against environmental toxins, especially lead exposure.","topics":["lead exposure","child health interventions","environmental toxins"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000056","summary":"I spend most of my day at a desk. I'd like simple movement habits I can try between meetings without buying equipment.","topics":["desk work","movement breaks","daily habits"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000057","summary":"I teach a community photography class. I want to discuss how an image communicates a point of view, not camera shopping.","topics":["fine art photography","visual meaning","community teaching"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000058","summary":"I'm planning a choir workshop on improvisation. Give me live examples of musicians building something together.","topics":["ensemble improvisation","choir workshops","live collaboration"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000059","summary":"I'm interested in community housing where older and younger residents support each other. I'd like actual examples rather than futuristic city pitches.","topics":["intergenerational housing","mutual support","housing case studies"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000060","summary":"I train volunteers to have respectful conversations about vaccines. I want approaches to trust, not a debate about whether science matters.","topics":["vaccine conversations","science trust","volunteer training"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000061","summary":"I work with small food producers and want to understand affordable ways to add missing nutrients to staple foods.","topics":["food fortification","micronutrients","small food producers"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000062","summary":"I run workshops for student councils. I need ways to include quiet pupils without requiring them to volunteer to speak.","topics":["student councils","inclusive participation","quiet pupils"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000063","summary":"I'm a software researcher interested in proof systems and the limits of AI reliability. High-level technical arguments are welcome.","topics":["formal verification","proof systems","AI reliability"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000064","summary":"I'm trying to understand what astronomical research says about the eventual fate of the universe. I don't need practical self-help.","topics":["cosmology","universe endings","astronomical research"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000065","summary":"I organize neighborhood mutual aid. Show me specific ways to ask for help and mobilize people who aren't already close friends.","topics":["mutual aid","weak ties","community requests"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000066","summary":"I'm researching the business model of independent websites as AI replaces search traffic. I want economics and compensation, not prompt tips.","topics":["web publishing","creator compensation","search traffic"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000067","summary":"I want to understand how climate shocks affect decisions inside families in South Asia, especially girls' futures.","topics":["South Asian families","climate shocks","child marriage"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000068","summary":"I'm a speech coach interested in how voice production can be learned. Please give me explanations beyond being confident.","topics":["voice production","speech coaching","vocal training"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000069","summary":"I make documentary exhibits and want examples of using spatial capture to tell stories about disaster recovery.","topics":["spatial capture","documentary exhibits","disaster reporting"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000070","summary":"I'm interested in how artists make environmental problems tangible. Show me installations or physical experiences, not only charts.","topics":["environmental art","installations","public engagement"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000071","summary":"I help parents of toddlers navigate developmental support. I want talks about service access and workforce gaps.","topics":["early intervention","developmental support","service capacity"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000072","summary":"I care about the economics that delay medical treatments. Show me research incentives and institutional obstacles, not personal wellness routines.","topics":["medical innovation","research incentives","treatment access"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000073","summary":"I want examples of sneaker designers turning an idea into a surprising physical product. Brand history alone isn't enough.","topics":["sneaker design","product storytelling","creative risk"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000074","summary":"I coordinate a peer tutoring club. I'd like examples of students learning through relationships with other students.","topics":["peer tutoring","student relationships","learning communities"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000075","summary":"I want to hear from people who have built a sense of self outside a demanding job. Hobbies and ordinary life matter more to me than promotions.","topics":["identity outside work","serious leisure","career transition"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000076","summary":"I'm helping a town decide whether to expand public cameras. I want competing arguments and enough detail to question vendors.","topics":["municipal cameras","vendor scrutiny","public accountability"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000077","summary":"I'm considering getting a health tracker, but I want a balanced account of false alarms and limitations before buying one.","topics":["health trackers","measurement limits","consumer decisions"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000078","summary":"I'm planning retirement and wondering how to find structure without a work identity. Advice aimed at people my age would help.","topics":["retirement transition","daily structure","purpose after work"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000079","summary":"I'm designing a science museum activity about satellite observations. I'd prefer enough detail to turn the idea into an exhibit.","topics":["Earth observation","museum activities","science exhibits"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000080","summary":"I'm a piano teacher looking for exercises that help beginners improvise together. Expert performances can be hard to adapt.","topics":["beginner piano","group improvisation","teaching exercises"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000081","summary":"I'm comparing ways local residents can influence budgets. I'd like concrete participation processes, not just encouragement to get involved.","topics":["participatory budgeting","resident voice","local processes"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000082","summary":"I'm trying to reduce compulsive phone checking as an adult. I want ideas that respect online friendships rather than a total digital detox.","topics":["adult phone habits","online friendships","screen dependence"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000083","summary":"I facilitate discussions for people dealing with bereavement. I want sensitive first-person stories, but not promises that loss is a gift.","topics":["bereavement groups","first-person stories","sensitive facilitation"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000084","summary":"I'm starting a small nonprofit and need help asking strangers for support without sounding transactional.","topics":["nonprofit outreach","asking for help","relationship building"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000085","summary":"I'm exploring solo parenthood and want candid accounts of the decision, including practical tradeoffs.","topics":["solo parenthood","family choices","lived experience"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000086","summary":"I teach teenagers about financial risk and want material about gambling that avoids glamorizing wins.","topics":["gambling literacy","teen financial risk","harm prevention"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000087","summary":"I want to understand whether AI can assist neonatal care. I prefer validation evidence over prototype excitement.","topics":["neonatal technology","clinical validation","care tools"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000088","summary":"I'm helping a school plan its AI policy. I want decisions staff can make now, not claims that all teaching will change.","topics":["school AI policy","staff decisions","classroom technology"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000089","summary":"I work in tourism and want methods for managing visitor pressure that protect residents. Technology is useful only if the tradeoffs are explained.","topics":["visitor management","resident impact","tourism tradeoffs"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000090","summary":"I'm taking up drawing again and want something I can try immediately. Please avoid turning art into another productivity goal.","topics":["beginner drawing","creative practice","low-pressure hobbies"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000091","summary":"I support first-generation engineering students through financial emergencies. I want examples of programs that help them finish their degrees.","topics":["engineering access","student emergency support","degree completion"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000092","summary":"I'm interested in privacy-preserving European digital infrastructure. I want concrete alternatives as well as criticism of dependency.","topics":["European infrastructure","digital sovereignty","privacy alternatives"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000093","summary":"I want to understand depression treatment research without being told a single explanation solves everything.","topics":["depression research","treatment mechanisms","scientific uncertainty"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000094","summary":"I'm a first-time manager trying to improve handoffs between remote colleagues. Give me concrete communication practices.","topics":["remote handoffs","team communication","new managers"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000095","summary":"I want to know whether clean energy has become affordable in poorer countries. Please distinguish generation prices from implementation barriers.","topics":["energy affordability","development constraints","implementation costs"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000096","summary":"I'm organizing a discussion about fairness in public services. I like arguments that connect ethical principles to institutional design.","topics":["public service fairness","ethical reasoning","institutional choices"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000097","summary":"I want to prepare a community talk about health inequity. I need approachable examples, but I don't want to present a personal story as proof.","topics":["health inequity communication","community education","evidence literacy"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000098","summary":"I lead an adult book group and want to discuss how technology changes our relationship with culture. We enjoy concrete examples more than predictions.","topics":["book groups","cultural ownership","technology discussion"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000099","summary":"I'm training volunteer group leaders to keep people engaged during long projects. I want advice that can work without workplace authority.","topics":["volunteer retention","long projects","shared motivation"]},{"id":"30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000100","summary":"I'm planning a reflective discussion for adults who feel stuck after a major life change. I want room for uncertainty rather than a quick reinvention formula.","topics":["life transitions","reflective discussion","uncertainty"]}],"schema_version":2,"scoring_model":"GPT-6","scoring_model_version":"assistant-curation-2026-09-04-v2","snapshot_id":"c7a395c7-ff55-5083-bb57-6fe23e0c2482","snapshot_sha256":"bdd48841fffdfcac37672c12718d7ad51acce754d6aed3f50bd6649c993e1741","source_commit":"c6ee11e40d38eb02e05dd918f9ee1fb5d2eb5054","source_migrations":["0001_create_monitors.sql","0002_add_monitor_name.sql","0003_finite_feed_core.sql","0004_recommendation_pipeline.sql","0005_telegram_bot_deduplication.sql","0006_genre_specific_profile.sql","0007_delivery_idempotency.sql","0008_admin_operations.sql","0008_human_annotation_lab.sql","0008_semantic_embeddings.sql","0009_global_channel_ownership.sql","0010_video_language_metadata.sql","0011_match_lab_curated_queue.sql"],"videos":[{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e351285662017788c62aa1a22dddaf6f221a35a215445b1c6b13d726aeb5f23f","description":"What would it take to build a democracy that works for everyone? Communications strategist Sally Kohn and entrepreneur Jeff Maurer discuss the systems they think need rebuilding — and what people will need to truly thrive within them. They debate whether politicians should face a wealth limit, what success should mean for those in office and whether the next generation will have more opportunities than the last. (This episode is part of “Idea Knock Down,” a series featuring experts with different perspectives taking on big questions — one block at a time.) (Recorded on June 12, 2026)\n\n0:00 - Idea Knock Down\n1:06 - Democracy for everyday people\n1:26 - Wealth limit for politicians?\n2:41 - The next generation\n3:38 - What should we rebuild?\n4:16 - Is authoritarianism winning?\n5:07 - Overused word in politics?\n6:15 - Hope for the future\n7:33 - Is privacy still possible?\n8:24 - A version of success with no losers\n9:23 - What would you tell a 15-year-old?\n10:03 - Mystery challenge\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nhttps://www.ted.com/series/idea_knock_down\n\nhttps://youtu.be/bXekc44oCM0\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Democracy","duration_seconds":716,"id":"0086f1ef-ed23-46cd-8c0c-5fcb874f3e84","published_at":"2026-06-30T15:00:06+00:00","title":"Should There Be a Wealth Limit for Politicians? | Idea Knock Down | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"bXekc44oCM0"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"68c464ad72a4b3ef002aeace116d12d31dae757fe8022c74798e27d46f922de7","description":"Pew Research Center president Michael Dimock has spent years listening to what Americans think — and what they're telling him right now is pretty dark. But buried in the data is something revealing: people are ready for bold democratic reform that the country hasn’t seen in decades. He explores how America's founders didn't create a finished system of government but rather launched an ongoing project that requires each generation to renew, adapt and reimagine democracy. The survey says Americans are ready to do the work.\n (Recorded at TED Democracy Philadelphia on June 13, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/michaeldimock\n\nhttps://youtu.be/pRiu3lxPZAw\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Democracy","duration_seconds":850,"id":"01407a62-aac4-4dd4-b728-559b60657f2d","published_at":"2026-07-04T15:00:01+00:00","title":"Why Democracy Requires Renewal | Michael Dimock | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"pRiu3lxPZAw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"3bc5b0945889b7407c61266a29d0d5c0b7465609f531b0b282a4d3af38669431","description":"Assoc. Prof. Hristo Chukurliev examines a fundamental mistake in Bulgaria’s education system and its impact on students, teachers, and society. The talk explores how education can move beyond memorization and place greater emphasis on critical thinking, meaningful learning, and modern approaches to assessment. Assoc. Prof. Hristo Chukurliev, PhD, is a faculty member in the Pedagogy Section of the Department of Foreign Languages and Cultures at New Bulgarian University. His academic interests include management models, digital transformation, the history of civilizations and religions, and the evolution of approaches to understanding and managing social processes. In higher education, his work focuses on transforming teaching methods and aligning education with the professions of the future.\n\nHe has served as Director of NBU’s Central University Administration since 2018 and has held various leadership positions at the university since 2002. He has been a member of the NBU Board of Trustees since 2016. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":539,"id":"0179d2e6-d03b-4b2c-a09a-3714293347ac","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:08:53+00:00","title":"What Mistake Did Bulgaria’s Education System Make? | Hristo Chukurliev | TEDxNBU","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"jIOdVEwIPqM"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"16e3ab70ff2697d4560f4515204972621fca037349cf6d47dcec7fee5881e694","description":"Space technologist Robbie Schingler cofounded a satellite company that images the whole planet every day, making visible changes on Earth. He shows how pairing that data with AI creates a \"planetary intelligence\" we can use to spot emerging threats we might not have seen otherwise. Now he asks: Can we close the gap between seeing a crisis coming and actually stopping it? (Followed by a note from TED guest curators Divya Siddarth and Audrey Tang) (Recorded at TED2026 on April 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/robbieschingler\n\nhttps://youtu.be/fd_TsNUpPWw\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Tech #ClimateChange","duration_seconds":723,"id":"01d73e24-112d-4b11-8bd9-a35c34e35422","published_at":"2026-08-20T15:00:37+00:00","title":"How Satellites and AI Can Protect the Planet | Robbie Schingler | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"fd_TsNUpPWw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"b2424e08d988d4efba9fad6c870133bf39c7b5cec71de8516ae046466082c39c","description":"Chiara Borgia affronta uno dei confini più delicati dell'essere adulti: quello tra il desiderio di proteggere i bambini e la necessità di dire loro la verità.\nAttraverso esempi concreti, il contributo della neuropedagogia e della Death Education, mostra come il silenzio e le bugie non proteggono davvero i più piccoli, ma li privano degli strumenti per dare senso alle proprie emozioni.\nIl suo talk è un invito ad abitare l'incertezza insieme ai bambini, restando accanto a loro con ascolto, autenticità e fiducia nelle loro risorse. Si occupa di sostegno alla genitorialità, accompagnando genitori, bambine e bambini nei passaggi delicati della crescita.\nÈ specializzata in Neuropedagogia e Death Education ed è operatrice Touchpoints Brazelton. Direttrice responsabile di Uppa e autrice di testi divulgativi dedicati all’infanzia e all’educazione. Svolge attività di formazione con professionisti di ambiti diversi, promuovendo uno sguardo integrato e transdisciplinare nel lavoro educativo e di cura. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":999,"id":"01f7773e-3b18-4dc9-8788-5dd66a5d4877","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:40:41+00:00","title":"Quando i bambini restano soli | Chiara Borgia | TEDxMessina","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"z03FTx8sEtI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"68d356fd4741103ca9f41aff9b102791d5e9250c4852cff738fb0facb21a5844","description":"¿Sabías que no somos lo que nos pasó, sino lo que elegimos hacer con aquello que vivimos? En esta conmovedora presentación, María Jesús Fernández explica cómo podemos sanar y convertirnos en los verdaderos constructores de nuestro propio relato de vida. A través de su experiencia de supervivencia, nos invita a mirarnos con más compasión y a recordar que nunca estamos solos y que somos muchos más de los que creemos. María Jesús Fernández Larraín es Ingeniera Comercial de la Pontificia Universidad Católica de Chile, con especialización en marketing y ventas. Actualmente se desempeña como profesora del curso \"Mercados 1 - Fundamentos del Marketing\" en la Facultad de Economía y Administración UC. Cuenta con más de una década de trayectoria en SKY Airlines, donde ha desarrollado su carrera en el área comercial. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1226,"id":"028f9196-c9d7-4517-9338-e97f68cde701","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:58:58+00:00","title":"Somos más de lo(s) que creemos | María Jesús Fernández | TEDxPUC de Chile","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"BLKUFYp7REs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"923e7b74ad6e6d44647729fbf80876930ebe1ff66cf370dcae2e6e721d586c9e","description":"NOTE FROM TED: TEDx events are independently organized by volunteers. The guidelines we give TEDx organizers are described in more detail here: http://storage.ted.com/tedx/manuals/tedx_content_guidelines.pdf\n\nWhat if the fast growing cities around the world could also heal their communities?\n\nTony Cho's talk explores building cities and spaces that prioritize community, sustainability, and long-term impact. Tony Cho is an entrepreneur, community builder, and leading voice in regenerative placemaking. A catalyst of Miami’s urban renaissance, he played a key role in transforming the Wynwood Arts District and founded the Magic City Innovation District, helping position Miami as a global hub for creativity and innovation.\n\nAs the Founder of Future of Cities, Tony continues to advance community-centered developments worldwide that activate creativity, restore ecosystems, and shape future-ready cities. Through education, storytelling, and hands-on demonstration of regenerative principles, he brings “ecosystems thinking” to life at projects such as the Climate & Innovation HUB in Miami, ChoZen Center for Regenerative Living in Sebastian, Florida, and the Phoenix Arts & Innovation District in Jacksonville, Florida. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":902,"id":"04376975-2f96-468d-ac12-edd2fcc6b58d","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:00:28+00:00","title":"A new vision for Miami | Tony Cho | TEDxMiami","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"CrHIwd8Mrzw"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"f9268f4d555a9055aebd911252b43c383d79de739b41277c14a936fae3f6c93e","description":"Humans aren't just social — we're ultrasocial, wired like bees and ants for deep connection. So what happens when smartphones take over childhood, tablets replace textbooks and AI companies infiltrate our kids’ lives? Social psychologist Jonathan Haidt lays out three principles of technoskepticism — and explains why, two years after sounding the alarm in \"The Anxious Generation,\" he's more concerned (and hopeful) than ever before.","duration_seconds":135,"id":"049def95-1183-4f85-9978-156628e8f2fe","published_at":"2026-07-12T19:00:28+00:00","title":"The best classroom upgrade might be less tech #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"9RHxDzSMVwg"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5915ca6b2bb16b3baedc200e705fffdc92ae30bf42320d7e138cf474ab8beccd","description":"At a time when our lives are increasingly shaped by algorithms, Camille Carlton's insightful talk explores a critical question: Who is technology really serving?\n\nCamille makes the case for technology designed in the public interest- not just for growth, but also for impact.\n\n Camille is Senior Director of Strategy and Impact at the Center for Humane Technology, where she works to ensure AI is guided by the public interest. Recognized as one of Business Insider's AI 100, she shapes national debates through litigation, legislative engagement, and public narrative strategies that translate complex technology issues into public action. With a background spanning public policy, industry, and academic research, she brings a cross-disciplinary lens to the most urgent technology questions of our time. Her work has been featured in The New York Times, Bloomberg, NBC News, MIT, and more. She holds a master's from the London School of Economics, where she studied technology and development. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":847,"id":"04af39e6-a880-4864-a1b9-4a4eb9890123","published_at":"2026-09-01T16:19:39+00:00","title":"The Future of  Human Meaning | Camille Carlton | TEDxMiami","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"92Aol5ssKzs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"7151aa3418de82807dc3799c63b1dc32498a3552d8b209dc0151f309c5217112","description":"Em sua palestra, Alê aborda sobre como podemos podemos ser mais felizes e aumentar nossa capacidade de amar, sob a perspectiva de suas experiências de vida. Alê Calderaro é um Advogado \"Aposentado\", Multiplicador dos Objetivos do Desenvolvimento Sustentável da ONU, formado em Business com foco em Sustentabilidade.\n\nEm sua talk, aborda sobre como podemos podemos ser mais felizes e aumentar nossa capacidade de amar, sob a perspectiva de suas experiências de vida. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":864,"id":"060fc68d-9193-4a10-99d0-8e43d12708ec","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:15:36+00:00","title":"Ser feliz e amar mais | Alê Calderaro | TEDxMaringáStudio","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"KxUdD5OZUEM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e717f72a0268ac11746b4f23817f0f6e7880eb42826b61774dd9e8c0a17086ae","description":"For generations, we've celebrated the loudest voices as the strongest leaders. We've been taught that confidence looks like speaking first, speaking often, and commanding every room we enter.\n\nBut what if we've been measuring influence all wrong?\n\nIn this talk, Michelle Gines introduces the concept of the Quiet Extrovert, someone who is energized by people yet leads through observation, intentionality, and meaningful connection rather than constant visibility. Drawing from her own experiences in leadership, publishing, and personal transformation, she shares how quiet influence often creates deeper trust, stronger relationships, and more sustainable impact than attention alone.\n\nUsing her M.O.R.E. Framework, Michelle offers a practical approach for recognizing and embracing a leadership style that has too often been overlooked. Rather than encouraging people to become someone they're not, she invites them to become more fully themselves.\n\nThis talk challenges the belief that influence belongs only to the loudest voices and offers a compelling alternative: authentic leadership built on clarity, presence, and purposeful action. Michelle Gines is a leadership strategist, publisher, and thought leader dedicated to helping accomplished professionals transform their expertise into lasting authority and meaningful impact. Throughout her career, she has worked alongside executives, entrepreneurs, healthcare leaders, ministry leaders, and authors, helping them uncover the wisdom within their experiences and communicate it with clarity and purpose.\n\nAs the founder of Purpose Publishing, Michelle has guided the publication of hundreds of books and has witnessed firsthand that the greatest value people possess often remains hidden beneath years of experience. This realization shaped her belief that true leadership is not about becoming louder but about becoming more intentional.\n\nHer work now centers on helping individuals recognize their unique influence, build trusted authority, and create legacies that extend beyond titles or accomplishments. Through speaking, coaching, publishing, and strategic consulting, Michelle encourages others to lead with authenticity, quiet confidence, and purpose. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":509,"id":"06390d78-f956-41d1-a754-c434863901e1","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:13:55+00:00","title":"The Quite Extrovert Advantage: Say Less, Become M.O.R.E. | Michelle Gines | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"CFZ0UoQy7Ww"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"a896680373ddee6748748d6ed2e315ae6da255dcbbd6bccd62d030ad86951681","description":"Citizen strategist Jon Alexander spent a decade gathering stories of democracy in action — and found that most change doesn't come from politicians or institutions. He shares examples from across the globe of ordinary people working together to improve their lives and communities, showing why the key to fixing things may already be in your own hands. (Recorded at TED Democracy Philadelphia on June 13, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/jonalexander\n\nhttps://youtu.be/tXZs2NuLFRA\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Democracy","duration_seconds":586,"id":"06f25ce5-4deb-4515-b062-81ab720cfa9a","published_at":"2026-08-25T15:00:37+00:00","title":"How Citizens (Not Politicians) Are Building the Future | Jon Alexander | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"tXZs2NuLFRA"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"29440199cef677d71bee2d487f0aabecd4fb143e39c7bd00a826d3b1ca233548","description":"Control is an illusion — and the leaders who chase it are holding their teams back. Workplace culture expert Jessica Kriegel explores the tactic that leaders who want to achieve extraordinary results should try instead. (Recorded at TEDxSouthlake on November 9, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/jessicakriegel]\n\nhttps://youtu.be/CXDObEQ2Dv0\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Business","duration_seconds":731,"id":"06fe03dc-3f2c-4736-baf4-09f0ce08bc60","published_at":"2026-07-12T15:00:38+00:00","title":"The Counterintuitive Secret of Leadership | Jessica Kriegel | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"CXDObEQ2Dv0"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"75c8189e432e638237d9053ac1d9159cdfbbb7647668a6409e6afccc31557493","description":"Want to become a K-pop superstar? Comedian and musician Charlene Kaye lays out the formula for breaking through in just a few easy steps, from rapping like a sexy baby to dancing like you’re making pizza in the Matrix. Part musical, part dance performance, part comedy show — this is your how-to guide for creating the next K-pop hit.","duration_seconds":108,"id":"096a9270-db43-454e-89a0-bff20e2d3f56","published_at":"2026-09-01T19:00:12+00:00","title":"The key to a K-pop hit? Vocal fry and fruit innuendos #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ROgs7a3wt2Y"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"6e8c895e3dd7124fef251c913a4f432449eb045df76b9ea4d402616fe476d170","description":"What if being a good patient doesn't mean being a quiet one? \n\nAfter years of medical dismissal before receiving a diagnosis of Stage IV endometriosis, licensed marriage and family therapist Aaliyah Maura realized that patient self-advocacy isn't about distrusting medical professionals- it's about becoming an informed, collaborative partner in your own healthcare decisions. In this powerful and deeply personal TEDx Talk, Aaliyah shares how learning to ask questions, recognize red and green flags in healthcare providers, and remain equally invested in her medical decisions ultimately changed the course of her life. \n\nThrough compelling storytelling and practical insight, she challenges the belief that patients should blindly trust authority, encourages patients to re-evaluate the patient-doctor relationship, offering a compelling perspective on why advocating for yourself may be the most important decision a patient can make. While inspired by her personal health journey, Aaliyah's message extends beyond the exam room, reminding us that the courage to advocate for ourselves has the power to transform every area of our life.  Aaliyah Maura, M.S., LMFT, is a licensed marriage and family therapist whose work explores the psychology of self-advocacy and the hidden barriers that keep people from using their voice.\n\nAfter years of navigating unexplained symptoms before being diagnosed with Stage IV endometriosis, Aaliyah became passionate about helping people become informed, collaborative partners in their own healthcare. What began as a personal journey evolved into a broader exploration of why speaking up can feel so difficult—not only in medical settings, but in relationships, the workplace, leadership, and everyday life.\n\nThroughout her career, Aaliyah has worked with individuals, couples, families, and survivors of domestic violence, helping people navigate life's most complex relationships and experiences with greater confidence and authenticity. Today, she serves as a behavioral health clinician within a primary care clinic, where she helps patients process the emotional impact of chronic health conditions, major lifestyle changes, and the challenges that often accompany long-term wellness. Working alongside primary care providers has deepened her understanding of the powerful connection between physical health, mental health, and the quality of the relationships people have with their healthcare teams.\n\nHer insights have been featured in Essence and The Knot, and she has appeared on multiple podcasts discussing relationships, emotional health, and self-advocacy. Through both her clinical work and speaking, Aaliyah is passionate about exploring why using our voice can feel so difficult—and what becomes possible when we understand the barriers beneath it.\n\nOutside of her professional work, Aaliyah enjoys traveling, discovering new restaurants, attending live concerts, and spending time with the people she loves. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":713,"id":"09b12ac2-d88b-46e5-be98-3fe5672d128c","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:39:08+00:00","title":"Why you need to advocate for yourself at the doctor's office | Aaliyah Maura | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"TG420roVSo8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"f27b6225e69608a8f524d0536af716bd87ee4fb01c60f4b95e00582fb26e8168","description":"\"Free your mind.\" — Three simple words from the 1999 movie The Matrix set actor and transformational coach Shruthi Seth on a 20-year quest to discover true happiness.\n\nSpeaking live at TEDxSFIT, Shruthi breaks down why modern life feels like a treadmill of endless chasing—and how you can break free from the \"matrix\" of external expectations.\n\nKey Highlights:\n- The blueprint society hands us\n- The Matrix quote that set off an internal alarm\n- Understanding Hedonistic Adaptation\n- How the ego keeps you trapped\n- Choosing your feelings vs. your actions\n- Reclaiming your internal power Shruti Seth is a versatile Indian actor, VJ, and entrepreneur who became a household name as the lead in the cult-classic fantasy series Shararat and as the long-standing host of Comedy Circus. Transitioning seamlessly from her early days as a prominent Channel V face to appearing in major films like Fanaa and Raajneeti, she has recently garnered acclaim in the digital space with series like Mentalhood and Bloody Brothers. Beyond the screen, she has established herself as a creative entrepreneur and a strong voice in the parenting community as the co-founder of \"The Mummy Party,\" a curated pop-up market for mothers and children. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":926,"id":"0ba2b957-264f-4fc9-8168-2f7e6d13f1a6","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:08:06+00:00","title":"Free Your Mind: How to Break Free From Society's Blueprint | Shruti Seth | TEDxSFIT","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"zdGUZA6WtGk"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"194b763a94f61aef398bdd007d743632a779ccb5c7f71cc47e394d80f44305ee","description":"Garrett Langley founded Flock because of a basic frustration: it was too easy to get away with crime in the US. His solution — a network of license plate readers, cameras, drones and audio sensors — has changed how police departments investigate crime, while also raising urgent questions about safety, surveillance and privacy. In this talk, Langley addresses the controversy around this widely debated technology and makes the case for why it will make the world safer. (Followed by a Q&A with TED Chairman Chris Anderson and a note from TED guest curator Bilawal Sidhu) (Recorded at TED2026 on April 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/garrettlangley\n\nhttps://youtu.be/v0BiDmPloBs\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Surveillance","duration_seconds":912,"id":"0c5f3f18-b53b-4636-885f-784d501f9062","published_at":"2026-07-17T15:00:07+00:00","title":"Can a Camera on Every Corner Make Us Safer? | Garrett Langley | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"v0BiDmPloBs"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"9c0d185f8242758e63021affa0543d1d2d615e41ff98f6eafc339d461461dcbf","description":"Can you stand up for what you believe in without participating in politics? Anti-career coach Danielle Roberts and political rule breaker Katie Paris discuss how to rethink power, work and civic life on your own terms. They explore how rest, community and meaningful participation can help you stay engaged in the issues shaping your life. (This conversation is part of “Idea Knock Down,” a series where two people with different perspectives take on big questions — one block at a time.) (Recorded on June 12, 2026)\n\n0:00 - Idea Knock Down\n1:08 - Is participating in politics necessary? \n1:29 - What is democracy?\n1:58 - The power of one conversation\n3:09 - Inviting someone into politics\n4:03 - Is social media good for democracy?\n4:41 - Opting out of politics\n5:11 - Abolishing the 40-hour work week\n6:13 - Joy as a form of resistance\n6:48 - Do people want to connect?\n7:30 - Feeling guilty for resting\n9:03 - Can young people fix what's broken?\n10:01 - Is compromise a sign of weakness?\n10:46 - Mystery challenge\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nhttps://www.ted.com/series/idea_knock_down\n\nhttps://youtu.be/oLOFkOxoPaU\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Democracy","duration_seconds":748,"id":"0d152163-328a-4a77-bb92-a354f7dfa15a","published_at":"2026-07-14T15:00:26+00:00","title":"Is Opting Out of Politics a Political Choice? | Idea Knock Down | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"oLOFkOxoPaU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"69509b55be8dbc4f0f7fc7c278228f4768ee7728e71a7ce26fea16080f03829f","description":"Allen tells his story of escaping external labels and overcoming prejudice. Quoting the motto “Just Do”, he reflects: “Growth is not meeting others’ expectations, but breaking away from them.” G12 student from Jinrui School Shanghai This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":748,"id":"0d5c1ee5-f8a6-41d9-af3a-9d0a81f2655a","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:19:14+00:00","title":"Tear Off “Invisible Labels”  | Allen Cao | TEDxJRSS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"VfM2C1gMDSk"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"d1c72e2ec5e0904ab61fcea837f90357242d1cae61155d45647eddad9313145f","description":"O que perdemos quando deixamos de cozinhar e passamos apenas a consumir? A partir das memórias de uma infância cercada por hortas, receitas de família e encontros ao redor do fogão, Katleen Lacerda reflete sobre como a pressa, a publicidade e os alimentos ultraprocessados estão transformando nossa relação com a comida. Unindo gastronomia, cultura e experiência pessoal, ela defende que comer bem é mais do que nutrir o corpo: é preservar histórias, fortalecer comunidades e exercer um direito. Um convite para recuperarmos a consciência sobre o que colocamos no prato e reconhecermos a cozinha como espaço de afeto, sustentabilidade e resistência. Katleen Lacerda é chef de cozinha, empreendedora e pesquisadora dos sabores que atravessam gerações. Inspirada pelas memórias construídas ao redor da mesa, transformou a herança culinária de sua família em uma trajetória marcada pela valorização da cultura, da identidade e das histórias que vivem na comida. Seu trabalho une técnica, criatividade e afeto para mostrar que cada receita carrega muito mais do que ingredientes: carrega lembranças, tradições e formas de enxergar o mundo. Por meio da gastronomia, Katleen convida as pessoas a redescobrirem o poder das memórias que alimentam quem somos. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":774,"id":"0da57c9f-4306-4232-91c9-2929f1442801","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:12:21+00:00","title":"Conhecendo e reconhecendo sabores | Katleen Lacerda | TEDxAraraquara","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"08UTIg6I36w"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"1f78b39c002dfa2e6c989e94647e1aab87740c2e7321a14e19a0a98d6e831716","description":"We're living through oil wars, wildfire smoke and extreme weather driven by a warming planet. But environmentalist Bill McKibben says one thing is finally going right: the price of clean energy has plunged, making it cheaper than ever to power the world without burning fossil fuels. He breaks down how energy from the sun became the best option on Earth — and why this narrow window of time may be our last real shot at a different future. \"Energy from heaven, not from hell,\" he says.","duration_seconds":87,"id":"0e2f8c7d-8246-4dd9-acbb-8cce1a397ae0","published_at":"2026-08-30T19:00:18+00:00","title":"It is cheaper than ever to power the world without burning fossil fuels #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"SSSErhHac8Q"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"312da34c1b5fde15324b74e812d0a93335803e23af0c2236370bfd5c3d6268a8","description":"Role as a Bureaucrat in Ground level Governance Sub Collector,Tiruvallur - I.A.S (2024),Tamilnadu This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":751,"id":"0e5c57c4-ac52-49fa-9565-ce6d9208d948","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:48:33+00:00","title":"From Gold to Governance | Dr Prashanth S | TEDxSRIT","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ts896TzmFOw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"726639aecdfb5d69c8ee4001a24f400fdbe29129eb622b0f37f09dc3c78e7c16","description":"In-person human engagement, the original face time, is more important that ever in today's digital age.  Take a chance on human connection, pay attention to what's personally important to others, show up in person. Mark Agnew builds businesses and personal performance by mastering the art of showing up — using partnership, relationship, and narrative to help teams, leaders and individuals unlock creativity and perform at their best. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":468,"id":"0f32ab40-dfe1-4dcb-8b2e-5ab829969c46","published_at":"2026-09-01T16:17:56+00:00","title":"The lost art of showing up in person | Mark Agnew | TEDxRaleigh","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"YidzmlTSv5Q"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c8c958b073a4738eca2e7ef972b0794063d5ce8e4186777ff4451d2355ebabb9","description":"Carol realizou expedições por mais de 20 países, incluindo o Acampamento Base do Everest, um laboratório para compreender comportamento humano, resiliência e tomada de decisão em cenários de alta complexidade. Dessa vivência nasceu a Arquitetura da Pausa, metodologia que convida todos a enxergar a pausa não como interrupção, mas como estratégia para ampliar consciência, fortalecer escolhas e construir carreiras sustentáveis. Com mais de 20 anos de experiência em empresas como Microsoft, Google e Grupo Pão de Açúcar, Carol Rocha construiu uma carreira unindo estratégia, desenvolvimento de pessoas e transformação de negócios. Ao longo de sua trajetória, liderou equipes multidisciplinares e iniciativas de Equidade, Diversidade e Inclusão, sempre conectando resultados sustentáveis ao potencial humano.\n\nCoautora dos livros Mulheres do Varejo e Carreira é Ação, encontrou no cicloturismo — com expedições por mais de 20 países, incluindo o Acampamento Base do Everest — um laboratório para compreender comportamento humano, resiliência e tomada de decisão em cenários de alta complexidade.\n\nDessa vivência nasceu a Arquitetura da Pausa, metodologia que convida líderes e profissionais a enxergar a pausa não como interrupção, mas como estratégia para ampliar consciência, fortalecer escolhas e construir carreiras mais sustentáveis. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":828,"id":"0f7765c8-07e6-40d8-aba1-c901a7823d3e","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:43:19+00:00","title":"Pausa também é caminho | Carol Rocha | TEDxEloy Chaves Women","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"v-1XnhAv708"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"1459b8c10891bf5afbd53dc85f3562821a7b5a45be7acfb11c2a60801b2dec23","description":"July expands the conversation on “boundary‑breaking” from individual experience to wider social stories. She shares her grandmother’s story of living for herself starting at 60, drawing links to trailblazing women such as Zhang Guimei and Marie Curie who broke down gender barriers. JRSS This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":760,"id":"0f91e663-a3aa-4e76-8ca4-fa3888d92ad4","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:17:01+00:00","title":"Breaking free from the cocoon | July Jiang | TEDxJRSS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"W3NmzS2zYoQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"6d7782e8bbe5613b88bf1da57c831345b27c06394c2d500d5028038e132d9ab4","description":"NOTE FROM TED: This talk only represents the speaker's personal approach to and understanding of mindset, healing, and the brain. TEDx events are independently organized by volunteers. The guidelines we give TEDx organizers are described in more detail here: http://storage.ted.com/tedx/manuals/tedx_content_guidelines.pdf\n\nMichael's Talk introduces us to the different factors in our lives that may be either protecting us or rewarding us, and how to navigate them and utilise each one for personal betterment.  Michael Saletta is a globally recognized authority on leadership, mindset, and human\nperformance – and one of the most operationally diversified leadership consultants in the world.\n\nKnown as “The Master Facilitator,” he has spent over 35 years working inside the operations of\nthousands of organizations across industries – from Fortune 500 companies and government\nagencies to entrepreneurial ventures and elite teams – impacting more than 300,000 individuals worldwide. His proprietary, science-backed frameworks – including Brain C.R.A.V.E.S.™ and Choice Mindset™ – are rooted in two decades of NeuroLeadership research. He is the founder of Saletta Leadership and author of the forthcoming book Choice Mindset. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1030,"id":"11f7960a-8ea1-4226-9aaf-7c2f44c88e41","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:42:05+00:00","title":"Choice Mindset vs. Positive Attitude | Michael Saletta | TEDxUAlberta","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ISaFPBW6LR8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"defa55805c6af171a52ef7db2911ff39708c7388a33ccb315c9f024143449324","description":"We believe growth comes from constantly\nchasing new sparks. So we behave like\ncorporate arsonists — lighting match after\n\nmatch. New project. New pivot. New trend. New\nsoftware. New hype.\nWe’re so busy striking the next match that we\nnever stop to feed the fire we already lit.\nThe result? Burned-out teams, exhausted\nfounders, and graveyards full of half-built ideas.\n\nGrowth doesn’t die from lack of ideas, just as a\nfire doesn’t die from lack of flame, they die from\nlack of oxygen. It dies from our refusal to do the\nunsexy, uneasy, disciplined work that turns a\nbright beginning into a lasting blaze. Attai Winner is a visionary Agri-fintech\nEntrepreneur, Engineer, and Financial markets\npractitioner committed to reimagining Africa’s\nfood systems.\nWith a degree in Marine Engineering, Attai brings\nanalytical precision, systems thinking, and\noperational discipline to one of the continent’s\nmost critical challenges: building resilient and\ninclusive agricultural value chains. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":891,"id":"135275c3-bbf5-4fb9-95ed-046a031d28aa","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:55:55+00:00","title":"Fueling the Future | Winner Attai | TEDxSamaru","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"mT4vK0ZPS90"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"b1632de3442d146bc352ca6e63f96fcb046486cf54bf18d317568d0ce1b292d0","description":"What if ancient myths could help us understand modern emotions? Through the stories of Yu the Great, Nüwa, and Jingwei, this talk explores how Eastern myths can give shape to feelings that are difficult to put into words, including pressure, self-doubt, and the emotional weight behind constant effort. Instead of treating myths as distant moral lessons, the talk presents them as an emotional language that can help us better understand what is happening inside us. Selina Lu is a high school student whose work explores the connection between mythology, psychology, and teenage emotional experience. Her independent research, Deconstructing and Reshaping “Archetypes”: How Chinese Mythological IP Character Design Fosters Critical Cultural Identity in Adolescents, examines how mythological figures can shape the way young people understand identity and culture. Building on this interest, she created a healing handbook that uses Eastern myths as symbolic tools for understanding pressure, self-doubt, and emotional complexity. Through research, design, and storytelling, Selina hopes to explore how ancient narratives can still offer young people a language for understanding themselves today. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":545,"id":"135eca4d-6074-4b49-be78-4b41fe9476a8","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:14:40+00:00","title":"When Emotions Are Too Complex: Healing Through Eastern Myths | Yuetong Lu | TEDxGCGS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"dlp2iU3ssYA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"4f04b06076dd6fe1c96916c238ac73461bed732c62633de12d3e09df7c3e3648","description":"O que uma camisa de futebol pode revelar sobre quem somos? Cássio Brandão, reconhecido pelo Guinness World Records como o maior colecionador de camisas de futebol do mundo, mostra que cada peça guarda muito mais do que conquistas esportivas: carrega histórias de identidade, pertencimento, fé e superação. Partindo das lembranças de sua infância na Vila Albertina e do exemplo de sua mãe, uma costureira que transformava tecido com paixão, ele conta como fez de um hobby um trabalho de preservação da memória. De Pelé e Garrincha à origem da camisa azul da seleção brasileira, Cássio nos convida a enxergar o futebol como parte viva da cultura — e a descobrir como paixão, propósito e método podem nos levar além dos limites que pareciam definidos desde o nascimento. Cássio Brandão é reconhecido mundialmente como o maior colecionador de camisas de futebol do planeta. Sua trajetória mostra que a paixão não apenas preserva o passado, mas também constrói legados capazes de conectar diferentes gerações por meio de histórias e memórias compartilhadas. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":748,"id":"1574d964-2197-415e-905a-30572ee68f24","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:11:04+00:00","title":"Da arquibancada ao Guinness: uma paixão vestida de história | Cássio Brandão | TEDxAraraquara","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"b5dhxTCUdew"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5da505a293f247ff37964ee04b0a6c485049edc45ba95619d1485c1d3ee979a5","description":"TEDxRGSV Youth - 404: The Forgotten Archive  - 2025 High school student from RGSV Year 12 Student This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":403,"id":"157f7b95-8906-4782-a410-e5fe4e04bdb3","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:57:01+00:00","title":"Where You Last Saw Yourself | Dinh Gia Bao Nguyen | TEDxRGSV Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"IMjkNuiowDc"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c05a66e8f19822a6aebd535efd3d1729214c9f6589ddd2f443386b9f76ba07b5","description":"Os esports já não são o futuro - são uma indústria global que já está a acontecer. Da alta performance à tecnologia, da propriedade intelectual à formação de talento, os esports estão a desafiar a forma como entendemos o desporto. E talvez sejam também um laboratório para percebermos como será o entretenimento e o trabalho do futuro.\n\n Pedro Almeida Couto é co-fundador da RELUPE (agência de investigação lusófona em esports) e membro do corpo editorial da RBDE. É ex Diretor de Educação e Investigação Científica na Federação Portuguesa de Desportos Eletrónicos e Diretor de Operações da Betclic Apogee Esports, doutorado em Gestão pela Universidade de Milão e do Porto e orientador de alunos de Doutoramento e Mestrado. É ainda autor de diversos artigos científicos em esports. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":722,"id":"15a5961c-defb-4c33-a3a0-141b9842fffa","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:56:34+00:00","title":"Esports: o laboratório que a big tech não esperava | Pedro Almeida Couto | TEDxPortoSalon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"yLbuMBX18ig"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"0843660c67c6105f290f326c3694faf6e4214c023b892eead8e34b721374c8b4","description":"What if the confusion isn't the problem, but the expectation that you must choose? In this deeply personal TEDx talk, Iris Muche explores bisexual invisibility, belonging, and why embracing life's spectrum is more powerful than fitting into a binary. \n\nhttps://www.irismuche.com  \n\nIris Muche is a psychosocial counsellor, trainer, and author. In 2021, she launched the blog \"Die Max Mustermann\" to inspire people to stop living their lives as placeholders waiting for something to happen, but rather to live self-determined lives guided by their own values. True to the motto: because your normal matters too! \n\nThis talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":396,"id":"15cf4fe5-30a5-4c7a-98c4-959a3f939043","published_at":"2026-09-03T17:00:27+00:00","title":"Why being bisexual made me feel invisible | Iris Muche | TEDxDonauinsel Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"yRgpJ-edmNs"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d61c9218badebbfafb1b0572df9e0c9fbcef8ef4c454047ee391caa9903ecea8","description":"We're living through oil wars, wildfire smoke and extreme weather driven by a warming planet. But environmentalist Bill McKibben says one thing is finally going right: the price of clean energy has plunged, making it cheaper than ever to power the world without burning fossil fuels. He breaks down how energy from the sun became the best option on Earth — and why this narrow window of time may be our last real shot at a different future. \"Energy from heaven, not from hell,\" he says. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/billmckibben\n\nhttps://youtu.be/9LTGzEzDat0\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Environment","duration_seconds":665,"id":"15fae0d7-3016-4ad6-9d8d-77850c8634e2","published_at":"2026-08-06T15:00:27+00:00","title":"Our Last, Best Chance to Cool the Earth | Bill McKibben | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"9LTGzEzDat0"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"ca1ce9934735d0fdddd4c9fb3289d8b78af2438a44a15d9b9dfa24e0d02779f6","description":"Designer Salehe Bembury turns sneakers into stories — like a sunset-colored shoe and footwear with a hiking whistle in the heel. In conversation with TED's Cloe Shasha Brooks, Bembury walks through some of his most coveted drops (for Crocs, New Balance, Versace and more) and breaks down the risk-taking behind his work. (Recorded at Play@TED on May 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/salehebembury\n\nhttps://youtu.be/E-Se-_A4o-Y\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Design","duration_seconds":838,"id":"182c3459-6285-400e-88a9-08b64fd55dd8","published_at":"2026-08-28T15:00:29+00:00","title":"The Creative Genius of Sneaker Design | Salehe Bembury and Cloe Shasha Brooks | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"E-Se-_A4o-Y"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"4916310d625aa0edd2659448cd2852eaf68b1c74485cb0293aedb6648e5dd67d","description":"Your career isn’t broken; the system is, and you’re allowed to redesign your life strategically instead of blaming yourself for not fitting a rigged game. Rafael Tsantes is a Glasgow-based designer and learning technologist who grew up in Athens, trained in theatre, retrained into communication design at the Glasgow School of Art, and built a career in the creative industries without financial backing or traditional entry routes. He now runs a purpose-led design studio focused on mission-driven organisations. His talk pushes back against the polished success narratives that dominate creative and tech industries - arguing that for people without safety nets, career-building isn't a heroic journey. It's a survival design problem. Rafael makes the case that \"failure\" is not the opposite of success - it's a signal about power, privilege, and access, and that design thinking is not a creative luxury but a political tool for navigating systems that were never built for everyone. Part personal story, part practical framework, his talk offers something rarer than inspiration: a realistic map for people who have to hack their way in. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":972,"id":"18ec67a0-47b9-4326-b65d-feab5e62a77d","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:55:52+00:00","title":"Survival Design | Rafael Tsantes | TEDxLeith","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"7S3iT1VZCsY"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"c8106eb00263b6df9d125f6e56a451027d1efa5368977578046ce14f3f04110c","description":"We have more data than any generation in history, yet reality has never felt harder to grasp. Artist Yiyun Kang is on a mission to translate the invisible crises of our time — from vanishing fresh water and collapsing ecosystems to the black box of AI — into physical experiences that people can feel. Art isn’t a luxury, she says. It’s essential to understanding and acting on our bigest problems. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/yiyunkang\n\nhttps://youtu.be/WY-jr1m3xq8\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Art","duration_seconds":737,"id":"199d608c-35e4-43f1-8032-eb2fc1b7c94e","published_at":"2026-07-19T15:00:26+00:00","title":"The Case for Making Art in a Crisis | Yiyun Kang | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"WY-jr1m3xq8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"606a7c1930f6185fd4dce67d868aca75f4146e1ae2c70fe473445542d0a7c860","description":"A tecnologia está quase tão humana como nós! Quanto mais tecnológicos nos tornamos, mais antropomórfica é a tecnologia. O que vai acontecer a seguir? O que vai acontecer às pessoas, e às relações, e à sociedade? Na verdade, não sabemos! O que sabemos é que há três necessidades emocionais básicas fundamentais para o seu funcionamento e que não nos parece que irão desaparecer em breve: conexão, pertença e propósito. \nNesta talk a Ana Isabel explora cenários em que estas necessidades são satisfeitas pela tecnologia e não pelas relações com outras pessoas. \n\n A Ana Isabel é psicóloga clínica e da educação. Com larga experiência em contextos de intervenção clínico, escolar e corporativo.  Ao longo do seu percurso tem aprofundado o estudo da comunicação humana e das competências emocionais, procurando compreender a forma como influenciam o bem-estar, as relações e o desenvolvimento de cada pessoa. Atualmente é diretora clínica da Winmotional. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":803,"id":"19ff4f04-5450-440a-88b3-0ab42253a0a9","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:56:15+00:00","title":"Porque não devemos humanizar a tecnologia! | Ana Isabel Lage Ferreira | TEDxPortoSalon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Fp1IorcEu64"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"acd445bcf33d4534bf1f080fc43a0b495b1ef8af546959f3985ad8c062f972c8","description":"Most people think burnout is caused by working too hard. It isn't. It's caused by working hard at the wrong thing, spending years responding to other people's expectations rather than making intentional choices about who you are and what you actually want. The real crisis facing high-achievers today isn't overwork. It's drifting through a successful life that doesn't feel like theirs.\n\nWhat makes this idea new is the reframe: the problem isn't your job, your career, or your workload. It's the absence of self-knowledge. We've spent decades telling burnt-out people to work less, set better boundaries, or simply walk away. But none of that works until you've done the harder, more uncomfortable work of understanding yourself, your values, your strengths, and what success actually means to you on your own terms.\n\nThe talk introduces a simple but powerful framework: the choice isn't between staying or leaving, or between success and fulfilment. The only real choice is between drifting and deciding. And once you understand that, everything changes, not because your circumstances change, but because you do. Nat Baldwin spent fifteen years as a high-achieving professional, building a career in intellectual property and insolvency and leading high-profile cases involving household names like Thomas Cook and Bolton Wanderers. By every external measure, he was succeeding. On the inside, he was drifting. It took a personal crisis, therapy, and a sabbatical across Southeast Asia, Everest Base Camp, and Europe before he realised that escape wasn't the answer - self-knowledge was. Now an ICF-qualified coach working with lawyers, accountants, and consultants, his talk makes a case that reframes the burnout epidemic entirely: the problem isn't overwork. It's spending years responding to other people's expectations instead of making intentional choices about your own life. The solution isn't to blow everything up. It's to stop drifting - and start deciding. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":933,"id":"1a27c5af-e977-48b4-9485-3e637744cbaa","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:37:47+00:00","title":"Stop Drifting. Start Deciding. | Nat Baldwin | TEDxLeith","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Er2RoFzbu-U"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d0b4c8cb1068af884a836bf777ca665f9fa8261631c861c89f48ec8cf6d13e46","description":"Jacob Collier walks onstage to conduct an orchestra ... but none of the musicians know what song they're about to play. In a joyful experiment in live music-making, Collier pieces together an iconic song with the VSO School of Music Orchestra (and a little help from the audience in the TED Theater). Turns out, all you need is some improvisational magic.","duration_seconds":180,"id":"1af4dd5e-11ca-42f8-bd4b-409281a4de23","published_at":"2026-06-25T12:00:01+00:00","title":"Hint: It’s all you need #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ytyzsAV4pxA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e6662f6801bdef68f1f4ad64c1d09add29e36b5a10acdc148d5d487b8f9e0bf7","description":"Minority adoption in the USA now Lemar Marie Brown is a corporate board director and retired a career with Shell plc and Goldman Sachs. She is a former Executive Professor at the Mays Business School at Texas A&M University, where she continues to guest lecture. Lemar earned an MBA in Finance from The Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania and a Bachelor of Science in Mechanical Engineering from Northwestern University.\n\nMost audiences know Lemar for her expertise in win-win negotiations. This TEDx Talk, however, reflects a deeply personal mission.\n\nHer first board was with CASA (Court Appointed Special Advocates), advocating for children navigating the foster care system. Through that experience, she learned that African American children are disproportionately represented in foster care and are adopted at lower rates than children of other racial backgrounds. When she and her husband decided to start a family, that knowledge led them to pursue adoption.\n\nIn this TEDx Talk, Lemar shares the adoption story of their beloved son and challenges common misconceptions about adoption in the USA. Her hope is that people who want to become parents will discover that infants and children are waiting to be adopted here in America. Every child should have the security, stability, and unconditional love of a family. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":589,"id":"1b42c0f4-7321-44a0-a682-95ffef341296","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:42:11+00:00","title":"The joy of brown eyes | Lemar Brown | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"pQJqX1Ng7nA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"8ba6a9f6b7f9ca2a544596a361b22445ad4e40e72c6d96336821f8b1535401d1","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\n\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\n\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\n\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\n\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\n\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\n\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\n\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology\n Young Nepali writer, orator and performer. She has gained public visibility through TheSpeaking Titan And Poet Idol platform and reached the voting stages of the poetry reality competition, reflecting her strong interest in spoken word, performance and creative expression. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":580,"id":"1ba92972-1fd8-4e88-8046-49116c9e4624","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:13:40+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Identity | Sapphire Shakya | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"U1xX2wmh2bA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2f77d29a248076b2c8285649fdfcd5dfd8e58f57201b43f731d2ebe7e96f8565","description":"Most people remain poor not because they lack resources, but because they cannot imagine\nopportunities beyond their current reality.\nFinancial knowledge teaches you how money works. Financial imagination helps you see\npossibilities others cannot see. Bala Hama Francis is an entrepreneur, author, educator, and financial literacy advocate with a background in Medical Sciences from the University of Maiduguri, Nigeria. Driven by a passion for economic empowerment, he transitioned from the medical field into entrepreneurship and has since become a leading voice in trading, wealth creation, and business development across Northern Nigeria and beyond. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1432,"id":"1bb47d45-6c53-4305-a6db-5cc5ef042113","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:55:39+00:00","title":"Financial Imagination | Bala Hama Francis | TEDxSamaru","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"6TZGlahbF5I"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"a26fc6f2b2a9634ff3ffe9de780d7085480c8357a5e18e05df9df1d5da46063e","description":"Under the watchful gaze of society, individuals often polish a flawless \"mirror\" to present to the world, leaving our true selves buried within imperfect echoes. Journeying across time and space – from the melancholic cadence of Nanyin blind storytelling ballads to the unrestrained release of karaoke nights – this talk examines how embracing the power of our \"masks\" allows us to rediscover authentic human resonance beneath the surface of external perception. Olivia Shen is a cultural researcher and community organiser dedicated to social inclusion. As the lead of accessibility initiatives for visually and hearing-challenged communities, she works to build practical bridges of empathy and communication. Olivia's research focuses on the intersection of identity and performance, specifically hothrough the academic study of Naamyam – an ancient Cantonese narrative singing tradition historically performed by blind storytellers. By investigating how these historical performers used character personas to amplify hard truths, she explores how modern individuals navigate social masks. In her TEDx talk, \"The Masks That Tell the Truth,\" Olivia invites us to reframe the everyday roles we play, showing how performance, artistic distance, and music can help us safely access and express our deepest realities. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":799,"id":"1d95147a-7b79-4612-be7a-8845500f398a","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:14:22+00:00","title":"Mirrors and Echoes: The Reality Behind Perception | Olivia Shen | TEDxGCGS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"JZLZrk-TWJg"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"cc3a7f4a2987db0341828519e4aedde6621b424e3747694832c9a651d1d4fe74","description":"Genome researcher Michael Snyder believes health wearables, such as smart watches and glucose monitors, can transform medicine, shifting from reactive to predictive. (In fact, he's such a big fan of these devices that he wears eight of them every single day.) From spotting an illness days before symptoms appear to helping prevent the onset of diabetes, learn why the future of health care may be on your wrist.","duration_seconds":117,"id":"1e00deb0-a151-471c-8cf6-57e15824c78e","published_at":"2026-08-13T19:00:33+00:00","title":"Your watch might be your new doctor’s assistant #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"RBq5hr8OmlE"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e26924549621855c1b4aa09c732428d95fca4cb036885bcf9e1a8b48017db47f","description":"After leading a well-established life as a pastor, father and husband, Paula Stone Williams could no longer deny her truth and transitioned. In this conversational and at times humorous reflection, Williams offers her perspective on the everyday experiences lost, gained and once taken for granted in her journey of trans womanhood.","duration_seconds":54,"id":"1fea4700-c62a-49f8-812f-dd80c3a14fee","published_at":"2026-06-30T22:00:36+00:00","title":"\"Would I do it all again? Of course, I would.\" #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"sbq1O_Eorc4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"3ca3f055e51b95622cb8d51d6be4ce4c8188b9f9c6ad02a959f738027705e72e","description":"We have never spoken about mental health more openly than we do today. But what happens after someone finally says, “I’m not okay?”\n\nIn this talk, Devashis Jena, Founder & CEO of Project C Foundation, an international mental health organisation, explores the gap between mental health awareness and meaningful action. We have become better at recognising the conversation, but are we better prepared to respond when someone actually needs help?\n\nThrough relatable experiences, humour, and a simple three-step approach: Notice, Connect, Respond, Devashis challenges us to think beyond awareness and asks what should happen next.\n\nHis message is simple: the goal isn’t for everyone to become a mental health expert. It is to build a world where we can recognise when someone needs help, connect them to the right support, and ensure that the right person responds with action. \"Devashis Jena is an entrepreneur, changemaker, and public speaker working at the intersection of mental health, social impact, entrepreneurship, and storytelling.\n\nHe is the Founder & CEO of Project C Foundation, an international mental health organisation that he built from Hyderabad and has expanded across eight countries. Through Project C, he has worked to build accessible mental health initiatives and partnerships, creating a broader ecosystem around emotional well-being and support.\n\nDevashis is also the Founder of In Conversation With Dev (ICWD), a storytelling platform that brings together founders, business leaders, athletes, creators, artists, and changemakers to explore the people and stories behind their achievements.\n\nAs an entrepreneur, Devashis has worked across strategic partnerships, community building, business growth, brand strategy, and organisational development, building collaborations and communities that connect people, ideas, and opportunities. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":932,"id":"21dc953c-036c-460c-b884-8f195bf7c300","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:14:23+00:00","title":"We Learned to Talk About Mental Health. Now What? | Devashis Jena | TEDxKLH Bachupally","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"DPqAPI8tKaY"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"4f2fb923d6529cadc58c5adb67d64c037dd852a5067af31a63167659afb6ca12","description":"AI education researcher Randi Williams has spent years studying how kids interact with technology and toys, and what she's found should make every parent stop and think. She reveals how, as tech companies embed AI inside toys, children are forming strong emotional bonds with machines. Explore what this means for their development — and why a playful fix might start with teaching kids to break the things they love. (Recorded at Play@TED on May 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/randiwilliams\n\nhttps://youtu.be/X5rOzZq2Ce8\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #AI","duration_seconds":821,"id":"22164bae-79ec-4a4b-8197-30844e1a6f4e","published_at":"2026-07-24T15:00:01+00:00","title":"How to Raise Kids Who Question AI | Randi Williams | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"X5rOzZq2Ce8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"135f2c4e1f61be0fb40f96c267f8031b5232c0bc6c33d4aac7a31e6777fa8d38","description":"Stop waiting to feel ready. Discover how facing anxiety, fear, avoidance, and uncertainty creates the evidence that builds confidence, courage, resilience, and encourages personal growth, one unplanned yes at a time. Dr. Michelle Chambers-Rollins is a trauma-informed mental health clinician and professor at UNC–Chapel Hill whose work examines how anxiety quietly governs the choices we make. Drawing on civic training, public systems work, and her book Faith to Win, she challenges people to reclaim agency and move forward even when certainty is unavailable. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":439,"id":"2245dee8-3616-413f-a9f0-ddcaefc7a408","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:54:09+00:00","title":"The unplanned yes | Dr. Michelle Chambers-Rollins | TEDxRaleigh","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"U7hq5xbskqI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"eef1463e2e71f73e34df9a97b6a6da35f29ace182eef58570f69ab5b056b6f4f","description":"Disconnecting and Reconnecting \n Year 13 student This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":511,"id":"2512236a-7e49-40d7-ac96-81470ba32fed","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:46:02+00:00","title":"How To Remember Yourself In The Age of Too Much Information | Rebecka Larsson | TEDxRGSV Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"H60LdUzwU_U"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e39a4c63df52fbbaa2d866ee40837b68ae7439fe771b9abded8db99de7efcd04","description":"Amanda explora a ideia de que muitas pessoas vivem estagnadas na \"sala de embarque\" de suas próprias histórias, adiando decisões importantes enquanto esperam pelo momento perfeito ou por garantias absolutas antes de agir. Conectando essa visão à sua própria jornada de transição do mundo corporativo para o empreendedorismo e às transformações trazidas pela maternidade, ela argumenta que as maiores mudanças não são geográficas, mas sim internas. Com mais de 20 anos de atuação no desenvolvimento de líderes e organizações, Amanda Fàvoratto acredita que experiências transformadoras começam pelas pessoas. Fundadora da Efeito Encantar®, une sua experiência em desenvolvimento humano, cultura organizacional e experiência do cliente para ajudar empresas a fortalecer relações e construir ambientes mais humanos.\n\nCom formação no Disney Institute, além de Psicanálise e especializações em Saúde Mental e Desenvolvimento Humano, dedica-se a inspirar líderes e equipes a fazer escolhas mais conscientes e a transformar desafios em oportunidades de crescimento. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":919,"id":"268bf898-0348-4c8f-9c92-278076b3d84d","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:43:38+00:00","title":"Ninguém decola esperando céu azul | Amanda Fàvoratto | TEDxEloy Chaves Women","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"hFmeSYE1Ipo"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"8da3aac75c778d52239e6a28b179d09096b13c4238b459261e890744b7c9ef46","description":"For the past 30 years, surviving as an internet business meant getting people to click on your website. But AI is now making that thousands of times harder, says Matthew Prince, CEO of Cloudflare, one of the world’s largest internet networks. He makes the provocative case for a different deal — whereby tech companies actually pay people for creating content that feeds their AI models. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 16, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/matthewprince\n\nhttps://youtu.be/YCldrsmxi_s\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #AI #Internet","duration_seconds":762,"id":"27882270-eef6-42ae-987c-02b9df7970cc","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:00:28+00:00","title":"How AI Is Breaking the Internet (and What to Do About It) | Matthew Prince | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"YCldrsmxi_s"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"bb9ff92d375951bf6672b6b1464397c269fc3c5f71a01e31cb19c1dae4112968","description":"Algorithms and AI don't just show us reality — they warp it in ways that benefit platforms built to exploit people for profit, says etymologist Adam Aleksic. From ChatGPT influencing our word choices to Spotify turning a data cluster into a new musical genre, he reveals how new technology subconsciously shapes our language, trends and sense of identity. \"These aren't neutral tools,\" he says, encouraging us to constantly ask ourselves: How am I being influenced?","duration_seconds":99,"id":"27af19cb-99a3-4b69-ad1d-8064c1c02fda","published_at":"2026-08-06T23:30:32+00:00","title":"If you think everyone is starting to sound like ChatGPT, you’re not wrong #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"smJQdhS-FJU"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"1a5672d3cd3bf932ad3a6ab0841a8c85f1b0e88f00883103a214e383a9efcfdf","description":"","duration_seconds":157,"id":"2809606c-328a-4419-8dbe-179154888106","published_at":"2026-07-02T21:50:34+00:00","title":"A special message for TED fans from Sal Khan #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"xhfwWqvaWPU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"141617ba97422a602d135f50f37399cdbbb9bf5ee0d9e40d5eff346afdedd88d","description":"This TEDx talk reveals a harsh truth: your zip code predicts your health better than your genes. Starting with a map of Baltimore showing opioid overdose deaths concentrated in West Baltimore (21217), the speaker argues that the deadliest “pathogen” has no DNA—it lives in policies, environments, and unequal opportunities. Using the fictional story of Maria, she explains three mechanisms: environmental deprivation, toxic stress that rewires biology, and the erosion of hope. The call to action: the next great health breakthroughs must come not just from labs, but from urban planning, economic policy, and restoring dignity. Health should not be predetermined by where you are born. Huangchuye, a young observer and thinker from China who is passionate about social justice and public health. He is deeply fascinated by the hidden systems that shape human destiny, particularly the realization that a person's well-being is often determined by their postal code long before birth. He believes that true scientific pursuit is not just about conquering viruses in a laboratory, but about having the courage to challenge and redesign the economic policies and social narratives that create inequality. Currently, he is dedicated to exploring how to build a world where health is not a privilege dictated by geography, but a fundamental right for everyone. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":648,"id":"28bcbfb8-20e7-4d84-90a1-173f8e5d27ce","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:14:59+00:00","title":"Why Your Zip Code is a Better Health Predictor Than Your Genes | Chuye Huang | TEDxGCGS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"_jUjF5Dl1pI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"4b55802264614b50222f1b45df1d8965c49d44d6987caee16a1894e6bceaf260","description":"Değişen dünyada sanatçı kimdir?\n\nUsta oyuncu Tamer Levent, dönüşüm çağında sanatın ve sanatçı olmanın anlamını yeniden sorguluyor.\n Tamer Levent, 13 Ekim 1950 tarihinde İzmir’in Karşıyaka ilçesinde doğdu. Türk oyuncu, yönetmen, sanat yönetmeni ve yazardır.\n\n1971 yılında Ankara Devlet Konservatuvarı Tiyatro Bölümü’ne girdi ve 1977 yılında mezun oldu. Kariyerine oyuncu olarak başlayan Levent, daha sonra Devlet Tiyatroları’nda yönetmen olarak görev yaptı. İlerleyen yıllarda Devlet Tiyatroları Genel Müdürü ve Genel Müdür Yardımcısı olarak da görev aldı.\n\nYönettiği oyunlarla Rusya, Kanada, Güney Kore, İran ve Kuzey Kıbrıs’taki festivallere davet edildi.\n\nHâlen Devlet Tiyatroları'nda rejisör kadrosunda yer alan Levent, Devlet Tiyatroları Opera ve Balesi Çalışanları Vakfı (TOBAV) ile Tiyatro Oyuncuları Meslek Birliği’nin (TOMEB) kurucusu ve ilk genel başkanıdır. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1478,"id":"28c41129-1ff9-4367-838a-ca9f55083304","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:09:16+00:00","title":"Sanatçı Kimdir? Dönüşüm Çağında Sanatı Yeniden Düşünmek | Tamer Levent | TEDxMarmara University","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"nJCpINuMH5s"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"4dd26ef62a23b5928344841181972cde44743ae786e18e316d2c5b40dcd7a771","description":"It's nearly midnight. The house is quiet. And you're opening the laptop again, because that's what leadership requires. Right? In this talk, CEO Davide Viola dismantles the leadership playbook we all inherited, work harder, stay later, carry more, and shows how it quietly damages our health, our homes, and our companies. Drawing on his MBA research into why businesses fail, a fintech CEO's stage 4 cancer wake up call, and his own company's rise from an employee NPS of 38 to a world class 80, he makes the case that leadership as we've defined it is incomplete. The tools to serve both people and profit are here now. Because we only get one life to lead, and it doesn't happen by default. It happens by design. Davide Viola is the President and CEO of Health First Network, a national organization supporting 160+ health and wellness retailers across Canada. Fortune 500 trained, he has spent 25+ years leading in CPG, retail, and healthcare across Canada and the U.S., including leadership of a $3.5B national brand. His MBA research into why businesses fail sparked the mission behind this talk: redefining leadership to measure human outcomes with the same rigor as financial ones. Above all, Davide is a dad, a husband, and a son. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":497,"id":"2a92cc5b-a193-4cb7-9399-130f22667d47","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:41:44+00:00","title":"Why the best leaders design for people, not just profit | Davide Viola | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"gb-YbkfB3as"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"3243175acebbdb5c3d57b54a3535e8c88678d8abdbd2b7678b4e291826135140","description":"Want to learn a new language but feel daunted or unsure where to begin? You don't need some special talent or a \"language gene,\" says Lýdia Machová. In an upbeat, inspiring talk, she reveals the secrets of polyglots (people who speak multiple languages) and shares four principles to help unlock your own hidden language talent -- and have fun while doing it.","duration_seconds":78,"id":"2a96957b-dfbe-4cbb-8cd6-e640b78e71cd","published_at":"2026-08-05T20:53:33+00:00","title":"You don’t need a special talent to learn a new language #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"wMAtRRCKtbE"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"a82af13db47cf907822486a6c5f4856854cc04fddc2331a57e5a6642d431d34c","description":"What if GLP-1s like Ozempic could do more than just tip the scales? Physician Dhruv Khullar traces the winding path of the \"moderation molecule\" — from a discovery in Gila monster saliva to a potential diabetes medication and addiction treatment — and how they could quiet the relentless noise of craving. The most surprising effects of GLP-1s may not be in the gut, but in the brain. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/dhruvkhullar\n\nhttps://youtu.be/N17wkLNutTQ\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Science #Health","duration_seconds":667,"id":"2a99bcc8-8b7f-473d-9881-16f0a7c2a432","published_at":"2026-07-09T15:00:38+00:00","title":"Can Ozempic End Addiction? | Dhruv Khullar | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"N17wkLNutTQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"8ff2ee2a6e1e58307e35109cb6dd2e093701b8600d5511bbf53fb825f608e82c","description":"角　潤一 : 人を惹きつける「オーラ」を放つ人間になるために、できること\n\n学生の頃から、人々を惹きつける「オーラ」を放つ人間になりたいと思っていた彼は、\n長年、日本の外交官として、紛争地帯や国連代表部など、世界各地で働いてきた。\n多くの要人たちと出会い、彼らと自分は何が違うのかを考える中で彼が見つけた、\n「オーラ」を放つ人間に共通するもの、そしてそうなるために、何ができるのか。\n\nSUMI Junichi : How to Develop an Aura That Draws People In\n\nSince his student days, he has dreamed of becoming someone who radiates an aura that naturally attracts and inspires others.\nOver the years, he has served as a Japanese diplomat, working in conflict zones, at the Permanent Mission to the United Nations, and in diverse parts of the world.\nThrough encounters with world leaders, diplomats, and influential figures, he began to wonder: What sets people with a powerful aura apart from everyone else?\nIn this talk, he explores the qualities they share and reveals the habits and mindset that anyone can cultivate to develop an aura of their own.\n\n\n “A Persian-speaking diplomat, made in Okinoshima, from the Land of Eight Million Gods.”\nBorn in Omori, Iwami Ginzan World Heritage site, raised in Izumo, Shimane,\nand one of Shimane Prefecture’s Goodwill Ambassadors.\nJoined Japan's Ministry of Foreign Affairs in 1998.\nDuring overseas training, he mastered Persian as a diplomatic specialty and\nearned a Master's degree in Near & Middle Eastern Studies from SOAS, University of London.\nHis postings have included Tokyo, Tehran (twice), Kabul, Baghdad, New York (Permanent Mission to the UN),and Seattle as Deputy Consul General.\nIn Seattle, he gained recognition as an “unconventional diplomat,” taking the stage at Ignite,a Seattle-born TED-style speaking event, singing at Japan festivals, and hosting large-scale monthly parties.\nReturning to Tokyo in May 2025, and currently serves in her present position. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1015,"id":"2c52a430-a46c-4879-87bd-509c39c1067b","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:15:55+00:00","title":"人を惹きつける「オーラ」を放つ人間になるために、できること | Junichi SUMI | TEDxKumamoto","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"IsOel6vzTrY"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"2bb542127ec54f7ea7b66f70efa40df941fe28588658b4c9cd7ea587c1f0a4d1","description":"","duration_seconds":108,"id":"2d430f4a-48f5-4cee-9f09-7fb98b423a69","published_at":"2026-06-26T23:00:24+00:00","title":"What's your 5-9 after your 9-5? #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"q4QDN6YbxSA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9295269a7d9196bd373826fcba3f4962fa2343e7673b6fa295e7b33c3086c56b","description":"The introduction of genetically engineered seeds in the mid-1990s fundamentally changed the economics of American farming. It locked farmers into a razor-and-blade business model of patented seeds and proprietary chemicals, exploded farm debt to a record $600 billion, and created the conditions for the largest corporate agricultural acquisition in history. This is the story of a former Wall Street analyst turned whistleblower and activist who called out a $63 billion acquisition made without a shareholder vote. She argues we can't change the food system by auditing our shopping carts alone. We must also examine the systemic financial levers that determine how we direct capital, demand transparency, and allocate subsidies. Robyn O'Brien is a former Wall Street food industry analyst turned whistleblower, who became one of the first major voices to connect the introduction of genetically engineered crops and glyphosate-based herbicides to the rise of food allergies, childhood cancers, and chronic disease in the United States. She is the author of The Unhealthy Truth and the founder of rePlant Capital, a regenerative agriculture lender providing affordable capital to farmers transitioning to organic and regenerative practices. She has appeared before Congress, briefed government health agencies, and testified in state legislatures on food policy. Her work connecting financial analysis to food system reform has been featured in the New York Times, Forbes, and CNN, and she holds an MBA from Rice University's Jones Graduate School of Business. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":802,"id":"2d69a6bb-b7c2-400a-8f38-7955b45b988a","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:16:29+00:00","title":"What would it take to fundamentally change the American food system? | Robyn O'Brien | TEDxBoston","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"gPVL9nA9mXg"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"cfb5f459dfdf5c2e07fd8ddd917386e032f62933bc10fee798d590ec20e86d82","description":"You've probably heard the warnings about digital privacy: read the terms of service, think before you share, be careful what you click. But AI ethicist Jen Golbeck says the problem runs a lot deeper than that. In this eye-opening talk, she reveals how corporations and governments built a system of \"data colonialism\" that tracks your movements, searches and habits — for profit and control. Learn how you can fight back against the surveillance state, starting with the devices in your own home. (Recorded at TED Democracy Philadelphia on June 13, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/jengolbeck\n\nhttps://youtu.be/ZX5nTV8lwr0\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Technology #Surveillance","duration_seconds":659,"id":"2f85351a-9922-48f0-8cf6-ac151c49788c","published_at":"2026-07-16T15:00:08+00:00","title":"How Surveillance Tech Erodes Your Privacy | Jen Golbeck | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ZX5nTV8lwr0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"086c3ff96186c4f6bb3a1df4fd4fb4e23e1c7f6fed0a2c4cd486a5ea419bc9ea","description":"Through her practice visual artist Julie Brook will explore the idea of what it is to be in our element.\nBrook makes sculptural work outside responding directly to the landscape and materials to hand. She will speak about her experience of living on the uninhabited west coast of Jura and how this influences her practice today. Julie Brook is a visual artist who works predominantly outdoors on large scale sculptural work. Her work is directly influenced by the many unique environments she has worked in including the west coast of Jura, the uninhabited island of Mingulay as well as further afield in South West Libya, Syria and Namibia. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1022,"id":"32530798-fd35-4970-a75d-c0d415b5b8af","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:52:09+00:00","title":"Being in Your element  | Julie Brook | TEDxUniversityofStAndrews","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"5SbThA9ew00"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"91e8d2f6d009280b1cbf64b2136bea49d45868a253a340617acd9129ed850cb4","description":"We often think of the forces shaping society as something outside ourselves: politics, algorithms, culture, or powerful individuals. But what if one of the most powerful hidden forces is much closer to home? This talk explores how ordinary people quietly shape the world around them through their everyday actions, choices, emotions, and behaviour. Drawing from psychology and concepts such as the Bystander Effect, Social Proof, mirror neurons, and emotional contagion, it introduces the “You Effect,” showing how small actions can gradually influence what a community accepts as normal. Ultimately, the talk challenges us to ask not just whether we can change the world, but what we are helping become normal Suparna V is a final-year Computer Science and Business Systems student at Panimalar Engineering College and is also pursuing a BS in Data Science and Applications from IIT Madras. Driven by curiosity, she enjoys building technology that creates meaningful real-world impact. She has gained international academic exposure, participated in Model United Nations (MUN), presented research at international conferences, and filed a patent based on her recent work. Beyond academics, she has completed an Advanced Diploma in Hindi, is a Bharatanatyam dancer with over a decade of training and performances across various stages, and was part of a Guinness World Record performance. A professional artist and freelance designer and developer, she believes creativity and technology go hand in hand. She has also organized and hosted technical, cultural, TEDx, and community events, bringing people together through ideas, collaboration, and creativity. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":735,"id":"32988f8a-aac7-4485-a7bd-66686c43312a","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:18:28+00:00","title":"What if the Hidden Force is YOU? | Suparna V | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"AtMBzpSZXFQ"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"fe32d0e43092d9e869be772b8c0d47138457a24da173383e636f769391c733ad","description":"You don't need political power to make real change, says activist Katie Fahey. She tells the story of how she led a successful movement in Michigan to end gerrymandering — the practice of drawing district lines to favor one political party — and how it all started with a simple social media post.","duration_seconds":178,"id":"334d1a2a-39da-4425-97f4-c0e7b621b83f","published_at":"2026-07-06T15:30:40+00:00","title":"This speaker took on gerrymandering in her state — and won! #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"qaBpMYrOKX4"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"562a54419361d3fc22079ee0cb5a8195641121b813155f14b6ea11196c042a68","description":"The genre-defying band Turkana Sessions takes us on a musical tour through the soundscape of northwest Kenya, with Elizabeth Korkel singing and Eddie Grey shredding on guitar. (Recorded at TED Countdown Summit 2025 on June 16, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/turkanasessions\n\nhttps://youtu.be/nLw3o-XgEdo\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Performance","duration_seconds":402,"id":"335a1c80-eefc-43bf-b2b1-9718451ac375","published_at":"2026-08-22T15:00:20+00:00","title":"A Musical Journey Through Turkana | Turkana Sessions | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"nLw3o-XgEdo"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"d858755481ce02f8049b4cde72b709b2a1474938e6417368bcb4e014271ea787","description":"Sienna explores the place technology plays in our lives. Sienna is a student at Marist-Sion College. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":298,"id":"3430efe7-15ba-4449-b8f5-6287b746ba0e","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:01:44+00:00","title":"The thief of our generation | Sienna Bucknall | TEDxMarist Sion College Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"UCA9V7rGFIw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"4875c31304254ba55c5fb576ab9346b91d9b194d27ea37acad39132354345180","description":"What do the world’s greatest innovators, entrepreneurs, and everyday problem-solvers have in common? They all faced moments where they had no idea what to do next.\n\nIn this inspiring and practical TEDx talk, David Settle introduces the concept of “Figure-It-Out-ness”—the often-overlooked skill of navigating uncertainty with curiosity, resilience, and action. Drawing from his experiences as an entrepreneur, educator, celebrity photographer, and tech startup founder, David challenges the belief that success comes from always having the right answers. Instead, he reveals how embracing the words “I don’t know” can become the catalyst for innovation, growth, and breakthrough.\n\nBlending personal stories, humor, and actionable insights, this talk equips audiences with a framework for tackling life’s biggest challenges when the path forward isn’t obvious. Whether you’re building a business, leading a team, pursuing a dream, or simply facing an uncertain future, you’ll leave with the confidence to stop fearing the unknown—and start figuring it out. David Settle is a celebrity photographer, entrepreneur, author, and tech startup founder dedicated to helping people and businesses turn ideas into impact. With more than two decades of experience spanning media, education, and entrepreneurship, he has worked with some of the biggest names in entertainment while building brands that blend creativity with innovation. As the co-founder of Moonlight, an on-demand marketplace transforming the photography and videography industry, David is passionate about using technology to solve real-world problems. A TEDx speaker, educator, and storyteller, he inspires audiences to embrace uncertainty, cultivate resilience, and develop the “figure-it-out” mindset that fuels breakthrough success. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":687,"id":"34472b89-3f29-4816-aec8-1a9bda08e721","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:48:28+00:00","title":"Figure-It-Out-ness: What to Do When You Don’t Know What to Do | David Settle | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"nB0SAvFaoFs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"772d912c8cc6d5d0558a8ea2bf97a653c6f5f08c935cc7c12422f7d237fb9824","description":"A baby’s cry is more than just a sound—it is a form of communication. But what if technology could learn to understand what that cry is trying to tell us?\n\nIn “How AI Identifies a Baby’s Cry,” Senthil explores how Artificial Intelligence can analyse the unique patterns hidden within a baby’s cry and help identify different needs or emotional states. Drawing from his passion for technology, open-source innovation, and building solutions for underserved communities, he looks at how AI can transform something as simple and human as a baby’s cry into meaningful insights.\n\nThis talk offers a fascinating glimpse into how technology can learn to listen, understand, and ultimately help us care better. Driven by a vision of creating technology-enabled, self-sustaining communities, Senthil believes that true innovation must bridge the digital divide and empower underserved populations. This vision led him to establish MetooMentor, a non-profit initiative dedicated to mentoring girls and young women in technology and innovation. What began as a small volunteer-driven effort has grown into one of Tamil Nadu's largest in-person technology meetup communities, positively impacting more than 1,000 young women.\n\nToday, Senthil actively contributes to the open-source ecosystem, continuing his mission to build technologies and communities that create inclusive, sustainable, and meaningful change. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":617,"id":"35540e2c-932e-4cc9-ae76-1453851c493a","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:10:13+00:00","title":"Can AI Understand a Baby’s Cry? | Senthilkumar Murugesan | TEDxKanniyakumari","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"xSZbi9ky3kc"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"b58f0833f72d27c4f820354f2919abb101ed8dda49dc321785589f981c7002aa","description":"In a world where we're told technology is eroding human connection, this talk offers proof of the opposite: that our digital tools can activate community faster and at greater scale than ever before in human history.\n\nMost people are sitting on invisible networks of goodwill, capacity, and support they don't know how to access. They wait to ask for help until they have \"the right connections.\" They hesitate to reach out to weak ties. They overestimate how much authority or proximity they need to mobilize others.\n\nMeanwhile, they're isolated, and working harder than they need to.\n\nThis talk gives them permission to activate what already exists. It shows them the specific behaviours that turn dormant networks into action: clear asks, emotional storytelling, and trust in strangers. Rosie Dalling coaches C-suite leaders at TikTok, Visa, Citi, M&S, and the UK Parliament, teaching confidence as a practical leadership skill, drawing on a background in global advertising and professional theatre at the Donmar Warehouse. But her talk doesn't begin in a boardroom. It begins when her puppy goes missing in a village where she's a stranger. Within hours of posting online, hundreds of people mobilise: neighbours, retired detectives, volunteer search teams. What follows becomes the foundation of her work with leaders - and her talk, The Invisible Network Waiting to Help You. Rosie argues that most of us are sitting inside networks we've never learned to activate, and that the ability to ask clearly, communicate humanly, and trust people you've never met isn't soft skill. It's the most powerful leadership tool most of us have never been taught. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1042,"id":"3596d7a5-e33e-47c4-9cbf-3ade7ac91497","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:40:38+00:00","title":"The Invisible Network Waiting to Help You | Rosie Dalling | TEDxLeith","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"_U_Pwt5cj1o"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"7b9efb8046ee060cac4ed54f67ccd2bd2d9d870df1eaec8d08f032bbc2af6c4d","description":"We often look for the missing force that will finally help us move ahead in life. But sometimes, that force is not something we find, it is something we build. This talk explores the journey from an ordinary background to creating stability, growth, and a life of our own, while learning to navigate anxiety and uncertainty along the way. It reflects on what truly gets built within us through the people we meet, the experiences we face, and the choices we make. At its heart is a simple belief: if I can breathe, I can live; if I can live, I can think; and if I can think, I can win.\n Rahul Dixit is a strategic Human Resources professional with nearly a decade of experience in Campus Recruitment, Talent Acquisition, Employer Branding, Industry–Academia Engagement, and Talent Development. He has collaborated with leading engineering and management institutions across India to strengthen industry partnerships and create employability-focused initiatives that bridge the gap between education and the workplace.\n\nHis expertise spans campus hiring, recruitment strategy, stakeholder engagement, employer branding, and talent pipeline development. Beyond recruitment, Rahul actively mentors students and contributes to discussions on employability, workforce trends, and experiential learning through guest lectures, panel discussions, and industry interactions.\n\nAs a Member of the Board of Studies for the Mechanical Engineering Department, he brings industry insights to curriculum development, helping prepare students for the demands of a rapidly evolving professional world. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1310,"id":"35dbad09-54fe-4aab-b5bb-067162663af1","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:18:36+00:00","title":"Moving Ahead in Life | Rahul Dixit | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"iGjQbsAcjaM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"06b1de467733081a8dfc3f6e63eff29e22c54b7c51ba9e5ec067174a6a5d8880","description":"What if your greatest strength isn't something you built but something you survived? In this talk, Elena Sarango-Muniz challenges the way we think about professional excellence. After 25 years in HR, she discovered that the qualities making leaders exceptional often trace back to childhood wounds... to old verdicts we've spent our careers trying to overturn. Your perfectionism, your caretaking, your relentless drive, they all have a story. But here's the paradox: stitched wounds are not the same as healed wounds. Learning to live with what shaped us isn't weakness. It's wisdom.\n \nThis talk is for anyone who's ever wondered why they work so hard, who they're really trying to prove something to, and what might change if they stopped trying to erase their wounds and started learning from them instead. Elena Sarango-Muniz is an executive coach and former HR leader with 25 years in global corporate environments. She works with senior leaders to uncover how their greatest professional strengths often began as old wounds they've spent careers trying to overturn. A Peruvian immigrant, Elena brings a mentor's warmth and a storyteller's eye to the work of emotional intelligence. She believes our scars tell us who we are. The question is whether we're still bleeding into them. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":676,"id":"36cc1d0d-8066-4c4a-9c4d-e4d2cd0a7079","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:24:13+00:00","title":"Stitched is not healed | Elena Sarango-Muniz | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"_x2RGyxU28I"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"347d43d6a091e38b74df7be0349e947eda8addfd8340b1508ea6edab57d8524c","description":"Drawing from over 14 years of experience across AI, data, cloud computing, Generative AI, robotics, and digital transformation, including his work with Google DeepMind, Jithin examines why intelligence alone is not enough. AI needs context — an understanding of people, environments, problems, constraints, and the decisions that truly matter.\n\nThe next phase of AI will not simply be about building bigger or smarter models. It will be about connecting intelligence to context and turning technological capability into meaningful impact. With over 14 years of experience in Artificial Intelligence, Data, Cloud Computing, and Digital Transformation, Jithin currently works with Google DeepMind, where he contributes to building AI and data foundations for large-scale organizations and national economies. His professional journey spans enterprise AI, Generative AI, robotics, cloud platforms, and digital transformation across multiple industries.\n\nAn accomplished technology leader, Jithin is passionate about translating cutting-edge AI research into practical solutions that create real-world impact. Beyond his technical expertise, he actively mentors founders, advises enterprises on AI adoption, and contributes to the global innovation ecosystem through thought leadership and knowledge sharing.\n\nAt TEDxKanniyakumari 2026, Jithin will challenge us to rethink the evolving relationship between humans and intelligent machines, exploring how Artificial Intelligence is not merely changing technology This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":525,"id":"38bc221c-87e6-4b52-9adf-d6c4b519a073","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:09:34+00:00","title":"The Last Mile of AI: From Code to Context | Jithin SL | TEDxKanniyakumari","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"6vSDdWUyJR8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"fdbc422adfe08791c1be5219f3027c7d00dda962ec506d7004f81046193e63b4","description":"What if the person you become is shaped by what you do in the next five seconds? We often imagine that character is built through big decisions and defining moments. But student speaker Rhea Joseph  offers a different perspective: who we become is often shaped by the small choices that happen so quickly that we barely notice them. From choosing whether to include someone who is standing alone to deciding whether to laugh at a cruel joke, help someone, or own up to a mistake, these moments may seem insignificant. But together, they compound. Every small decision becomes a vote for the person we are becoming. Drawing from psychology, neuroscience and her own experience, Rhea explores why the brief gap between an impulse and an action can be so important, particularly during our teenage years. That pause gives us an opportunity to step back, question our instinct and choose how we want to respond. Her central question is simple but powerful: Which version of me do I want these next five seconds to build? For the next generation, learning to notice that moment could be more valuable than learning how to make the perfect decision every time. Because character is not built in one extraordinary moment. It is built through thousands of ordinary ones. And sometimes, becoming someone you are proud of begins with nothing more than five seconds and one better choice.\n Rhea Joseph is a high school student with a keen interest in psychology, human behaviour, and the small choices that shape who we become. She is passionate about exploring how young people can build stronger mindsets, habits, and character through everyday actions. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":562,"id":"38d43f78-6c75-4c8f-823f-3b19c7ad094c","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:14:14+00:00","title":"How Five Seconds Can Change You | Rhea Joseph | TEDxWisdom World School Amanora","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"8TDRTC_Id7g"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"83da19b37be7369d0abece8bc6780d8e3f05649d8ad05ce5a04f18a4bea6444f","description":"A talk parte de uma tensão deliberada: quando falamos de smart cities, a maioria das pessoas pensa imediatamente em tecnologia: sensores, dados, plataformas, inteligência artificial. A proposta é desafiar essa ideia, mostrando que a cidade verdadeiramente inteligente não é a que tem mais tecnologia, mas a que coloca as pessoas no centro de cada decisão.\n\n Foi Vice-Presidente e Vereador da Câmara Municipal do Porto, responsável pelos pelouros do Ambiente e Transição Climática e da Inovação e Transição Digital, entre 2013 e 2025. Presidiu aos Conselhos de Administração da Porto Ambiente e da Águas e Energia do Porto, da Agência de Energia do Porto e da Associação Porto Digital, e integrou o Conselho de Administração da LIPOR. Foi Vice-Presidente da Energy Cities e membro do Grupo Consultivo de Presidentes de Câmara da Missão Europeia para 100 cidades inteligentes e climaticamente neutras. Presidiu ao Fórum do Ambiente da EUROCITIES entre 2018 e 2023, após ter sido Vice-Presidente. Ao longo de três mandatos, liderou uma visão estratégica para o Ambiente, Ação Climática e Inovação no Porto, contribuindo para a transformação da cidade. É licenciado em engenharia eletrotécnica pela FEUP e possui um mestrado em telecomunicações, com distinção, pela University of Manchester. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1064,"id":"39054a5e-2850-496c-a7ea-b7ccd89e32fb","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:57:05+00:00","title":"A cidade inteligente não se vê | Filipe Araújo | TEDxPortoSalon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"lka4NCBmQgg"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9ee5b06033bd98c167aa0368346f53e07141030b199ba8c5b02bd56eba0bd0a6","description":"Taking from her experience as a young person surrounded by social media, Franka challenges the notion of self-optimization. She argues for a new paradigm in which we stop treating our lives as projects needing to be improved. Franka Baum from Germany is a first-year social sciences student focusing on international relations and political science. She is particularly interested in politics and in understanding human agency and behavior. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":683,"id":"3ba32906-7855-4b86-8408-727d33676350","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:03:14+00:00","title":"Why we should stop optimizing ourselves | Franka Baum | TEDxAU College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"DhelZFL9ZUg"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e69e0815be30ef235c60582dc3cc0b29990ddb912b8823b9b7563baef61f34e3","description":"How does studying the end of time change your perspective of life on Earth? Cosmologist and TED Fellow Katie Mack joins designer and podcaster Debbie Millman to talk about the story of the cosmos — from the Big Bang's ancient light still glowing across the universe to the five ways it might all come to an end — plus what aerobatic flying taught her about staying present. (Recorded at TEDNext 2025 on November 10, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/katiemack26\n\nhttps://youtu.be/GRQlIAWVO_E\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Astronomy","duration_seconds":1187,"id":"3cc351f4-503e-441e-b205-a6fe9184f68b","published_at":"2026-08-24T15:00:29+00:00","title":"A Cosmologist’s Guide to the End of the Universe | Katie Mack and Debbie Millman | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"GRQlIAWVO_E"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d91a1ccd13c4e80083403c99b6459abd05098a3a0e2b051448cbaa4844d69b08","description":"Leadership expert Melissa M. Mikus breaks down why most workplace friction isn't about personality clashes or bad intentions — it's about not knowing how to effectively communicate. Her solution? A small, visible and easy tweak that anyone can implement right away. (Recorded at TED@BCG on October 23, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/melissammikus\n\nhttps://youtu.be/xx8mwytj4CY\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Business","duration_seconds":595,"id":"3d1503e3-5397-4645-8497-6a5f866daa90","published_at":"2026-07-03T15:00:02+00:00","title":"A Simple Solution to Fix Workplace Miscommunication | Melissa M. Mikus | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"xx8mwytj4CY"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"06702ab33705c038586748c6b04389a0476a07ad6dbcc1ef06bacf6fc8c563df","description":"What do you want to be when you grow up? It's a universal question — and yet at some point, people stop asking you. Drawing on a nationwide survey across the Philippines, consumer researcher Lance Katigbak makes the case that countries should keep track of their citizens' ambitions and aspirations. His radical proposal: What if governments didn't just track GDP but also started measuring dreams? (Recorded at TED@BCG on October 23, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/lancekatigbak\n\nhttps://youtu.be/rTubAg019-A\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Economics","duration_seconds":554,"id":"3e287c31-2127-404e-8c72-cbc68dde99af","published_at":"2026-08-21T15:00:17+00:00","title":"Why Countries Should Measure Dreams and Ambitions (Just like GDP) | Lance Katigbak | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"rTubAg019-A"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"2e2eed03e29327c2ce8282aff5d52fe6a0269fb4a880e2b0170aa863b57a78ba","description":"Malala Yousafzai has spent her life advocating for girls' education — surviving an assassination attempt at 15, meeting with world leaders and then watching hard-won progress collapse when Afghanistan fell to the Taliban in 2021. That moment of despair forced her to completely rethink what it means to create change, and what she discovered replaced her shattered optimism with something more powerful and more honest. Hear how to keep fighting for the future you want, even when hope feels lost.","duration_seconds":85,"id":"3f9c510c-f519-453b-9ae9-31a566ec8101","published_at":"2026-08-31T18:24:59+00:00","title":"\"There are no magic words that can help you figure out how to be an activist” #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"EirmZYlDb_E"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2718e5c9d46a3f53fbfafd17284d7c4cfd23db6410d5785356bd9b18cb91b7ce","description":"Weber Martins parte de sua trajetória de quase quatro décadas estudando Inteligência Artificial para discutir os efeitos da rápida disseminação das IAs generativas, especialmente sobre crianças, adolescentes e a educação. Segundo sua argumentação, vivemos uma era de “falsa síntese”, na qual sistemas conseguem produzir textos e conteúdos sofisticados sem necessariamente representar compreensão. No ambiente educacional, o risco surge quando o estudante obtém resultados aparentemente excelentes sem realizar o esforço cognitivo necessário à aprendizagem. Weber contesta, nesse sentido, a expressão “dívida cognitiva”: para ele, determinadas perdas devem ser compreendidas como dano cognitivo, pois etapas importantes do desenvolvimento e da aprendizagem podem não ser plenamente recuperáveis posteriormente. A situação é agravada pelo aumento do tempo de tela, pela crescente mediação tecnológica das relações e pelo uso da IA até mesmo como fonte de apoio afetivo.\nApesar dos riscos, a palestra não apresenta uma visão contrária à Inteligência Artificial. Weber defende que a tecnologia deve ser um meio, e não um fim, e que seu valor está em ampliar capacidades humanas. Na educação, destaca sua própria experiência com sistemas tutores inteligentes e aponta abordagens contemporâneas nas quais a IA funciona como um “andaime” para conduzir o estudante progressivamente a níveis mais elevados de aprendizagem. O objetivo deveria ser alcançar uma situação próxima ao flow: desafios suficientemente complexos para estimular desenvolvimento, mas não tão difíceis a ponto de gerar aversão ou abandono. O mesmo princípio é aplicado às relações humanas e ao cuidado: tecnologias podem oferecer novas formas de interação e assistência, desde que preservem mediação humana, subjetividade, vínculos e valores éticos.\nA mensagem final é um chamado à ação imediata e à vigilância crítica. Para Weber, não há sentido em rejeitar uma tecnologia que oferece possibilidades antes consideradas impossíveis, mas também é inadequado entregar a ela processos cognitivos, educacionais e relacionais sem compreender seus efeitos. A IA deve ser empregada para fazer evoluir a inteligência humana, e não para substituir o esforço necessário ao seu desenvolvimento. A música autoral “Eterno Agora”, apresentada durante a palestra, reforça a ideia de que o momento para enfrentar essas transformações é o presente. Assim, sua proposta combina abertura à inovação com responsabilidade: experimentar o novo, dialogar e utilizar a IA, mas mantendo mediação humana, pensamento crítico e atenção permanente aos possíveis danos. Weber Martins is a professor and researcher working at the intersection of Artificial Intelligence, Experimental Psychology, and education. A Full Professor at EMC/UFG and a faculty member at ECISS/PUC Goiás, he was among the pioneers of neural network research in Central Brazil in the 1990s. His work has included the development of innovative software for behavioral research and intelligent tutoring systems. Combining scientific rigor with a human-centered perspective, Weber explores how technology can support individual differences in learning. At TEDxGoiânia, he proposes rethinking education so that intelligent systems help recognize, respect, and support the uniqueness of each student. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1016,"id":"4059fca9-86bc-4018-8681-bf895875b1b8","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:01:00+00:00","title":"Inteligência Artificial: um meio para evoluir a inteligência humana | Weber Martins | TEDxGoiânia","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"17IfLTztF80"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"4d886b266925f8f824bc75da5d3516660023f1a119d13e598ae0430828d81e1d","description":"An insightful exploration of the invisible forces that influence our decisions, relationships, leadership, and success. Drawing from over four decades of experience in corporate leadership, management, and education, CMA TCA Srinivasa Prasad shares practical insights and compelling experiences to help audiences recognise the forces shaping their lives. The talk encourages us to look beyond the obvious, understand what truly drives our choices and actions, and transform these unseen influences into forces for purposeful living and extraordinary growth. CMA TCA Srinivasa Prasad is a Management Consultant with over four decades of experience in management consulting, finance.\n\nHis expertise spans ERP implementation, Financial Planning, Cost & Management Accounting, Budgeting, Business Process Reengineering, Change Management, HR Management, Data Analytics, and Business Intelligence. He has led major ERP initiatives and served as Executive Director (Finance & Accounts).\n\nA passionate educator, Shri. Prasad has trained thousands of executives, managers, and students across public and private sectors. His programmes cover ERP, Business Analytics, Digital Transformation, Time Management, Advanced Excel, Power BI, and Financial Management.\n\nHe holds a Post Graduate Diploma in Management from AIMA, is a Cost Accountant from ICMAI, and holds a Bachelor's degree in Physics from the University of Madras.\n\nA sought-after speaker, his TEDx talk, \"Hidden Forces,\" explores the invisible forces that shape our decisions, relationships, and leadership This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1082,"id":"412736a9-7e12-4ee2-84b3-ee7b8bea478f","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:14:36+00:00","title":"The Invisible Forces That Shape Our Lives | Srinivasa Prasad | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"UCCkV0MaevY"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"d53b8e4f23e186881881f50088ca64fb042a11e5a2229289ac145b113c856598","description":"O que guardamos nos porões que existem dentro de nós? Sônia Bossolani usa essa metáfora para falar sobre lembranças dolorosas, ressentimentos e sentimentos que tentamos esconder, mas que continuam influenciando nossa vida. Ao compartilhar a perda de uma sobrinha em um acidente e o longo processo de elaborar o luto, ela revela como encontrou, atrás da dor, uma culpa que ainda precisava ser compreendida. Um relato íntimo sobre a coragem de iluminar nossas feridas, rever julgamentos e reconhecer a humanidade em nós e nos outros — abrindo espaço para o perdão, a libertação e a paz. Sônia Bossolani é gestora social e atua há décadas no desenvolvimento de projetos voltados ao acolhimento e à proteção de crianças, adolescentes e famílias em situação de vulnerabilidade. Ao longo de sua trajetória, construiu uma carreira marcada pelo compromisso com o cuidado, a escuta e a promoção da dignidade humana. Sua experiência profissional a colocou em contato com histórias de superação, resiliência e transformação que reforçam diariamente o valor dos vínculos humanos. A partir de vivências pessoais profundas, Sônia também passou a refletir sobre a importância de reconhecer e compreender os momentos mais difíceis da vida como parte fundamental do processo de crescimento e autoconhecimento. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":857,"id":"4178c37b-cc96-49d0-baa0-1e32721431a9","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:13:04+00:00","title":"O porão | Sônia Bossolani | TEDxAraraquara","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"X8-bB-h2jDo"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"7e00d97ef703efa5854ef398455f6ddaa2e1367fb4bb4aa8dd7784c881a64af4","description":"Artist Yinka Ilori believes that joy is inherited — handed down through the colors, rituals and gatherings of the people who raised you. In this vibrant tour of his work, he draws on what people say brings them joy and reveals the one surprising pattern that runs through almost every response. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 17, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/yinkailori\n\nhttps://youtu.be/wqOY7Y0w7pA\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Art","duration_seconds":511,"id":"41d8e40c-0b8d-4853-8a8a-17e455f6d1ca","published_at":"2026-08-13T15:00:30+00:00","title":"How I Turn Joy into Art | Yinka Ilori | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"wqOY7Y0w7pA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"b888fb403dc66ecd964bc74890ac2ba04f19604b5c3d37133cfd266069c394a1","description":"Natália nos mostra que mesmo nos momentos mais difíceis de nossas vidas quando estamos mais vulneráveis é possível nos reencontrar, superarmos os obstáculos e nos reinventar usando o que chamou de logística afetiva, e que todos podemos usá-la a nosso favor. Natália acredita que reputação é construída muito antes de qualquer conversa ou oportunidade de negócio. Ao longo de sua trajetória, ajuda empresas, líderes e especialistas a transformarem conhecimento, propósito e experiência em autoridade capaz de gerar confiança e impacto.\n\nCriadora do Método Influente®, atua como Conselheira em Branding e Estratégia para Negócios B2B, conectando comunicação, liderança e posicionamento para fortalecer marcas e relações. Seu trabalho já apoiou centenas de empresas e profissionais em diferentes países a ocuparem espaços de influência de forma autêntica e consistente. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":849,"id":"44d964ec-4d6f-4b1e-b9d0-fd8e9ddb9603","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:42:56+00:00","title":"Logística Afetiva: como liderar em meio ao caos | Natália Soares | TEDxEloy Chaves Women","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"BK_KgBZmcn8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9c22c1a3f9b2fb8f7d9d757b1b412c4e53398231179efdcda3adce8cc31da3b0","description":"In this powerful and thought-provoking talk, Angel G. Henry explores the global cost of emotional immaturity and the hidden damage it causes within organizations, institutions, and society. Drawing from more than two decades of experience in technology, project management, organizational development, and leadership, Angel reveals how emotionally immature individuals and systems can normalize dysfunction, silence important voices, erode trust, and create environments where people are forced to survive rather than thrive.\n\nWith her characteristic insight and candor, Angel challenges the traditional understanding of leadership and authority, asking us to confront a difficult question: What happens when the people and systems entrusted with power lack the emotional maturity required to use it responsibly? She examines how unchecked emotional immaturity can manifest as defensiveness, avoidance, manipulation, blame, poor accountability, and the inability to engage with difficult truths—and how these behaviors can become embedded in organizational cultures and passed from one generation of leaders to the next.\n\nAngel argues that emotional maturity is not simply a personal virtue but a critical leadership and organizational imperative. She calls on leaders to move beyond performative professionalism and embrace the deeper work of accountability, self-awareness, empathy, respect, and psychological safety. Her message is ultimately a call to break cycles of dysfunction and build workplaces and institutions where authority does not diminish human dignity, but strengthens it.\n\nAngel G. Henry is an Organizational Development Consultant, award-winning author, and professional speaker focused on emotional maturity, leadership systems, and workplace culture. With over 20 years of experience across technology, project management, and leadership development, she helps organizations align people, process, and purpose without sacrificing human dignity.\n\nToday, Angel G. Henry stands as a compelling voice for emotionally mature leadership and healthier organizational cultures, reminding us that the greatest cost of emotional immaturity is not merely personal—it is global, systemic, and often carried by generations who come after us. Angel G. Henry is an Organizational Development Consultant, award-winning author and professional speaker focused on emotional maturity, leadership systems, and workplace culture. With over 20 years of experience in technology, project management, and leadership development, she helps organizations align people, process, and purpose without sacrificing human dignity. Angel’s work examines how emotionally immature systems normalize harm, silence voices, and create a legacy of dysfunction—challenging leaders to redefine authority, accountability, and respect. She is the founder of Amulet Consortium This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":484,"id":"45285a21-d48b-41fd-ae4a-04417d6f4836","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:09:53+00:00","title":"The Global Cost of Emotional Immaturity | Angel Henry | TEDxMaitama Studio","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"KJWQY0dimxI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5581a88a5221c9501a73a137dd6304519099a6084c5cb79f9b224d01b8342f0b","description":"Noise isn't always disturbance; sometimes it is data. The experiences, we overlook sometimes can reveal something important about who we are and where we're headed. I'm Sowdeswari, an aspiring embedded engineer with a keen interest in technology, sports, and meaningful conversations. I'm passionate about podcasting and connecting with people through their stories, while also being active in throwball. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":691,"id":"459d00b7-6c71-414d-a92a-488deb711711","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:49:35+00:00","title":"Powered by Noise | Sowdeswari S | TEDxKGCAS","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"AGA0N9KEO2k"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"ba6508ed26b506af22583df38fc4bf45fb4d5c319ebf438b8b3491ff56db1a47","description":"In his talk, Eric challenges one of the most common modern beliefs: the idea that we do not need help. Starting from a simple comment left on his social media, he shows how everyday life depends on invisible systems, shared knowledge, and the contributions of countless other people. What looks like independence, he argues, is often built on unseen interdependence.\n\nHe then reframes familiar ideas such as money, work, leadership, and education through a different lens. In his view, money is stored help, jobs are exchanges of help, and careers grow as our ability to offer meaningful help grows. This shift opens a broader way of thinking about value, learning, and contribution in a fast-changing world.\n\nEric’s talk is ultimately a call to rethink how we relate to one another. Rather than celebrating isolation, he invites the audience to recognize that stronger families, organizations, and societies are built through mutual support, shared responsibility, and a deeper respect for the help we receive and the help we offer.\n\n By day, Eric is an equity partner at a financial services firm with more than 60,000 clients. He holds multiple advanced and accredited financial designations, including Certified Financial Planner®. Beyond finance, Eric was named national Best Business Influencer of the Year for his social media channels.\n\n\n\nHaving built an audience of more than half a million followers, his live keynotes and workshops educate through a distinctive style of visual storytelling. Eric is also a three-time best-selling author, including USA Today and Amazon best-seller lists, and has published numerous Forbes articles. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":625,"id":"45fc5f77-7919-42e6-9572-410d0f7cd829","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:47:30+00:00","title":"The Biggest Lie You Believe: I Don't Need Any Help | Eric McDermott | TEDxVUAmsterdam","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"UtjpIHy4LHQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2c1e7cc6949ecedfb598875bb0a9938ca37fea1d0238b3bfed629e1d153c65e7","description":"As artificial intelligence evolves, it’s changing how we learn.\n\nMarcus Fontoura walks us through a transformative journey of learning and what it means for us and generations to come. His talk explores what parts of learning will remain fundamentally unchanged and what will likely change with AI. \n Marcus Fontoura is currently in his second tenure as Technical Fellow and Corporate Vice President at Microsoft, where he works as CTO for Azure Core. He is also the author of A Platform Mindset: My lessons from developer to CTO, published in 2025 by 8080 Books.\n\nMost recently, he was the CTO at Stone.co (2022 2025), where he led the engineering organization. Previously, in his first tenure as Technical Fellow and Corporate Vice President at Microsoft (2013-2022), Marcus worked as the chief architect for Azure Compute and led the Azure efficiency team. Marcus also had posts at Google, Yahoo, and IBM Research. He was a post-doctoral researcher at Princeton University and has received his Ph.D. in Computer Science by the Pontifical Catholic University of Rio de Janeiro, in Brazil (PUC-Rio) This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":722,"id":"470f4646-ab87-4c6b-84de-e508aab14a50","published_at":"2026-09-01T16:20:22+00:00","title":"Is AI changing how we learn? | Dr. Marcus Fontoura | TEDxMiami","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"BrxsQtFcHDg"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"919bb7764fee35bc5b0bf8d76c678f712a8e74256bcb4c457d1b507501289ed9","description":"Did you know you're using your computer wrong? Indeed you are, says technologist and comedian Alan Resnick. Thankfully, he has a simple solution to stop data leaking out from your desktop. (Recorded at TEDNext 2025 on November 10, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/alanresnick\n\nhttps://youtu.be/6YNOQTqNfgQ\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Comedy","duration_seconds":635,"id":"49255585-f098-4c9c-bd84-9322f71f95f0","published_at":"2026-08-07T15:00:30+00:00","title":"Save It to Your Desktop! | Alan Resnick | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"6YNOQTqNfgQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"855e0a52c45af356714694c3a4ab20ca2d96c1b88a246e84860d6d0b4753fc15","description":"Exploring the motivation behind good deeds, Logan’s talk investigates how validation can distort our intentions. Logan contrasts his journey with validation against his grandfather, a World War II freedom fighter, who avoided praise, saying “anyone would have done it.” But not everyone did, and Logan unpacks why and what this contrast reveals about the true drivers of moral action. Logan Eliot is a recent graduate from the University of St Andrews with a degree in Economics and Sustainable Development. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":900,"id":"49642e1f-4412-4369-80c1-ae9ccd258505","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:52:27+00:00","title":"The Bravest Act Gets No Likes | Logan Elliot | TEDxUniversityofStAndrews","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"dkl0zr3RuTE"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"4ac303e0c5a11fcf68a96b4a4ef9ab3da1e2d3ddb8e2a7009ea7da3474ed0c6e","description":"First impressions matter, so how do you make yours count? Communication consultant Rebecca Okamoto outlines five simple ways to introduce yourself in 20 words or fewer, setting up any interview or conversation for those three crucial words: \"Tell me more.\"","duration_seconds":132,"id":"4b5b5e61-706e-4274-9c18-611c72ee2a7b","published_at":"2026-07-21T22:30:25+00:00","title":"No more panicking when someone says, “Tell me about yourself”","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"dEBhiP1yZZY"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2814ea7549989399bb92d6a1832d2f6b040e74532d1c1a67a9b690c107fc1f20","description":"Equip children with the resilience and emotional intelligence required to navigate challenges, bounce back from setbacks, and thrive. Kari is an accomplished author, educator and researcher with more than 30 years’ experience in education, wellbeing and human development. Author of 'Raising a Mentally Fit Generation', Kari is passionate about translating emerging science into practical, evidence-informed strategies that help schools and families support young people to thrive. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":493,"id":"4ba632e5-7afe-4c4f-89e3-5a77a525d718","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:20:00+00:00","title":"Fences, not ambulances! | Kari Sutton | TEDxQUT","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"IxpeD0yc0oo"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"27b6bd09bce46f92597d16330b5b53e1907644f81435c239cc5ab1475bb97f00","description":"《红楼梦》是一部从未停止被讨论与解读的文学经典。然而，当代人以现代视角重新审视它时，往往容易忽略作品深处的社会背景与人性困境。\n在这场演讲中，历史文化博主张志浩将目光投向《红楼梦》中“袭人”这个复杂角色，以一个小丫鬟的成长历程为起点，深挖隐藏在书本情节中的权力结构、性别文化与社会矛盾。\n他通过这场分享，不仅带我们重温经典，还直面一个重要的提问：今天的我们，真的已经摆脱那些古老的困境了吗? 图书作者\n文物与博物馆硕士\n知名历史文化博主 This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1120,"id":"4c1a5595-c5bc-41a1-9716-49dd5d176e2b","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:46:41+00:00","title":"《红楼梦》里看不见的袭人：古今观念中权力与性别的交锋 | 志浩 张 | TEDxNanshan Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"H3yNbWMrudY"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"3e8572d1515d2ce332f187a37aafd0d800e33a4429619b5a2fe1fe057b537734","description":"Nesta palestra, Dilze Percílio questiona a ideia de que \"ver é crer\" e propõe uma reflexão sobre como as relações humanas sempre foram mediadas por interpretações e representações. A partir de autores como Erving Goffman, Bauman e Byung-Chul Han, ela argumenta que, na sociedade contemporânea, marcada pela velocidade, pela performance e pela superficialidade das conexões, passamos a conviver cada vez mais com personagens e avatares, reduzindo o espaço para relações genuínas e para o conhecimento profundo das pessoas.\n\nA palestrante explica que a cultura digital deixou de ser um ambiente paralelo à vida real para se tornar parte integrada da própria cultura. Nesse cenário, a distinção entre o real e o artificial torna-se cada vez mais difícil, pois identidades, comportamentos e expectativas são moldados simultaneamente por experiências humanas e por sistemas tecnológicos. O resultado é uma sociedade em que a imagem e a capacidade de construir narrativas sobre si mesmo ganham relevância crescente, muitas vezes superando a autenticidade.\n\nCom base em sua experiência na área de gestão de pessoas, Dilze compartilha reflexões sobre os limites da inteligência artificial em processos de recrutamento e análise de talentos. Segundo ela, tecnologias podem acabar privilegiando quem sabe construir melhores avatares digitais, enquanto deixam de enxergar competências humanas reais. Encerrando com uma experiência pessoal sobre aceitação e identidade, a palestrante convida o público a abandonar os personagens impostos pela sociedade e a valorizar a autenticidade em um mundo cada vez mais marcado pela integração entre pessoas e tecnologia. Dilze Percílio is a sociologist with a Master’s degree in Sociology of Culture and over 30 years of experience in people management and cultural transformation. She works with organizational culture, strategy, innovation, diversity and leadership, combining analytical thinking and a systemic perspective to connect human relationships with business strategy. She is the founder of SPRINT Career Mentoring and Director at APOIO Negócios, leading organizational mentoring and talent-development initiatives focused on preparing organizations for the age of Artificial Intelligence and Human Innovation. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":891,"id":"4cd953ba-dc11-40d9-a314-5d10c06353b3","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:00:28+00:00","title":"Além do Ver para Crer: Humanidade em Tempos de Avatares | Dilze Percílio | TEDxGoiânia","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"oMUgyn3Uasc"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"7f2c510496d1e093776fc7628a0bf2c97b8794893a54a81aefedb65bc9945247","description":"Genome researcher Michael Snyder believes health wearables, such as smart watches and glucose monitors, can transform medicine, shifting from reactive to predictive. (In fact, he's such a big fan of these devices that he wears eight of them every single day.) From spotting an illness days before symptoms appear to helping prevent the onset of diabetes, learn why the future of health care may be on your wrist. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/michaelsnyder\n\nhttps://youtu.be/tIgO_Sjh3tQ\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Health #Tech","duration_seconds":835,"id":"4e9aa32b-901b-49b6-ba7d-4dc8c8a720fc","published_at":"2026-07-10T16:00:39+00:00","title":"Why I’m Obsessed with Health Wearables (and You Should Be Too) | Michael Snyder | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"tIgO_Sjh3tQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"abc471e17a3a998f5605e20522a1dade08ec500af0c635c8e37d62cd677a91e1","description":"O que significa ser visto de verdade? Patrícia Assunção relembra momentos em que se sentiu invisível — na infância, na adolescência, no casamento e até trabalhando em seu próprio bar — para refletir sobre quantas pessoas atravessam a vida sem serem verdadeiramente notadas. Ao reencontrar o teatro e desenvolver oficinas com mulheres em situação de vulnerabilidade, ela descobre que recuperar a voz é também recuperar presença, dignidade e existência. Por meio de encontros capazes de reconstruir vínculos e transformar histórias, Patrícia nos convida a abandonar a indiferença e fazer algo simples, mas profundamente humano: olhar, enxergar e reparar. Patrícia Assunção é atriz, educadora e produtora cultural que acredita na arte como ferramenta de encontro, pertencimento e transformação social. Ao longo de sua trajetória, desenvolveu projetos que utilizam o teatro para ampliar vozes frequentemente ignoradas e criar espaços de expressão para pessoas em situação de vulnerabilidade. Seu trabalho busca revelar histórias que muitas vezes passam despercebidas, mostrando que toda pessoa carrega experiências, talentos e perspectivas que merecem ser vistas e ouvidas. Por meio da arte, Patrícia convida o público a refletir sobre quem escolhemos enxergar — e quem permanecemos deixando invisível. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":403,"id":"4ecfcc39-d4ef-4e8c-aa33-25bc4979dee6","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:12:39+00:00","title":"A arte traz à luz as humanidades que aprendemos a ignorar | Patrícia Assunção | TEDxAraraquara","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"rSbjOfRYMw0"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d4cb3664004a79dc4320ac0d4c353f45885ee9fc15f4b459fc11470c7b7dfaa4","description":"14-year-old student Miles Wu is obsessed with origami, transforming everyday scraps of paper into unexpected creations. In a joyful talk, he explores the ancient art form — from making Christmas ornaments and pigeons to folding patterns that can hold 10,000 times their weight — and shows all the possibilities hiding inside a single piece of paper.","duration_seconds":116,"id":"524dd423-5576-4220-9c12-b84872c84e9e","published_at":"2026-08-22T19:00:09+00:00","title":"“A single piece of paper can hold endless possibilities” #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"FcSgd6nyjLk"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"b33e92cef798e62a07e220fa176b346fc97213ef14690946dee21cac99c443bc","description":"We often think of “influencers” as a small group of social media personalities. But my core idea is this: the influencer model has quietly become the template for modern work, and it is driving widespread burnout.\n\nIn a place like Leith, with its long-standing independent shops, creative entrepreneurs, and hidden digital businesses operating behind beautiful tenement doors, this shift is especially visible; family-run cafés now film reels at dawn. Independent retailers learn algorithms to survive. Freelancers and digital creators build personal brands from their flats. Visibility is no longer optional; it is expected.\n\nWhat makes this idea new is that it reframes influencer burnout not as a niche issue affecting a few online personalities, but as a structural transformation of work that affects entire communities. We are all being asked to turn identity into performance and engagement into income, and that the real \"Alchemy\" is transforming digital pressure into sustainable, human-centred influence through boundaries, agency, and digital wellbeing. Anthony Emebo is a PhD candidate at Edinburgh Napier University researching the effects of social media on well-being, a digital marketing professional with over eight years of industry experience, and a Grow with Google Lead Coach who has trained more than 20,000 entrepreneurs across Europe, the Middle East, and Africa. His talk argues that influencer burnout isn't a niche problem affecting a handful of online personalities - it's a window into something much bigger. The influencer model, he argues, has quietly become the template for modern work itself. Family-run cafés film reels at dawn. Independent retailers learn algorithms to survive. Freelancers build personal brands from their flats. Visibility is no longer optional, it's expected. Anthony makes the case that we are all being asked to turn identity into performance, and that the real alchemy lies in transforming that pressure into something sustainable, human, and genuinely ours. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":961,"id":"52c73cb4-2c2f-4b9c-9f1c-f57f72693d1f","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:40:35+00:00","title":"What happens when visibility becomes part of our work? | Anthony Emebo | TEDxLeith","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"LBMAkfSCTXU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"41533373e4709edc2577cd2e6728eafe32b75774eb56dfd1ce3d5bf626ba84ee","description":"Max’s speech is the sincerest example of boundary‑breaking. He openly talks about his inner struggles during speech preparation and recounts how he found the courage to stand on this stage. G11 student from Jinrui School Shanghai This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":706,"id":"53a4c5b7-9ea1-4d7f-aec9-96bad8432318","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:17:14+00:00","title":"True Boundary‑Breaking: Expressing Your Authentic Self | Max Wang | TEDxJRSS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"QJfsuVwqeu4"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"0f542b5488db5b91a24d3b2ae297073fde59c7d8e48177b578a0ca0757241544","description":"Can you stand up for what you believe in without participating in politics? Anti-career coach Danielle Roberts and political rule breaker Katie Paris discuss how to rethink power, work and civic life on your own terms. They explore how rest, community and meaningful participation can help you stay engaged in the issues shaping your life. \n\nIdea Knock Down features experts with different perspectives taking on big questions — one block at a time. Made possible with the support Visit Philadelphia.","duration_seconds":51,"id":"541f792c-5b97-4457-8da2-c1c6ad5be721","published_at":"2026-07-18T19:00:35+00:00","title":"The way we work right now may need a redesign for modern times #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"eSilxmZbaR0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"99272fb148b0e08bbadcf851ff2867bbe98a6324dd6582fa45159e2c65a1d546","description":"Como preparar a sociedade para decisões capazes de afetar o clima do planeta? Partindo dos riscos associados aos eventos climáticos extremos, o comunicador Ronesier Corrêa apresenta o debate sobre tecnologias de modificação climática e defende que ele não permaneça restrito aos círculos científicos. Ao relembrar sua trajetória na comunicação e investigar experiências históricas de produção de chuva atribuídas ao cientista Frederico de Marco, ele encontra em Araraquara um ponto de conexão entre ciência, memória local e participação cidadã. Uma reflexão sobre a importância de tornar temas complexos acessíveis, estimular o pensamento crítico e envolver a sociedade nas escolhas que podem moldar o nosso futuro climático. Ronesier Corrêa é jornalista investigativo, produtor de arte e pesquisador dedicado ao resgate de histórias que o tempo quase deixou para trás. Ao longo de sua trajetória, tem atuado na preservação e divulgação da obra de personagens fundamentais da ciência brasileira, com destaque para o trabalho de pesquisa sobre o cientista araraquarense Frederico de Marco. Seu interesse pela intersecção entre história, ciência e sociedade o levou a investigar como descobertas capazes de impactar o mundo podem permanecer desconhecidas até mesmo em seus locais de origem. Por meio da pesquisa e da comunicação, busca ampliar o reconhecimento de histórias que ajudam a compreender o passado e a refletir sobre os desafios do futuro. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":536,"id":"54bd3ec6-9088-479a-8ad6-637c458aab6e","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:12:03+00:00","title":"Do passado ao futuro: Araraquara e o debate sobre geoengenharia | Ronesier Corrêa | TEDxAraraquara","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"vO6kWg6Huw8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e480b0a6865bcb26d9fdce135510eb09d6346f9e543656f1cab0045f78053748","description":"Most people believe a photograph becomes art because of what is in front of the camera.\nI believe fine art begins long before the camera is raised.\nA fine art photograph is not simply a record of what we see.\nIt is an expression of how we see.\nThe image exists before the image. Ebuka Mordi is an award winning fine art photographer, filmmaker, Sony Partner, Adobe Ambassador, Adobe Rising Star, and Skillshare Top Teacher. His work blends documentary storytelling, fashion, portraiture, and culture, creating images that are rich in emotion, atmosphere, and authenticity. Through both photography and film, he captures stories that celebrate human connection, identity, and the beauty of everyday moments across the world. His work is driven by a passion for building bridges between worlds, using visual storytelling to connect people from different backgrounds, cultures, and experiences through shared human stories. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":605,"id":"5504807d-b578-49a0-bb36-e6dd6fb8ba4d","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:55:31+00:00","title":"The Image Before the Image: What Creates Meaning in Fine Art Photography | Ebuka Mordi | TEDxSamaru","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"aTOFIyQeK0k"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"7852d34e308cd5821dfc62a5ca3517a3fd50453e16da5337b0cb9e0af72d789c","description":"Arriving in the devastated neighborhoods of Los Angeles after the 2025 Southern California wildfires, journalist Nonny de la Peña started scanning the remains — what firefighters called “Nuketown.” Beneath the rubble, her team uncovered a surprising range of things that had survived. From a shockingly undamaged car to family heirlooms in a safety deposit box, see how her team found the story by stepping inside it. (Recorded at TEDxManhattanBeach on November 15, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe 