s":85,"id":"5680ada8-afc9-400d-899a-490f6a428cc0","published_at":"2026-07-14T22:30:12+00:00","title":"Turns out, your body has a much broader definition of “stimulation” than you might think #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"q8JktBmHGvc"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e06bfafe51088837e375bfe8665968e8e58c3d81ceff676ba9bd49f26ef6539e","description":"14-year-old student Miles Wu is obsessed with origami, transforming everyday scraps of paper into unexpected creations. In a joyful talk, he explores the ancient art form — from making Christmas ornaments and pigeons to folding patterns that can hold 10,000 times their weight — and shows all the possibilities hiding inside a single piece of paper. (Recorded at Play@TED on May 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/mileswu\n\nhttps://youtu.be/1U4dPKq6mJ4\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Design","duration_seconds":372,"id":"57bd219a-a23a-4528-8f84-b899c4d5d4f9","published_at":"2026-06-26T15:00:19+00:00","title":"Origami, the Ancient Art Form Solving Modern Problems | Miles Wu | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"1U4dPKq6mJ4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5631640c154a52acff81d01d100bb326a43d99a9581c5dfa1d7639617340298c","description":"Chronic conditions are something that effects a large portion of the population. Despite this, workplaces are not designed to support individuals with chronic conditions. It is important to reimagine the workplace and identify specific strategies employers can use to support employees with chronic conditions better.\n\nAlyssa McGonagle is an Associate Professor of Psychological and Organizational Science at the University of North Carolina at Charlotte. Alyssa has been studying the challenges that workers and organizations face around chronic health conditions, and how to address them, for the last 15 years. Alyssa recently published a book on the topic titled, “Chronic Health Conditions and Work: Proactive Strategies for Supporting Employees and Doing Well by Doing Good.\" \n\nThis talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":791,"id":"57c1da6f-d2ff-43f0-a1b3-b86a13b72a8f","published_at":"2026-09-04T17:00:37+00:00","title":"Why do people hide chronic illness at work? | Alyssa McGonagle | TEDxUNCCharlotte","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"nhLzEf3DiKs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"d74ce683b858c8c6158b0cfe9d71bad66bd9391d463432da25f60bf3f4106f2c","description":"While AI can achieve high‑level performance and avoid mistakes, errors are essential for high‑school students’ learning and self‑discovery. Instead of letting AI become an excuse to skip struggles and trial‑and‑error, students should treat AI as a learning partner and preserve space for human imperfection and personal growth. Shao Xu is a twelfth grade student. His topic was Embracing Mistakes in the AI Era. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":832,"id":"57fb8b66-f5b9-40c2-a8b8-97cf4cfc394f","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:36:42+00:00","title":"TEDxBeijing Haidian Future Academy Youth | Shao Xu | TEDxBeijing Haidian Future Academy Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"OExVP_bVvxw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"68a4c75f7110759ef22c363347bc51e4e1e356107cbc9ff381b408285728187e","description":"As artificial intelligence grows more powerful, so do the risks. In this riveting talk, Dr. Roman Yampolskiy looks at the long-term implications of advanced artificial intelligence. \n\nHis talk asks one of the most important questions of our time: Can we control what we create?\n Roman V. Yampolskiy is a tenured associate professor in the department of Computer Science and Engineering at the Speed School of Engineering, University of Louisville (2008-). He is the founding and current director of the Cyber Security Lab and an author of many books including Artificial Superintelligence: a Futuristic Approach. During his tenure at UofL, Dr. Yampolskiy has been recognized as: Distinguished Teaching Professor, Professor of the Year, Faculty Favorite, Top 4 Faculty, Leader in Engineering Education, Top 10 of Online College Professor of the Year, and Outstanding Early Career in Education award winner among many other honors and distinctions. Yampolskiy is a Senior member of IEEE, AGI and Member of Kentucky Academy of Science. Dr. Yampolskiy’s main areas of interest are AI Safety and Cybersecurity. Dr. Yampolskiy is an author of over 300 publications including multiple journal articles and books. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":868,"id":"58cf06fb-8ea8-4132-be63-2064dc1406d4","published_at":"2026-09-01T16:20:03+00:00","title":"AI ethics and the future of safety | Dr. Roman Yampolskiy | TEDxMiami","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"VQzK3cPWYTQ"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"2f5f49acdd26c7377f47432528ddbfff4707c0dc4796e7eb05115a88b059769c","description":"\"Music can unlock a frozen memory that melts into the seeds of our creativity,\" says musician Rosanne Cash. Reflecting on the power of memory, solitude and community, she performs \"The Sunken Lands\" and \"Particle And Wave\" and meditates on some of life's most poignant and bittersweet themes.","duration_seconds":40,"id":"591b93e1-e59a-425a-93f3-b5e06f215611","published_at":"2026-07-19T19:00:32+00:00","title":"That song deep in your mind has something to tell you #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"4BV-4fRVkrA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"44abe5b7ac9581479f6806f48dedaa98639a1d056e36676c3ad32445b6668939","description":"Ana Garcia apresenta o teatro não apenas como um espaço ou uma manifestação artística, mas como uma linguagem e uma forma de existir. Segundo ela, todos interpretamos diferentes papéis no cotidiano, escolhemos figurinos, ensaiamos diálogos e improvisamos comportamentos. A partir dessa percepção, Ana defende que o papel mais importante que uma pessoa pode assumir é o de protagonista da própria vida, escolhendo como deseja construir e contar sua história.\nAo recordar sua trajetória como menina periférica, Ana relata que precisou enfrentar a expectativa de seguir uma profissão considerada segura e abandonar o roteiro que outras pessoas haviam escrito para ela. Após reconhecer, durante uma sessão de terapia, que a arte era parte essencial de sua identidade, deixou o curso técnico que frequentava e começou a estudar teatro. Pouco depois, participou de sua primeira peça profissional, transformando a coragem de seguir sua vocação em uma maneira concreta de criar um mundo possível para si.\nA palestrante também mostra como o teatro pode facilitar a expressão de sentimentos, o encontro e a participação no mundo. Essa compreensão surgiu tanto em suas aulas com crianças quanto após seu diagnóstico tardio de autismo, quando percebeu que a prática teatral a ajudava a interpretar situações cotidianas e a construir formas próprias de existir. Para Ana, qualquer pessoa pode fazer teatro, assim como pode protagonizar a própria vida: não é uma questão de talento, mas de disponibilidade para o encontro, a mudança, o diálogo e o próximo movimento. Ana Garcias is an actress, playwright, poet, storyteller, and Theatre student at the Federal University of Goiás (UFG). Her artistic and academic work explores theatrical games as a means of unlocking creativity, expression, and human connection.\nMoving across theatre, arts education, poetry, and storytelling, she investigates how theatrical practices can extend beyond the stage and become tools for everyday life. Through performances, workshops, and artistic experiences, Ana invites people to reconnect with their creative essence and discover theatre as a space for presence, imagination, and transformation. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":521,"id":"59726bb4-b0e7-442f-9acd-b5df27b847ce","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:45:44+00:00","title":"O que te facilita existir no mundo ? O teatro além do palco  | Ana Garcias | TEDxGoiânia","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"vRLA4Q5JGuM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"02d648601eef37f0b61e59ac05912504278cd54829ea8384535fcf726abd8bc9","description":"Artificial Intelligence has made intelligence more abundant than ever. Yet, as the world faces climate change and other complex challenges, the gap between knowing and doing continues to grow.\n\nIn this thought-provoking talk, Mridula explores what we risk losing when technology makes thinking, creating, and learning effortless. She challenges us to rethink the value of attention, curiosity, empathy, creativity, and struggle—the qualities that shape us as humans.\n\nRather than asking what AI can do, she invites us to ask a more important question: What should we never stop doing ourselves? An entrepreneur, climate advocate, art enthusiast and medical aspirant, Mridula is passionate about building solutions at the intersection of sustainability, creativity, and innovation. Through her ventures, she works to make environmental action more accessible while encouraging young people to turn curiosity into meaningful impact. At TEDxKanniyakumari, she will challenge one of our oldest assumptions about what makes us human in an age of artificial intelligence, inviting the audience to rethink intelligence, creativity, and the qualities that no machine can truly replicate. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":785,"id":"5b6f7da2-16ac-4cd3-9cee-04791db0ee0f","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:10:00+00:00","title":"A Smarter Species, A Struggling Planet: | Mridula Manasvi R K | TEDxKanniyakumari","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"4GpYvTUi4b8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"8d50a71fe18ce602d563f75a350b2bde9561b8fd2f2f8053ec3bbfbbb34e701e","description":"For centuries, we’ve treated voice as talent, something bestowed rather than built. \nBut voice is not a fixed gift. It is a system.\nWhen the voice is understood as biology and behaviour rather than identity or talent, it becomes something people can shape, not something that feels unpredictable.\n\nThis shift matters far beyond performance. It affects how people lead, speak under pressure, navigate conflict and communicate in moments that matter.\nWhen people understand the mechanisms shaping their voice, they gain agency - and with agency comes the ability to be heard with clarity, confidence and intention. Taylor Wilson has spent over 25 years as a professional singer, actor, and voice teacher across the UK and internationally. At 26, she nearly lost it all when significant vocal damage forced her off the stage. Returning to performance, she discovered that rest hadn't restored what she'd lost, and set out to understand why. That search transformed her practice entirely. Now one of only five Master Trainers in Scotland in the science-based Estill Voice Training model, Taylor's talk challenges one of our oldest assumptions about voice: that it's a gift. She argues that voice is a system - shaped by biology and behaviour, not talent or identity. When people understand the mechanisms shaping their voice, they gain agency - and with agency comes the ability to be heard with clarity, confidence, and intention. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":867,"id":"5ca791c8-9189-4b5f-8b71-6a7f1b84e419","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:37:39+00:00","title":"Voice is a Skill, not a Gift | Taylor Wilson | TEDxLeith","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"19eHSZFRi9A"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"977fd5f5220718f50b7dafdaccf042d9cc51c4b0df8df13cfef67e663c1fe10c","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\n\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology A creative and articulate young individual whose strengths lie at the intersection of visual art, communication, and critical thinking. Through her participation in Model UN and the World Scholar’s Cup, she has developed the confidence to express her ideas clearly, engage in discussions, and think on her feet. Her passion for character art and doodling adds a strong visual perspective to her approach to problem-solving. With a growing interest in architecture, she enjoys exploring spatial structures and understanding how things work. Her journey reflects curiosity, adaptability, creativity, and a constant desire to learn and question the world around her. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":451,"id":"5f161343-fb9d-48d7-8e0b-f094d0a2431a","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:12:33+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Identity | Prachi Thapa | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"BbiJfs5oBts"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"8367f92128f5883c2fb1bbcd8f4a7fa2584cfe3a331aeee97d068ef1e1eed3d0","description":"Welcome to Club Reality, where no matter what life throws at you, the music never stops. In this talk and performance, musician ELEW shares his path from jazz pianist to DJ — and the unexpected philosophy he finds in both. (Recorded at TEDNext 2025 on November 11, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/elew\n\nhttps://youtu.be/qWD0hHvsDLk\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Music","duration_seconds":421,"id":"6057f7b6-7bce-4777-8f2a-e44db6718342","published_at":"2026-07-18T15:00:27+00:00","title":"Life Lessons from the DJ Booth | ELEW | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"qWD0hHvsDLk"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2b1d55d4c3304cd9dbdb7f4d447a242e429004e9512aefe9b491ea8dc2026e90","description":"Much of who we become is shaped by beliefs inherited from our parents, teachers, society, and experiences. This talk explores the process of unfolding those beliefs, questioning the patterns that define our choices, and consciously creating a life that aligns with who we truly want to become. Er. Sonesh Bhardwaj has over 18+ years of experience in the services industry, heading various portfolios under sales/marketing/operations/training. She has been awarded various awards/accolades by numerous organisations & universities worldwide for her significant & vast contribution towards education, life balancing & empowering mankind. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":781,"id":"62380efc-301e-4fbc-b159-f66645dc6c63","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:58:39+00:00","title":"Who Taught You to Be You? | Er Sonesh Bhardwaj | TEDxMoulsari Avenue","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"UMOgAHkViPM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"3cd3a62bf78d2e0d024b5cf72e4e6315eac7f0a3152a419f586078c9354fcff8","description":"Ashleigh shares her insights and understanding on the truth about school. Ashleigh is a student at Marist-Sion College. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":320,"id":"640b8ee9-b638-4f21-b091-a4f41dd97d4b","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:45:58+00:00","title":"The truth about school | Ashleigh White | TEDxMarist Sion College Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"6bmEAvW8lik"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e0eac30d4d966372f9388d0c707ee5e55ceb7627953b45a88e3991784a6b9e2f","description":"Jacob Collier walks onstage to conduct an orchestra ... but none of the musicians know what song they're about to play. In a joyful experiment in live music-making, Collier pieces together an iconic song with the VSO School of Music Orchestra (and a little help from the audience in the TED Theater). Turns out, all you need is some improvisational magic. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 16, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/allyouneedislove\n\nhttps://youtu.be/t2FoxBZD3tI\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #JacobCollier #TheBeatles","duration_seconds":867,"id":"6451bc2d-f38e-44ce-8d61-d2f5b0ad5fd0","published_at":"2026-06-25T12:00:06+00:00","title":"You Know This Song (but the Orchestra Doesn’t) | Jacob Collier & VSO School of Music Orchestra | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"t2FoxBZD3tI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"88551f1340017d50826a68e41b562f9aea69d40a5cfb41904e1c1516e2fa51b1","description":"We go on a Gambling Journey - the current state of gambling, what happens in our brains and how to stay stopped from gambling. Speaker, Podcaster and Coach, Hugo helps Gamblers, Alcoholics and Addicts find Recovery. Over 25+ years in the arena allows Hugo the perspective from the suffering person to being recovered. Changing how Recovery works. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":768,"id":"64c9ce2d-ddc3-4288-856d-124a5b6802f2","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:43:34+00:00","title":"The dark world of gambling | Hugo Vrsalovic | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"o_ij55CPGio"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"11c8c8401c5d07dec152d5f040c480557c5f3042f9ba2f286eba3295819d3bfd","description":"Six months into a catastrophic war in Iran — and with no end in sight for the war in Ukraine — the world is embroiled in conflicts with no apparent exit plan. In this interview, political scientist and Eurasia Group founder Ian Bremmer breaks down what these wars are costing people around the world — and which one he's more worried might spiral out of control. (This interview, hosted by TED's Helen Walters, was recorded on July 28, 2026.)","duration_seconds":77,"id":"65406328-2d22-4bb8-a726-4549148ec122","published_at":"2026-07-30T20:00:16+00:00","title":"The Iran war has cost billions — but its toll goes far beyond the global economy says @Ian_Bremmer","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"P4-3XTiiogo"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"f502ac7f9a61b8f139629c20f57cf4ac2e71dbcd30de44ad896d0e3b82c0e752","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology\n A curious, articulate, and thoughtful young individual with a passion for psychology, human behaviour, the cosmos, and life’s unanswered questions. She is a natural communicator who enjoys turning curiosity into meaningful conversations and making others feel understood. A strong leader and accomplished speaker, she has served as School Captain, Vice Captain, A-Level Club President, and Deputy Secretary General of XIMUN 8.0. Her achievements include Valedictorian, Best Speaker, multiple debate and quiz wins, MUN recognitions, and national-level participation, reflecting her confidence, curiosity, and leadership. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":844,"id":"6833ed7b-c6dc-4e83-86ef-fdb051ae5ac3","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:13:24+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Identity | Smarika Poudel | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"y2yWYVR7W10"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"ac5fb0e1c845bac7a77e1da7515b12b1feb762749b60bb6abfc8c50d8e5c27db","description":"One month before the Rio Olympics, runner Alexi Pappas couldn't hit her splits in practice. She was begging her watch to change its mind. Then her coach told her to take it off — and shared the best advice she's ever received. That single piece of wisdom led her to break a national record and changed how she chases her goals, carrying her through ultramarathons, a memoir and three films. Bad days aren't a detour, she says — they mean you're right on track. (Recorded at TEDNext 2025 on November 11, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/alexipappas\n\nhttps://youtu.be/kz-I5zIGbj4\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Psychology","duration_seconds":311,"id":"685560ff-c8a8-4a76-b604-5058086ed631","published_at":"2026-06-23T15:00:17+00:00","title":"Why I Love My Bad Days | Alexi Pappas | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:27:56.580764+00:00","youtube_video_id":"kz-I5zIGbj4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"8c1d9c6bca088de60c0024f1bcfd2016352df8cc42e554c826f69b9efaeee9c7","description":"What happens when your job becomes your identity? “You Are Not Your Job” explores the risks of tying our self-worth too closely to work and how hobbies, interests, and meaningful pursuits outside of our careers can help us build a stronger identity, navigate career change and job loss, and create a more fulfilling life beyond work. Celeste Bell is a former C-suite HR executive turned professor and researcher whose work focuses on identity, work, and resilience. She’s also a 70-time marathoner who has run on every continent and uses serious leisure as a live experiment in what it means to build a self that’s bigger than a job title. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":507,"id":"687eec34-8082-4d26-9855-dbbdc5567630","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:57:56+00:00","title":"You are not your job | Celeste Bell | TEDxRaleigh","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"FEDI0fic6mE"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"0ce0dc0a5b27c7630e53d1c0c5adef241db7ce66ee06e76a040b3bf24ba98332","description":"Why do our bodies become stronger and more capable in our early years, only to gradually decline as we age? This talk explores the evolutionary concept of antagonistic pleiotropy, where the same biological traits that benefit us earlier in life can create costs later on. Using examples such as testosterone and calcium regulation, the talk reveals how our genes carry trade-offs shaped by evolution long before we were born. It then connects this scientific idea to the choices, habits, relationships, and ways of surviving that we carry through life, asking us to recognise which “contracts” are no longer serving us and what we can choose to renegotiate. Ms. Lakshana B J is an undergraduate Computer Science and Engineering student passionate about leadership, innovation, and lifelong learning. Active in student communities and technical initiatives, she is a dynamic leader interested in public speaking, event management, and collaborative learning. She serves as Lead and Creative Head of the Automation Club and Coordinator of the Mathematics Club, organizing technical workshops, meetups, and student engagement activities. Her leadership extends to Model United Nations (MUN), where she has served as Executive Board Chairperson and United States Under‑Secretary‑General (USG), demonstrating strong communication, diplomacy, and organizational skills. A member of IEEE and the BIS Society, Lakshana has worked on technical projects while exploring emerging technologies and new concepts. Through her dedication to learning, leadership, and empowering peers, she inspires those around her and embodies innovation and collaboration. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":561,"id":"68cb5dd0-36be-4142-bd64-a0e9299174d6","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:16:32+00:00","title":"The Contract You Never Signed | Lakshana Bhaskar | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"i6KMacZUacY"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5a7fff8e507e8f9fba2aea383dc85e14e55b71e7f23d6ff8ba81305669e239e8","description":"For generations, many Christians inherited a familiar story: stay faithful, serve God, and eventually marriage will come. But what happens when the world changes while the advice stays the same? Through humor, research, and cultural observation, Sekou Browne explores how demographic shifts have quietly transformed Christian dating—and why many faithful people aren’t failing as much as they’re navigating a different world than the one the advice was written for. Sekou Browne is a writer, filmmaker, and cultural commentator exploring the intersection of faith, relationships, and modern culture. Across essays, fiction, film, and public conversations, his work examines the stories people inherit—and what happens when those stories no longer match the world they live in. He is the founder of Yoked, a publication examining modern Christian singleness, and the author of the forthcoming novel Coming Up Short. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":579,"id":"69aa1795-80dc-4b7b-a955-691d118bba6e","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:43:42+00:00","title":"Your pastor can’t find you a husband | Sekou Browne | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"aqEKoYaVF3g"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"a1675f24d51ad608cfe50d1290bd6af942adb01cb4a6de820ff3c505b09340dd","description":"How do capital, taxation, and business shape the economic relationship between developed and developing nations? This talk explores the flow of capital across borders, using the example of multinational businesses and investments moving between economies. It examines how taxation, infrastructure, business growth, and the preservation of capital influence economic development. By understanding these interconnected forces, the talk offers a broader perspective on how countries can balance growth, taxation, and investment while working towards their economic goals.\n Highly accomplished and strategic Senior Finance & Transfer Pricing Professional with 18 years of experience spanning corporate finance, taxation, transfer pricing (TP), governance, and risk management. Proven track record in leading global operations, driver/methodology restatements, TP documentation automation, and robust financial controls. Currently serves as Vice President and Operational Transfer Pricing Lead at Standard Chartered GBS, managing large-scale intercompany allocations, quarterly provisions, and complex transformation initiatives. Expert in global risk frameworks, including UK ACGR controls, FRCF transitions, and ERMF/M7 alignment. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":996,"id":"6a1cb446-04ac-4d93-b3b1-837b826382da","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:19:28+00:00","title":"The Hidden Forces of Global Finance | Meenakshi Ramakrishnan | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"HxvMplH4C8E"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c041738357455ee945c0c3dd5192c2bb500d2638baa680a54e7fa53a50e71585","description":"This talk explores the forces that shape a nation beyond its visible institutions, economy, and political systems. Focusing on Germany, it delves into the unseen ideas, values, cultural influences, and innovations that have shaped the country across generations. It examines how history, collective beliefs, and human ingenuity can influence the identity and direction of a nation. Through Germany’s journey, the talk invites us to look beyond what is measurable and recognise the invisible forces that shape societies and their future.\n Mr. Iwan Siemens holds a Master's Degree in Political Science, specializing in Sustainable Development in Multilevel Governance of the European Union, from the University of Bremen.\n\nHe began his career with the German Ministry of Internal Affairs and the Federal Office for Migration and Refugees, working extensively on migration and asylum matters in Greece, Italy, and Malta. He also served as a member of the European Union Agency for Asylum (EUAA), contributing to refugee protection and migration governance.\n\nIn 2019, he joined the German Federal Foreign Office as Liaison Officer in Kosovo. After a year in Berlin in 2024, he was appointed Vice Consul in Chennai in 2025.\n\nHis return to Chennai is a homecoming. In 2010, he spent six months at IIT Madras as an exchange student, developing a deep appreciation for the city's culture, people, and academic excellence. Fifteen years later, he returned to strengthen Germany–India ties. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":623,"id":"6ad20f11-6396-4f95-8ba6-ddb8c07b5967","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:15:16+00:00","title":"The Invisible Forces That Shaped Germany | Iwan Siemens | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"y_7s0v5EXd8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"498ebf98cfea7f921ad69355f7021616cad11e319246335a8370aad65e4f1996","description":"The Deering Quartet performing the \"Presto\" movement from Antonio Vivaldi's The Four Seasons is a nod to the lasting endurance of a musical piece created by a human being who lived almost 300 years ago.  The Deering quartet is a dynamic string ensemble dedicated to bridging the gap between classical tradition and contemporary innovation. Based in Miami, they deliver captivating performances that blend timeless masterpieces with modern favorites. Their mission is to inspire diverse audiences, foster a love for string music across generations, and create a lasting legacy. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":196,"id":"6ca8c5f4-aebc-49f6-b408-2c62f6e8f4be","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:48:33+00:00","title":"The Future of Music is Human | Deering Quartet | TEDxMiami","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"C_mcQuIeERk"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"7af10f023eef34db387856a3ce35ab76fb5312f9dd2ab9d31af4061d1d417a50","description":"Larissa May has spent a decade building digital wellness programs for young people, and she's discovered something quietly radical: the smartphone doom loop of screen dependence and anxiety isn't inevitable, and joy (not restriction) is the way out. She makes the case for a different relationship with technology — starting with swapping screens for some analog fun. (Recorded at TEDNext 2025 on November 10, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/larissamay\n\nhttps://youtu.be/fOTD04cjKPo\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Technology","duration_seconds":888,"id":"6db78689-e0c7-4ee6-958e-105da467a02a","published_at":"2026-07-30T15:00:37+00:00","title":"How to Escape the Smartphone Doom Loop | Larz May, Elise Hu | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"fOTD04cjKPo"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d6f86e76c4cee38d1b1fb6c3c31c5fac2ba394b68bc167098d0f275f2b1b4e13","description":"As AI agents take over more tasks at work, the question isn’t whether or not humans matter — it’s how we make our impact count. Leadership expert Vinciane Beauchene challenges some commonly held assumptions about how AI will transform the workplace, sharing a blueprint for leaders to design organizations where people can focus on what truly makes a difference.","duration_seconds":115,"id":"6e7b4edb-f2d8-465e-8181-818990df3eba","published_at":"2026-08-20T19:00:01+00:00","title":"The question isn’t whether or not humans matter — it’s how will we make our impact count? #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"P9s8Sn72Od0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c7bbcf1c82c111fdc21a992d84c9024a1081a95c1c07d21e3c6bf4d5aebf24a4","description":"Patients often believe withholding information protects them from judgment, embarrassment, or difficult conversations. In reality, those omissions can have life-threatening consequences.\n\nIn this compelling TEDx Talk, Dr. Shiela R. Ford, a nurse, educator, and healthcare leader with more than 32 years of experience, explores why patients sometimes choose not to tell the truth and how fear, shame, mistrust, and communication barriers can compromise clinical decision-making. Through powerful personal stories and real-world healthcare experiences, she challenges audiences to rethink the role of trust in patient care.\n\nDr. Ford argues that trust is not a soft skill but a clinical necessity. When patients feel psychologically safe enough to be honest, healthcare providers can make better-informed decisions, improve outcomes, and strengthen the relationships at the heart of quality care.\n\nThis talk is a call to action for patients, healthcare professionals, educators, and healthcare organizations to build environments where truth is welcomed, communication is encouraged, and trust becomes an essential part of every healthcare encounter. Dr. Shiela R. Ford is an accomplished nurse leader, educator, TEDx speaker, author, consultant, and Founder and Executive Director of The Nurse Dr., a 501(c)(3) nonprofit organization dedicated to strengthening the nursing profession through mentorship, leadership development, workforce preparation, healthcare education, and community engagement. She also serves as an Accreditation Commission for Education in Nursing (ACEN) Emeritus Peer Evaluator.\nWith more than 32 years of nursing experience and over 20 years in nursing education and executive leadership, Dr. Ford has led accredited nursing programs, mentored future nurses and nurse leaders, and served the profession through national accreditation, speaking, scholarship, and community outreach.\nShe is the author of The New Nurse Playbook: How to Survive and Thrive from Day One, a TEDx speaker, and publisher of The Nurse Dr. Substack, where she frequently writes to support new nurses and address the challenges they face entering, navigating, and remaining in the nursing profession. Her work also explores nursing leadership, trust, patient safety, mentorship, workforce development, and professional excellence.\nDr. Ford is passionate about inspiring the next generation of nurses and empowering them to lead with compassion, confidence, integrity, and excellence. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":612,"id":"700c1633-714e-447c-a9a6-b22ef7983098","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:40:15+00:00","title":"Why do some patients lie? | Shiela Ford | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"BhT1gKlJ1wI"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"91b7c765eba6319961930ef601b19732e5e6d94b6a662729aa67b21a62b937f5","description":"Did progress on your next big project stall out? Transformation expert Mary Martin refers to this moment as the \"messy middle\" — the frustrating gap between starting and finishing a goal, when you might lose confidence or give up altogether. Offering three ways to find glimmers of progress when the going gets tough, she explores what to do when you feel stuck. (Recorded at TED@BCG on October 23, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/marymartin\n\nhttps://youtu.be/P5ZCRBV5z0w\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Goals","duration_seconds":722,"id":"737d8232-b7d2-4847-8f88-46b591ea2656","published_at":"2026-08-27T15:00:25+00:00","title":"How to Get Past the “Messy Middle” of a Big Change | Mary Martin | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"P5ZCRBV5z0w"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"bdc91da7035f50bbf61adb7d8fc017561751d191326b020f27331200398ae934","description":"Deep tech entrepreneur D. Scott Phoenix spent years building AI — now, he believes we're on the cusp of a profound merger between humans and machines. Reframing the AI debate through the lens of evolutionary biology, he shifts the question from whether we should fear or embrace AI to whether we understand what's at stake if we get it wrong. Hear his provocative case for why we need to \"eat the AI.\"","duration_seconds":79,"id":"749d5225-e9a7-45ec-b64a-7a3a99b880ff","published_at":"2026-07-24T20:45:06+00:00","title":"D. Scott Phoenix thinks instead of fearing of AI, we should embrace it completely #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"FGRTMRjtj3U"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"29460563ea97b2298df3a0051b1e4f432ba9a75324787946a3fffdf375613f09","description":"Bakış açımız her şeyi değiştirebilir mi? \n\nÖzlem Mekik, bakış açımızı değiştirmenin hayatımıza ve yaşadıklarımıza nasıl farklı bir anlam katabileceğini anlatıyor. Özlem Mekik, 1981 yılında İstanbul’da doğdu. Küçük yaşlarda keşfettiği mutfak tutkusunu profesyonel kariyerine taşıyarak İstanbul Üniversitesi Turizm İşletmeciliği bölümünden mezun oldu. Turizm ve gastronomi alanında kariyerini sürdüren Mekik, Türkiye’nin önde gelen gıda firmalarında yönetici olarak görev alarak sektörde önemli deneyimler kazandı.\n\n2004 yılında yemek ve eğlence kültürünü bir araya getiren “Ziyade Fasıl” markasını kurdu. Daha sonra ITQI’nin “Üstün Lezzet Ödülleri” jürisinde görev alarak bu unvana sahip sayılı kadın şeflerden biri oldu.\n\n“Özlem Mekik ile Günümüz Lezzetleri” adlı kitabıyla tariflerini ve gastronomi birikimini okuyucularla buluşturdu. Antik Çağ’a ait “Troya Tatlısı”nı modern dokunuşlarla yeniden yorumlayan kültürel gastronomi projesinde yer aldı. \n\nGünümüzde televizyon programcılığı ve danışmanlık çalışmalarını sürdüren Özlem Mekik, yemek ve kültür alanındaki deneyimlerini paylaşmaya ve mutfak kültürüne yeni projelerle katkı sunmaya devam ediyor. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1056,"id":"756b8410-7daa-4940-8961-7821456ef54e","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:09:39+00:00","title":"Değiştiremiyorsan Başka Bir Yerden Bak! | Özlem Mekik | TEDxMarmara University","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"HlwmNZtNurc"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"98a5ab8206b9333d686d7d5421c4dde48b962772d93d85200cbb7d7605e74afd","description":"What if the most powerful force in achieving something great isn’t certainty—but a sliver of possibility? In this inspiring talk, Jamie Aarons explores how extraordinary achievements often begin with a tiny spark she calls “The Glimmer of Maybe.” Sharing the story of her self‑propelled continuous Munro round, she reflects on being driven not by certainty but by the very real possibility of failure. Through storms, exhaustion, and with some incredibly sore feet, she demonstrated that resilience grows one choice at a time—and rarely alone. This talk highlights the power of community and tenacity, and invites audiences to rethink what’s possible when we let the mind lead the body, when we welcome the prospect of failure as a catalyst, and when we embrace the quiet but powerful idea that maybe—just maybe—something extraordinary could happen. Jamie Aarons is an enthusiastic adventure racer, ultra runner, cyclist and kayaker as well as a devoted social worker. In 2023, she embarked on a self propelled Munro challenge where she scaled 282 Scottish peaks over 3000 feat. Jamie finished the challenge in 31 days 10 hours and 27 minutes, thereby breaking the previous record. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1005,"id":"7570ae5e-bb18-44f5-8e5b-a111011a46c6","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:51:37+00:00","title":"Why I climbed 282 Scottish mountains | Jamie Aarons | TEDxUniversityofStAndrews","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"DzBGmK5H6_c"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"fd49732a96e8cf0fe6a7fe3fdb83d1636d7bdbeb6c5e9a8a299d6e7cbd887253","description":"At seventeen years old, top of her class, prom queen, and dreaming of the Ivy League — Tsion Michael walked into her guidance counselor's office and was told five words that were supposed to end her story:\n\n\"Your future is illegal.\"\n\nWhat followed was a decade in the shadows. No college financial aid. No legal path forward. No certainty about tomorrow. Tsion was undocumented — not because her family had done anything wrong, but because her father, a multilingual CPA and strategic leader, made the courageous choice to go all in for the liberation of his people in Eritrea. That sacrifice triggered a legal consequence none of them saw coming.\n\nIn this riveting, deeply personal TEDx talk, Tsion introduces the concept of the Rented Identity — the suffocating experience of performing a restricted version of yourself that does not technically belong to you.  She traces her decade-long fight from the shadows to citizenship. And she reveals the unexpected truth she found on the other side: the very season that was meant to erase her was actually forming her.\n\nToday, Tsion is an HR executive, author, doctoral candidate, and co-founder of Arise and Shine Global — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit championing immigrants, refugees, and marginalized communities worldwide. Her mission is clear: no one should ever be told their potential has an expiration date.\n\nThere is a superpower hidden in every wound that did not destroy you.\n\nThis talk will help you find yours. Tsion Michael is a TEDx speaker, HR executive, author, and global advocate whose story begins with five words that were supposed to end it: \"Your future is illegal.\"\nA former undocumented immigrant who spent a decade in the shadows, Tsion went on to build a 20-year career in finance and human resources leadership across 40+ countries, earning an MBA from Liberty University, a Labor and Employment Law degree from Tulane University Law school, and is currently a Doctoral Candidate at Liberty University. \n\nShe is the founder of Inspire Every Life LLC, a consulting firm, and the host of the Inspire Every Life podcast — a personal development and leadership show focused on discovering one's purpose, alignment, and breaking free from fear and mediocrity.\n\nDriven by her own immigration journey, Tsion co-founded Arise and Shine Global alongside her father — the same man whose courageous sacrifice for the Eritrean liberation movement set her story in motion. The foundation is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit dedicated to championing vulnerable populations through housing, education, resources, and advocacy.\n\nShe is the author of The Aligned Woman: Is It Well With Your Soul? and the forthcoming Inspire Every Life: A Journey to Transformation and Unleashing the Power Within.\nTsion speaks, consults, and leads from one unshakeable conviction: the world does not need the rented version of you. It needs the real one. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":549,"id":"768ba7eb-0839-40d7-9164-68bab2d2f73f","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:50:22+00:00","title":"From Undocumented to Unprecedented | Tsion Michael | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Pja0pgyVyh4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2744b4a29177861cadde13890599a91e3690a92401df41b3094c4954c6a5ea89","description":"¿De qué sirve alimentar el cuerpo de un niño si dejamos que el narcotráfico y el crimen organizado le roben el alma? A partir del legado histórico de la lucha contra la desnutrición infantil en Chile, el expositor plantea que la verdadera seguridad pública no se logra con más cárceles, sino nutriendo emocionalmente a la infancia a través del afecto y el sentido de pertenencia. Su propuesta impulsa políticas públicas que utilicen el deporte, la música y la cultura en las escuelas para construir \"tribus\" protectoras que alejen a los jóvenes de la vulnerabilidad Felipe Kast Sommerhoff es Ingeniero Comercial y Magíster en Economía de la Pontificia Universidad Católica de Chile, y Doctor en Políticas Públicas de la Universidad de Harvard. Es economista, investigador, consultor y político chileno, miembro fundador del partido Evolución Política (Evópoli), del cual fue su segundo presidente. Se ha desempeñado como ministro de Planificación entre 2010 y 2011, candidato presidencial en las elecciones primarias de 2017 y diputado de la República en representación del distrito n° 22 de Santiago entre 2014 y 2018. Desde el 11 de marzo de 2018 hasta el 11 de marzo de 2026 ejerció como senador por la Circunscripción 11, Región de la Araucanía, período en el que también asumió como presidente de la Comisión de Hacienda del Senado. Ha sido una voz activa en el debate económico y político nacional, abordando temas de política fiscal, desarrollo social y reformas al Estado. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1232,"id":"76cbee84-ba69-4053-9528-47d2af2954b0","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:00:09+00:00","title":"Un vaso de leche y un beso en la mejilla | Felipe Kast | TEDxPUC de Chile","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ZuFXW7WREc0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c89f113ad9ff3c21a2dc63a59dfada501e8d900662acea225a1a204fc2be4770","description":"What if belonging is not something we wait to receive, but something we choose to create?  \nIn this powerful TEDx talk, Khady Gaye introduces the world to Teranga—a Senegalese concept that goes far beyond kindness. Teranga Is a way of leading, living, and connecting that transforms strangers into community and workplaces into spaces where people feel seen, valued, heard, and respected.\n\nDrawing from her personal journey across continents, cultures, and boardrooms, Khady challenges us to rethink what it truly means to welcome others. She shows that belonging is not a soft skill—it’s a human and leadership imperative for all of us, whether we’re managing teams, coordinating non‑profit programs, hosting community networks, or bringing people together at home\n\nThis talk is an invitation to lead differently, connect more deeply, and discover how one simple act of genuine welcome can change lives, teams, and communities. Born and raised in Ghent with Senegalese roots, Khady Gaye is a multilingual HR leader with Master’s degrees in Translation and Human Resources Management. With more than 20 years of experience across 26+ countries, she now leads people strategy for the EMEAI region in a large airline company. Khady is a mentor, global speaker, and proud breast cancer warrior who embodies and promotes the Teranga spirit. She actively volunteers with organizations that support outreach and educational opportunities for underserved children This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":557,"id":"7926443f-e051-42af-95e2-9d3b2279bb2b","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:44:04+00:00","title":"Teranga: Why belonging begins with welcome | Khady Gaye | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"rh4wnRYfAs8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e681ed296009580acde912d1877914d6282eca0e671700d5512c61fe31cb7115","description":"n an interconnected world where ideas, technology, and challenges move effortlessly across borders, why does higher education remain so divided?\n\nIn this thought-provoking talk, Prof. Ts. Dr. Pasupuleti Visweswara Rao explores how universities can move beyond geographical and disciplinary boundaries to become truly global networks of knowledge, collaboration, and innovation. Drawing from his international experience across research and higher education, he argues that talent is universal, but opportunity is not.\n\nFrom interdisciplinary learning and global collaboration to AI-powered education and research with purpose, he presents a vision for universities that build bridges rather than borders.\n\nUltimately, the future of education will not belong to the biggest universities, but to the most connected—because when education moves beyond borders, humanity moves beyond limitations. Prof. Ts. Dr. Pasupuleti Visweswara Rao is an internationally acclaimed scientist, academic leader, and biotechnology researcher with an exceptional record of advancing biomedical science and fostering global research collaborations. With expertise spanning biotechnology, biochemistry, diabetes research, medicinal plants, and nanotechnology, he has dedicated his career to translating scientific discoveries into meaningful societal impact.\n\nAt TEDxKanniyakumari 2026, under the theme \"Redesigning Reality,\" Prof. Ts. Dr. Pasupuleti Visweswara Rao will share insights into how scientific curiosity, interdisciplinary collaboration, and relentless innovation are reshaping the future of healthcare, biotechnology, and human progress. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":834,"id":"79e56dde-66da-4a30-a494-c912029ead18","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:40:07+00:00","title":"Beyond Borders: Reimagining Higher Ed | Prof. Ts. Dr. Pasupuleti Visweswara Rao | TEDxKanniyakumari","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"2VKLTxtuyVI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"24183c06a8986dd8b68644513306ead0fcab26ca249362d4d33cb8cb2a8c5f11","description":"In 'Bekar Ko Akar', Neelam Sarangi shares her inspiring journey of transforming discarded, 'bekar' waste into meaningful and beautiful 'aakar'. She reveals how creativity and conscious thinking can turn the useless into useful, proving that sustainability begins with seeing value where others see waste. Neelam Sarangi is an Indian eco-entrepreneur and the founder of Bekar Ko Aakar, a social startup based in Jhansi, Uttar Pradesh, that focuses on upcycling solid waste into beautiful art pieces and functional products. Fondly nicknamed \"Kabaad waali Didi\" (The Scrap Sister), she has gained nationwide recognition for her work in transforming garbage into wealth. The literal meaning of her startup's name, Bekar Ko Aakar, is \"Giving shape to the useless.\" She began her journey by collecting discarded materials like old tires, plastic bottles, metal scraps, copper wires, wooden logs, and shoes to creatively upcycle them into home decor, ornaments, and public art installations. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":981,"id":"7f8a6dea-dd0e-4669-9673-6c34bf267e0f","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:38:39+00:00","title":"Bekar Ko Akar | Neelam Sarangi | TEDxBistupur","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"GCnBVjpCtVI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2da8e307fe91386a8ab572cbcd9f654faf07570c1ef13c883bda622cb5087fe5","description":"This speech explores the importance of Culturally Relevant Pedagogy in creating classrooms where every student feels valued and represented. Students come from many cultural and linguistic backgrounds, including those who communicate using American Sign Language, and it is important that schools do not erase or ignore these identities. The speech will highlight how teachers can naturally incorporate culturally relevant practices through inclusive texts, examples, and classroom discussions. It will also emphasize the need for educators to reflect on their own biases before teaching, so those assumptions do not impact how they support different students, ensuring equitable and respectful learning environments for everyone. She is a university student full of passion to teach and make change. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":737,"id":"80dfc3f2-261e-49b8-afed-c903e58d95b0","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:59:50+00:00","title":"Learning That Looks Like Us | Colin Martinez | TEDxSST Corpus Christi Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"qUQ5B3ucbso"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"778e485e4a52bdf13897ad61156c3696f4fe7f28c300b3f90e292c9c8a3bd972","description":"NOTE FROM TED: TEDx events are independently organized by volunteers. The guidelines we give TEDx organizers are described in more detail here: http://storage.ted.com/tedx/manuals/tedx_content_guidelines.pdf\n\nThe future of technology doesn’t just depend on innovation. It also depends on who gets to participate. \n\nRuthe Farmer's moving talk takes us through the benefits of expanding access to STEM education and diversifying the tech workforce- and how these can reshape what gets built next. \n\nRuthe Farmer is the Founder and CEO of Last Mile Education Fund, the only nationwide safety net addressing the graduation gap among aspiring STEM talent from low-income communities. A serial social entrepreneur and former Obama White House advisor, Ruthe has spent her career expanding access to technology and innovation workforce pathways. She envisions a world where the tools and technology used by all, are created by all. \nShe has launched and scaled multiple enduring national education initiatives, including tech and engineering programs and strategy for Girl Scouts of the USA and Aspirations in Computing. Her work has led to bipartisan federal legislation that unlocked millions in Department of Defense funding to expand computing education at 3421 JROTC host schools, increasing access for 4 million US high school students each year. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":600,"id":"8123b567-ad1a-4b38-95a0-ab7d8c13accb","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:53:56+00:00","title":"The Future of Talent | Ruthe Farmer | TEDxMiami","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"nai1VgmEXW0"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"bc344af765ff3e9ffb2012827f4f60e08660d6197533709b72b02d682cb9ee24","description":"What does it take to stay in control of your life online? AI ethicist Jen Golbeck and TED Fellow Shalini Kantayya discuss how algorithms, data collection and AI shape the choices you make every day. They explore whether it’s possible to use the internet without giving up your privacy, how to push back against big tech and what AI should never be allowed to decide. (This episode is part of “Idea Knock Down,” a series featuring experts with different perspectives taking on big questions — one block at a time.) (Recorded on June 12, 2026)\n\n0:00 - Idea Knock Down\n0:58 - Manipulated by an algorithm? \n1:26 - What should AI never decide?\n2:05 - Your data = your property?\n2:46 - How to push back against big tech?\n3:57 - How to keep privacy on the internet?\n4:54 - A better internet\n6:27 - What is democracy?\n7:02 - Mystery question\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nhttps://www.ted.com/series/idea_knock_down\n\nhttps://youtu.be/04bCu-8W_bo\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #AI","duration_seconds":572,"id":"815b7211-ea08-46e6-a91d-fd219a0b5dcc","published_at":"2026-07-07T15:00:01+00:00","title":"How Algorithms Manipulate You (and How to Fight Back) | Idea Knock Down | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"04bCu-8W_bo"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"808701b5ffd8850980663b53ffc2523d5904b19dbf862e420b7df2fadc96d9a9","description":"When Yidan Xu's father was diagnosed with advanced chronic kidney disease in 2023, she came to see that the real issue wasn't the absence of testing tools — it was that people simply weren't getting tested. So as a designer, she created Urify: a kidney disease early-warning system built into toilet cleaning products. With these everyday, low-barrier items, she transforms “treatment after the fact” into “prevention in daily life.”\n2023年，徐一丹的父亲确诊慢性肾病中晚期——这让她意识到，问题不在于检测技术的缺失，而在于“检测行为本身没有发生”。作为设计师，她把答案做成了Urify：一款融入马桶清洁产品的肾病预警设计，用低门槛的日常物品，把疾病“事后治疗”，提前变成“日常预防”。 Yidan Xu is a London-based product designer and innovation researcher, graduated from Innovation Design Engineering at Imperial College London and the Royal College of Art. Her work operates at the intersection of people, science, and design.\nHer individual work has received international recognition, including being named a UK James Dyson Award runner-up, a global top 20, and being featured by Dezeen and Reuters. Her current project is being incubated at Imperial College London, reflecting her focus on translating complex scientific challenges into accessible, real-world solutions with meaningful social impact.\nShe is also interested in exploring cross-disciplinary collaborations and opportunities to extend the impact of her work.\n(She has also contributed to projects at Huawei and Xiaomi Auto, applying a human-centred design approach to complex product systems.) This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":563,"id":"820cd5bd-e776-4718-aeaa-f5837c3aed25","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:55:02+00:00","title":"Why We Miss the Early Signs of Illness | Yidan Xu | TEDxNew Channel Qingdao","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"QwKnfMUsO2w"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"6d532f28da6d0834cf0cbfb6fd107d67f4b3ecda3c63f8211212a80b88edd5d3","description":"One of the biggest diseases affecting leadership today is disease of importance.\nPeople become successful and suddenly become inaccessible.\nThey become powerful and suddenly become rude.\nThey acquire wealth and suddenly become superior.\nThe world does not need more important people. It needs more good people.\nPeople who choose kindness, courage, integrity, service and who refuse to lose themselves.\nAnd if enough of us make that choice, we won’t just change our communities and our countries.\nWe will change the world. Hon. Zainab Buba Galadima is an entrepreneur, poet, retired politician and an advocate for inclusive leadership. With academic and professional experience spanning Nigeria and the United Kingdom, she has served in various public and private sector roles, including as Technical Assistant to former Vice President Prof. Yemi Osinbajo and as an elected councillor and Minority Leader under the Abuja Municipal Area Council.\n\nA multiple-degree holder with master's qualifications in Management & Leadership and Information Technology Management, she is currently pursuing a PhD in Leadership and Strategic Studies at the Nigerian Defence Academy. Beyond public service, she is the founder of several businesses and social impact initiatives focused on entrepreneurship, community development, and youth empowerment.\n\nDriven by a passion for good governance, national unity, and women's participation, Zainab continues to champion ideas and initiatives that create lasting impact across communities. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1111,"id":"8260a949-87d3-4a9c-8a37-a5eecf586908","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:55:47+00:00","title":"I Refuse to Lose Myself | Zainab Buba Galadima | TEDxSamaru","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"dm5y1nnyAO8"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"bbf81d5cca5a40c3126d30cfb4515dc81c5528fac9f56865170e9bd24d6b241e","description":"The idea that depression comes from a chemical imbalance in the brain has shaped treatment for decades. Neuroscientist Lisa Monteggia shares research suggesting that's not the whole story — and shows how a decades-old drug could upend what we thought we knew about depression, with the potential to offer relief within hours. (Recorded at TEDxNashvilleWomen on August 21, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/lisamonteggia\n\nhttps://youtu.be/3Ae4qCvQIcM\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Science #Depression","duration_seconds":792,"id":"83005258-df6f-4037-a84a-2d5a4a6961a8","published_at":"2026-08-16T15:00:17+00:00","title":"Why Depression Isn’t What We Think | Lisa Monteggia | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"3Ae4qCvQIcM"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"42acc2f448f57d2a122dedf0ffff3d1b0c1a796e92a8c89a29876b5e88a339d0","description":"Step inside artist David Szauder's mind, where strange, extravagantly dressed characters live in an ever-changing opera. In this visually stunning talk, he presents a digitally rewired world — part AI-generated dreamscape, part inner theater — and probes the line between artist and algorithm. (Recorded at TEDNext 2025 on November 9, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/davidszauder\n\nhttps://youtu.be/GTCZz_QWBKs\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Art","duration_seconds":418,"id":"83340a12-6054-421c-9d75-d5da57ac6c20","published_at":"2026-06-28T15:00:06+00:00","title":"Welcome to My AI Surrealist World | David Szauder | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"GTCZz_QWBKs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"fdeef260aea54dd85fc3aebe56702b957c0588ba86eeab3dd499d697d2a26ee0","description":"Teacher burnout is often framed as a personal wellness issue, with educators encouraged to practice mindfulness, maintain work-life balance, and engage in self-care. While these strategies can support individual well-being, the dominant self-care narrative can unintentionally shift responsibility for burnout from educational systems to the teachers themselves. Drawing on research exploring the lived experiences of special education teachers who have experienced burnout, this talk challenges the assumption that teacher stress is primarily an individual problem. Instead, it highlights how structural demands—such as excessive workloads, compliance pressures, and limited resources—contribute to burnout across the profession. By reframing the conversation, this talk invites educators, leaders, and policymakers to move beyond individual coping strategies and consider how educational systems can better support teachers in sustainable and meaningful ways. She is an experienced teacher and recently got her PhD in educational sciences. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":274,"id":"84336b8a-354e-48ca-b976-2a40aee0cd2b","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:59:12+00:00","title":"Stop Telling Teachers to Practice Self-Care | Amber Click | TEDxSST Corpus Christi Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Wmcz6rxRsuo"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"60442b5297a0521b0e2851758b50ff3ff071dac93baf857fad7174f7486c4153","description":"K.P. Karunakaran explores how drones are transforming the world, from manned aerial vehicles to advanced systems powered by IC engines. He shares his vision of pushing this technology further, with the ambition of making drone ambulances a reality and redefining engineering. Dr. K.P. Karunakaran is HAL R&D Chair Professor at IIT Bombay, after serving in HAL (1984–92) and IIT Bombay since 1994. His expertise is Manufacturing Automation, CNC, Hybrid Manufacturing, and machine building. He developed two Electron Beam Hybrid Manufacturing machines and pioneered Plasma Focused EB technology for lower-cost systems. He aims to deliver indigenous products at one-tenth import cost. A drone pioneer, he demonstrated drones in India in 2006, built a manned drone in 2021, and trains 750 students each semester to build drones. His current focus is drone ambulances and tipjet helicopters. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1742,"id":"848d8d13-2c6d-40ef-8e09-5f845e044e18","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:09:38+00:00","title":"Drones Are No Longer Toys | K.P. Karunakaran | TEDxSPCE","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"7Ig7riN5Dug"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"aebbade3591431f7850302d9363defea477261296ba097482704f4a281a32662","description":"One month before the Rio Olympics, runner Alexi Pappas couldn't hit her splits in practice. She was begging her watch to change its mind. Then her coach told her to take it off — and shared the best advice she's ever received. That single piece of wisdom led her to break a national record and changed how she chases her goals, carrying her through ultramarathons, a memoir and three films. Bad days aren't a detour, she says — they mean you're right on track.","duration_seconds":87,"id":"857a5563-91fd-49bc-9e6a-1e75fafb0e86","published_at":"2026-08-16T19:00:02+00:00","title":"Bad days aren’t a detour— they mean you’re right on track #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"K7af3rZd6PA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"f11e0dcdff7bf0426baca9736f68012787a08d27682fb2e0f5d7afd1aae9b0a6","description":"我们为什么总觉得“看不过来”？——现代人生活在被影像支配的时代，我们不停观看，却似乎很少真正“看见”。\n从手机相册、社交媒体到我们的收藏夹，卢焘直面现代人“盲目观看”的困境，探讨“看”如何从感官体验变成了压迫性机制，并带来无止境的焦虑。\n他的演讲是一次哲学式的反思，提出一个深刻的问题：在过剩的观看中，我们如何真正“看见”？ 「情节严重社」频道主理人\n哲学与人文内容创作者 This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":771,"id":"86460b3a-fb66-4d3f-b0a2-acd77e2d5855","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:48:25+00:00","title":"“看”的现代困境：我们观看一切，却什么也看不见 | 焘 卢 | TEDxNanshan Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"CTMjdD4wPPA"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"7ebcae3aa095f7dd9d18fe935a5a1aa03a8a8ff719861790f2cb5ca888bea18d","description":"AI artist Mick Mahler has a counterintuitive take: the more powerful the machines get, the less the technology actually matters. Showing delightful examples of his own art, from jazz-playing spiders to a Kafka-inspired beetle film, he explains how you can use new technology to serve your vision (not replace it). (Recorded at TEDNext 2025 on November 10, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/mickmahler\n\nhttps://youtu.be/w24zeYdwnXU\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #AI","duration_seconds":455,"id":"86c1f448-ea09-4dd1-98f4-ba3f3c277aa8","published_at":"2026-07-27T15:00:17+00:00","title":"How to Stand Out in the Ocean of AI Slop | Mick Mahler | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"w24zeYdwnXU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"aca2dceb189eb67f2a14138f24451a2ca1bf6249f61992eb8a209aab5b8142a7","description":"When we were 10, the world felt enormous - a little scary, perhaps - but also limitless, like everything was waiting for us to discover \nTEDxRGSV Youth - 404: The Forgotten Archive  - 2025 High school student from RGSV Year 11 student This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":602,"id":"88a0e9c6-9538-449f-a47f-e94080e35933","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:57:14+00:00","title":"The Missing Files of Our Imagination | Thanh An Nguyen | TEDxRGSV Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Bj4afbGtLCk"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"62ad5cbd00f048a79fe34280caa1c8fb45b20d2ce6cf6a75df2358e6757afc2d","description":"Why prioritizing short term goals over a long enough time horizon can greatly influence success in all phases of your life. Anthony Rapillo started cooking as a way to honor his mother after she passed away nearly 20 years ago. He's doing just that with Layla's Son Hospitality, a burgeoning company specializing in experience-driven restaurants, bars, catering and hospitality consulting. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":624,"id":"89888500-de41-49bf-9538-fdd7bc6bb041","published_at":"2026-09-01T16:18:04+00:00","title":"Stop Making 5 Year Plans | Anthony Rapillo | TEDxRaleigh","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"MGQKxZ2FIVk"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"6021ff1f04f5e70db916e1d8fc781280b8791d54fabb8bb666ad7dd0e35cf1c7","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\n\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\n\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\n\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology Strategic communications and knowledge-management professional with extensive experience in development-sector communication. Her work has covered issues including climate change, health, gender equality, WASH, financial inclusion and organisational storytelling. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":620,"id":"89f90c93-33d6-4b85-a588-54ba90d4380c","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:13:32+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Identity | Pallavi Dhakal | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"lK3DLUOVT9A"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"13a220cc7d9a0775b08095e40ca6a4fb3b313dcbf8a6cdb6e56d256d6470967b","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\n\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology\n An outspoken, dynamic leader with a natural flair for public speaking and debate, Sunny leads his class with confidence, vitality, and clear conviction. Driven by a deep enthusiasm for global perspectives and debate, he is a World Scholar’s Cup (WSC) Global Qualifier and a recognized orator who thrives on the big stage. Whether guiding his peers in the classroom or commanding the room during a presentation, Sunny consistently turns ideas into engaging, impactful dialogue. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":151,"id":"8b4c1af1-5a20-4bee-85d4-423efed63705","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:12:56+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Identity | Sunny Das | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"WUg-XPfJRic"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"b2f8e0b1673b6d10e0e48849827e9a7b80f464385c95ffa4ecbfc65bcc2247ad","description":"Luciana acredita que as conexões mais transformadoras acontecem quando nos permitimos viver o novo. Ao longo de sua trajetória, tem criado experiências que unem cultura, aprendizado e desenvolvimento humano, incentivando mulheres a ampliarem repertórios, fortalecerem vínculos e construírem novas possibilidades. Seu trabalho ultrapassa fronteiras ao promover encontros em diferentes países, sempre guiado pela certeza de que cada experiência pode marcar o início de uma nova travessia. Luciana acredita que as conexões mais transformadoras acontecem quando nos permitimos viver o novo. Ao longo de sua trajetória, tem criado experiências que unem cultura, aprendizado e desenvolvimento humano, incentivando mulheres a ampliarem repertórios, fortalecerem vínculos e construírem novas possibilidades.\n\nSeu trabalho ultrapassa fronteiras ao promover encontros em diferentes países, sempre guiado pela certeza de que cada experiência pode marcar o início de uma nova travessia. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":683,"id":"8b6867f5-7d72-400d-9b1d-ead62e34d532","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:46:16+00:00","title":"Perdi o chão e descobri que tenho asas para voar | Luciana Mitri | TEDxEloy Chaves Women","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"uVRNExT5Jys"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"431aee60cbf100252bca7d817418fa2c4c0fec2d67bad8c7acafafea0dacb067","description":"The places where we grow aren't always classrooms or universities. A gaming community where I least expected to learn skills, is a place that I learnt a lot of lessons from. I’m Varun KJ, a third-year Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning student who loves exploring technology, building new ideas, and trying things outside the usual path. I’m also a gamer, and gaming has helped me build my confidence, communication, and problem-solving skills in ways I never expected. Alongside academics, I’m involved in Toastmasters, startups, and creative projects. I’m always curious about learning new things, meeting people, and turning my experiences into something meaningful. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":666,"id":"8b9efda8-6a9d-4d4f-9244-df90382ff6bf","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:49:43+00:00","title":"Life Lessons I Learnt From My Gaming Community | Varun KJ | TEDxKGCAS","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"DzGllFMvwy8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"223be173ae03ed9f79b2577d2e031e746e4108d2a4e16d129f7426aafc19a625","description":"Not every achievement comes with a camera, applause, or congratulations. Some of the most important victories happen quietly, when nobody is watching. This talk explores the power of small wins and the invisible progress we make through everyday choices, whether in our health, relationships, habits, or personal growth. Drawing on stories of perseverance and failure, including the journey of Sachin Tendulkar, it shows how seemingly insignificant steps gradually shape who we become. The talk reminds us that progress is not always a dramatic finish line, but the collection of small, unseen miles that make us stronger and more capable than we were before.\n Mr Suvarnanidhi Rao is Associate Director – Talent Acquisition, Campus & Vendor Lead at Dentsu Global Services, where he oversees end‑to‑end campus hiring, strategic vendor partnerships, and large‑scale talent acquisition initiatives. With extensive experience in building talent pipelines for global delivery centres, he focuses on aligning recruitment strategy with business growth, employer branding, and early‑career development. In his role, he designs and executes campus engagement programs, collaborates with premier institutions, and shapes frameworks that attract and nurture future‑ready talent for the digital and creative economy. A strong advocate for skilling, diversity, and inclusive hiring, Suvarnanidhi brings a strategic, people‑first perspective to how organizations can build resilient, high‑performing teams in a rapidly evolving workplace. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1098,"id":"8ba3debc-c69c-444a-b908-af9d2b95eced","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:19:54+00:00","title":"The Power of Small Wins that Nobody Sees | Suvarnanidhi Rao | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"bshxBf9s1hc"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"71fe03313fce7f80faca290a22e731ee61a257016cf966856fa8e725aec97a4c","description":"This talk explores what makes a city not only competitive, but human. Drawing on\ninterviews with investment promotion agencies and economic development\norganizations, it reflects on how investors and decision-makers from different\nnationalities look beyond infrastructure, technology, and business conditions to ask a\ndeeper question: can people feel safe, welcomed, connected, and able to build a life\nhere? At its heart, this is a talk about future cities that are smart, sustainable, and\ndeeply human.\n\n Alia El Banna is a Professor of Marketing at Porto Business School, co-director of the International MBA, and an entrepreneur. Her research examines how places are represented and experienced, and how these representations shape the decisions and behaviours of residents, tourists, investors, and skilled workers. She focuses on place brand co-creation, authentic place narratives, the factors influencing place-related decisions, and the role of media in crafting compelling stories about place. Her work explores how the images of countries, cities, towns, and neighbourhoods intersect with culture, identity, heritage, and belonging, shaping stakeholder perceptions in meaningful ways. Alia’s research has been published in leading marketing journals and as book chapters by top academic publishers, and she has received multiple best paper awards at international conferences. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":932,"id":"8baad6e2-af2d-4297-bfae-503d7b7ed796","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:57:07+00:00","title":"What makes a city human? | Alia El Banna | TEDxPortoSalon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"HJibxNWRUzI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"502606ac654cf06511744d7eebf3fca9648c28f2d01a8498968d22c29940e374","description":"Harday Gupta shares how saying “yes” to opportunities, even without having all the answers, transformed his journey from a backbencher into an entrepreneur and author. Through his experiences in building a company, entering the SME IPO ecosystem and growing a venture to over ₹25 crore in annual turnover, he explores the power of curiosity, relationships and continuous learning. The talk challenges us to rethink expertise and reminds us that sometimes, the willingness to learn and take the right opportunity matters more than knowing everything from the start. Harday Gupta is an entrepreneur and communication professional who built his career around one simple philosophy: never say no to an opportunity to learn. A Tourism Management graduate, he entered the communication industry after being advised to explore a different career path. Over seven years, he worked across three agencies in Noida, Chandigarh and Delhi before launching his own venture. What began with strong industry relationships and a willingness to learn eventually led him into the SME IPO ecosystem, where his company has managed more than 75 IPO mandates since 2023. His work helped the venture grow from zero to a turnover exceeding ₹25 crore in a year. Alongside entrepreneurship, Harday pursued a PhD, completing his thesis in three and a half years, and has authored a book with another in the works. His journey reflects curiosity, adaptability, relationship-building and the courage to embrace opportunities even when they fall outside one's expertise. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":419,"id":"8c37d242-3f89-4ce1-9a6d-2196b82440d3","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:46:20+00:00","title":"IPOs after Tourism | Harday Gupta | TEDxAraghar","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"A0cdrw6QPOc"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"62f25e3f447a70d02b956eef1e0ac1e943d0209a398f32addc25985499efb325","description":"What if humor is more than entertainment-and actually a tool for healing? Clinical psychologist and stand-up comedian Dr. Kristen Wynns explores how humor can help us create connection, cope with adversity, and build resilience. Drawing from psychology, her own unexpected journey into comedy, and deeply personal experiences, she reveals that a sense of humor isn’t just something you’re born with-it’s a skill we can strengthen. Her challenge: become a little less serious, a little more silly, and discover how laughter can change the way we move through life’s hardest moments. Dr. Kristen Wynns is a child and adolescent psychologist, founder of Wynns Family Psychology, and author of The No Wimpy Parenting Handbook, whose work has shaped parenting and youth mental health for over 25 years. A sought-after media expert, speaker, and stand-up comedian, she blends psychology and humor to help people build resilience, confidence, and meaningful connection. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":556,"id":"8d030da9-d7fe-44fd-9175-8c79327fd86b","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:54:17+00:00","title":"The Healing Power of Humor | Dr. Kristen Wynns | TEDxRaleigh","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"vh0DWLQCmG0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"bb6cf77786e1bdca16400da431699ba21802813dbba009f7cef0af4b031fdb20","description":"Defensora da aprendizagem ao longo da vida, Débora acredita que a verdadeira transformação acontece quando ampliamos quem somos, e não apenas o que fazemos. Ela encontra nessas experiências inspiração para cultivar disciplina, curiosidade e a coragem de recomeçar. Com mais de 20 anos de experiência na indústria de tecnologia, Débora Milone lidera projetos de transformação organizacional e inovação em empresas globais, conectando estratégia, pessoas e tecnologia para impulsionar mudanças com impacto sustentável. Engenheira eletricista e mestre em Administração de Empresas, desenvolveu sua carreira conduzindo iniciativas de transformação digital e desenvolvimento de negócios em diferentes países.\n\nDefensora da aprendizagem ao longo da vida, acredita que a verdadeira transformação acontece quando ampliamos quem somos, e não apenas o que fazemos. Mãe de Rafael e Gustavo, maratonista e apaixonada por música, encontra nessas experiências inspiração para cultivar disciplina, curiosidade e a coragem de recomeçar. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":684,"id":"8dc8b3e6-688d-427a-9dc1-1e90e8a0959e","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:41:16+00:00","title":"Que nova versão de mim espera para nascer... | Debora Milone | TEDxEloy Chaves Women","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Hx3XexhwaEE"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"acfbc3e4a4f80a739e8e2bc01d27f1e90061076070c39a37ff8c87c4636efde7","description":"Jason’s speech is a micro‑epic of personal growth. From giving a tearful charity speech in a park in primary school to grabbing every chance for international exchange and class presentations in high school, his story brims with real‑life struggles, preparation and breakthroughs. G11 student from Jinrui School Shanghai This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":724,"id":"8e4f9e97-5e08-489e-a9b7-efb061f5c261","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:17:50+00:00","title":"From “I Can’t” to “I Can” — A Ten‑Year Long March of Courage | Jason Wang | TEDxJRSS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"tePwr3AMUh0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"1e65d2dc7cdb35ec522a2deeb44fa05d64c82c589374592df5ce5a26a39dfbf1","description":"Anna Carolina reflete sobre a fadiga atencional provocada pelo excesso de estímulos digitais e sobre como espaços aparentemente neutros, como galerias no modelo do “cubo branco” e residências minimalistas, podem perder a capacidade de envolver as pessoas. Segundo ela, ambientes desprovidos de personalidade, memória e afeto não são necessariamente acolhedores. A pandemia tornou ainda mais evidente que a arquitetura influencia diretamente o bem-estar físico e emocional e que nenhum espaço é verdadeiramente neutro.\n\nA partir de experiências familiares com doenças neurológicas e limitações físicas, a arquiteta passou a investigar a relação entre ambiente, comportamento humano e neurociência. Ela questiona o modelo tradicional usado como referência na arquitetura: um homem adulto, destro, fisicamente íntegro e neurologicamente considerado “normal”. Esse padrão ignora mulheres, idosos, pessoas com deficiência, autismo, TDAH, depressão e inúmeras outras particularidades. Para Anna Carolina, projetar exige compreender que cores, formas, sons e texturas podem ativar estados de ansiedade e alerta ou favorecer relaxamento e segurança.\n\nComo exemplo prático, ela apresenta a expografia de uma exposição de Siron Franco, estruturada pela neuroarquitetura em oito módulos dedicados a temas como ditadura, Césio-137, feminicídio e sincretismo religioso. Cores, texturas, organização espacial e sons foram utilizados para modular a carga cognitiva e aproximar o visitante da tensão e da carga emocional das obras. Sua conclusão é que, diante da saturação de estímulos visuais, ver já não basta: é preciso sentir. A arquitetura, assim como a arte, deve transformar o espaço em uma experiência vivida pelo corpo. Architect and urban planner, co-founder of Arte Plena Casa Galeria, and a council member of CAU-GO, the Architecture and Urbanism Council of Goiás. Her work focuses on sensory and affective architecture, developing residential and commercial projects that integrate space, perception, art, and human experience. She also works in cultural and event production, bringing a sensitive and artistic perspective to architecture and institutional practice. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":554,"id":"8f6a7216-bd72-4261-b87d-4e778386aa6e","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:56:42+00:00","title":"Neuroarquitetura: modos de vi(ver) e sentir os espaços | Anna Carolina Cruz | TEDxGoiânia","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"in7dIpOSlE4"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"cab8f056acdcc5b67dffd54faa47491e889b44618eb6848b1fd32547db1b1b66","description":"Lead poisoning robs the world's kids of millions of IQ points a day and kills more people than wars, natural disasters and road accidents combined — yet we treat it as a problem we've already solved. Drew McCartor, who runs the nonprofit Pure Earth, has spent nearly two decades proving it doesn't have to be this way. He presents their three-step fix that's already working in places from Georgia to Ghana, and makes the case that we can finally beat a crisis hiding in plain sight. (This ambitious idea is part of The Audacious Project, TED’s initiative to inspire and fund global change.) (Recorded at TED2026 on April 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/drewmccartor\n\nhttps://youtu.be/57afWpv-9u4\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Health","duration_seconds":591,"id":"8fd39b00-30bb-4f19-8028-ff30332e2c11","published_at":"2026-07-23T15:00:36+00:00","title":"The Invisible Threat Taking Away Our Brightest Minds | Drew McCartor | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"57afWpv-9u4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"cada236c92e879998a4d68182ac2ff32dfbdd1149969a750cfeb6ff41f29cdfc","description":"The reality of the AI revolution, and an optimistic guide to navigating uncertainty and constant change.\n\n Professor Michael Milford is a globally recognised researcher, the Director of the QUT Centre for Robotics, and one of Australia’s leading voices on the opportunities and risks of AI and robotics. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":575,"id":"8ffae189-a0cf-4934-9abf-2cdb08edf49f","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:32:58+00:00","title":"What experts aren't telling you about AI | Prof. Michael Milford | TEDxQUT","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"sdpY0wjK7wY"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"b3cd067570dad6ccccaa564d891ab03a31f794bd4d72350df3851cc6808daec2","description":"María Alejandra, a 10th-grade IB student at Gimnasio El Hontanar, explores how culture influences the way we express ourselves, connect with others, and understand the world around us. Through her talk, she highlights the power of creativity and self-expression as ways of celebrating identity, sharing ideas, and building meaningful connections across cultures. María Alejandra is a dedicated 10th-grade IB student at Gimnasio El Hontanar who brings confidence, curiosity, and thoughtful insight to everything she does. As a young speaker, she enjoys exploring meaningful ideas, expressing her perspectives, and connecting with others through authentic communication. Her commitment to learning and willingness to challenge herself reflect the qualities of an internationally minded student and a promising young voice. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":728,"id":"932b6d4e-ffde-4fa0-bf62-76a513afab30","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:40:57+00:00","title":"Culture, expression & creativity | MARIA ALEJANDRA BEDOYA | TEDxGimnasio El Hontanar Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"KXgyRRGi7kI"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"ea8b622dcdc6b288502fff6a7c64dc6272541aa1b40062c1bd6040f9f1e44e77","description":"When was the last time you just ... played? Creative health scientist Katina Bajaj thinks adults are in a \"play deprivation crisis,\" where we've replaced our fun-loving human nature with optimization and efficiency. She makes the case that play isn't a reward for productivity but rather a survival skill — and invites you to rediscover the freedom of not knowing where something will end up. (Recorded at Play@TED on May 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/katinabajaj\n\nhttps://youtu.be/LGb0_ed_euA\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Creativity","duration_seconds":722,"id":"933f143d-48d2-408d-8df0-f6d5ad92a6d5","published_at":"2026-08-14T15:00:18+00:00","title":"How Play Boosts Your Creativity and Resilience | Katina Bajaj | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"LGb0_ed_euA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c15a7914f25ba8988085f761105fb32b4002f22614f90e2783441a34237f3bff","description":"O que acontece quando o caos encontra a poesia? Com a mente acelerada e a sensação de estar sempre atrasado para a própria vida, Tarcio Costa encontrou na literatura de cordel mais do que uma forma de expressão: encontrou estrutura, pertencimento e propósito. Nesta talk, ele conta como a disciplina dos versos e das rimas o reconectou com suas origens e o ajudou a transformar dores e inquietações em linguagem. Ao entrelaçar arte, experiência pessoal e reflexões inspiradas na logoterapia, Tarcio mostra que encontrar sentido não elimina o caos — mas pode nos oferecer um lugar seguro para onde voltar. Tárcio Costa é ator, diretor, encenador, poeta e cordelista. Sua trajetória artística é marcada pela valorização da cultura popular brasileira e pelo uso da palavra como instrumento de expressão, memória e identidade. Ao longo de sua carreira, levou sua arte a diferentes públicos e territórios, conquistando reconhecimento nacional e internacional por trabalhos que unem tradição, criatividade e reflexão. Em suas apresentações, teatro, poesia e oralidade se encontram para mostrar que histórias, quando compartilhadas, têm o poder de atravessar fronteiras e conectar pessoas. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":879,"id":"94bee91d-9cd2-49de-8803-e2e069010342","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:11:33+00:00","title":"À procura do meu sentido | Tarcio Costa | TEDxAraraquara","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"hG0KTY7BlNE"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"b6e7a47d0e00887af841672dbbee215dbafaca1021cfb09d1cb64d3d3c66ab78","description":"Can a five-minute walk change how you feel all day? Exercise scientist Keith Diaz shows how your body is built for the kind of movement that modern life has quietly erased — and suggests something refreshingly doable: making time for small doses of movement sprinkled throughout the day, as a way to boost your brain and body.","duration_seconds":93,"id":"951703bb-2da5-4e64-8a13-36dfec2d24eb","published_at":"2026-07-31T18:00:36+00:00","title":"Your body is build for movement — so make it a part of your day #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"MIgbq4217n4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9fa6f1602e63b1e4ba727fd494e47439fc2dbade12d35b57fd82c3b2d8d14cc9","description":"What happens if you're never chosen?\n\nFrom an early age, many women are taught that life begins after someone chooses them, a partner, an employer, an opportunity. We learn to wait for permission, validation, and the \"right time\" before building the lives we truly want.\n\nIn this TEDx talk, Krysta Monet explores what happens if that moment never comes and why waiting to be chosen may be one of the greatest limitations we place on ourselves. \n\nThrough her deeply personal decision to become a single mother by choice, she challenges the belief that a meaningful life must follow a traditional timeline and offers a new perspective on agency, courage, and creating a life on your own terms.\n\nThis isn't a talk about motherhood. It's about identity, possibility, and the quiet revolution that begins when we stop asking, \"Who will choose me?\" and start asking, \"What will I choose for myself?\" Krysta Monet is a writer, speaker, and cultural commentator exploring the complexities of modern womanhood. Through essays, conversations, and live experiences, she examines the invisible rules women inherit about love, ambition, identity, loneliness, and the courage to choose lives that feel true instead of simply expected. Her work invites women to question cultural narratives and create lives on their own terms. She has been featured in media outlets such as Cosmopolitan and FOX26. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":593,"id":"972c30a7-3baa-4a87-aa66-bae6c35f16ec","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:48:46+00:00","title":"Single Mother by Choice | Krysta Monet | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"qDJywWjuu_8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"daff8156adeabe94c3d879cddb4ca5d7ea983cd8cf7332839e900692e695388a","description":"Truth is rarely one-dimensional; it can reveal itself differently through different perspectives and experiences. This talk explores the “glitches” in reality that challenge our assumptions and remind us that every perspective holds a piece of the truth. Professor of Managerial Communication and business communication consultant, Dr. Madhavi Gokhale brings decades of experience in teaching, leadership, and storytelling. n alumna of IIT Bombay, and a former Program Chairperson at the School of Business Management, NMIMS, she has trained professional across institutions such as SPJIMR and the National Police Academy blending communication, literature, and leadership This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1497,"id":"97791bed-fda5-4f8f-add3-d5639e823cd2","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:09:25+00:00","title":"Glitches In Reality or Truth Uncovered? | Dr Madhavi Gokhale | TEDxSPCE","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"RuZXiC5jC7k"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"de0e21e2f7f0816a554dcc1f8bc1ff787893205fa8be1daaec613bf67a782bb8","description":"¿Sabías que en 1903 una joven chilena tuvo que vestirse de hombre durante cinco años para poder trabajar tranquila y acceder a un mejor sueldo? A partir de este relato, la charla cuestiona si realmente estamos mejor hoy en día, demostrando que el progreso real de una nación no se mide solo en cifras de crecimiento económico o del PIB, sino en la expansión concreta de las oportunidades de vida de las personas. Al final, el expositor nos recuerda que, aunque el desarrollo actual no elimina las injusticias pendientes, la herencia más valiosa es habernos entregado un país con muchas más posibilidades que el que recibieron las generaciones pasadas. José Tomás Valente es Ingeniero Comercial y Magíster en Economía de la Pontificia Universidad Católica de Chile, y cuenta además con un MPA/ID (Máster en Economía y Desarrollo Internacional) de la Escuela de Gobierno de la Universidad de Harvard. Actualmente se desempeña como cofundador y CEO de Betterplan, una fintech dedicada a la asesoría de inversiones y gestión de patrimonio, que fundó en 2020. Anteriormente fue asesor económico del Ministerio de Hacienda de Chile e investigador del Centro Latinoamericano de Políticas Económicas y Sociales de la Universidad Católica (Clapes UC), además de colaborador editorial de The Economist Intelligence Unit y columnista del diario PULSO (La Tercera). Ha sido también docente en las facultades de Derecho y Periodismo de la Pontificia Universidad Católica de Chile. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1100,"id":"9a570518-dd17-4610-962b-f0c4adecd7bb","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:38:16+00:00","title":"¿Estamos mejor? | José Tomás Valente | TEDxPUC de Chile","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"xHGSAQ_pdCo"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"1812ffcb600a57033b367a3e042e60c222d717c15f9713f01b97621fa73701c3","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\n\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology\n Clear-thinking, articulate, and poised, Saurya brings both athleticism and sharp communication to every role she takes on. Serving as House Vice Captain, she leads by example—demonstrating resilience and drive whether steering house initiatives or competing as a accomplished swimmer. Marked by her strong command of the English language and her outspoken nature, Saury is a persuasive speaker who effortlessly presents well-reasoned arguments and inspires those around her. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":406,"id":"9cf63e33-fb0d-475e-98f4-f39f6d7e36ec","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:12:46+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Identity | Saurya Pandey | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"MTWlmREFQ74"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"108e4d39d96056e40f490b46dd313e658f89ec52840b23a2d071259a55e9b4fb","description":"For years, the warning has been: smartphones are destroying a generation. But developmental psychologist Candice Odgers says that decades of data on teens tells a different story — violence, alcohol use and pregnancy are at historic lows, and research shows social media may not actually be the culprit for mental health problems. Hear her analysis of what’s really troubling kids these days, and why banning them from online spaces may make things worse. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/candiceodgers\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Vzbz--aPQLE\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #SocialMedia","duration_seconds":744,"id":"9d73493a-f55c-41bf-a323-90454f6cc024","published_at":"2026-07-02T15:00:19+00:00","title":"Why a Social Media Ban Won’t Save Teens | Candice Odgers | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Vzbz--aPQLE"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c04478943a58e02d2442f16fcd7f4db48fed6f14348016b3c25441fd7f391e28","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\n\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology\n Creative, outgoing, and effortlessly approachable, this New Delhi native is currently pursuing a Bachelor’s in Business Management while actively exploring a wide range of interests. A national-level Muay Thai champion and former Sports Captain, they blend physical discipline and resilience with a cheerful, welcoming demeanor that naturally brings people together. Their diverse background spans event hosting, public speaking, and community service. Deeply expressive and curious, they find inspiration in sports, music, writing, and daily journaling. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":440,"id":"a0f36e83-6d56-407e-9726-f37533183552","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:12:12+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Identity | Palmo Sherpa | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"w1DELz06Yqs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"f13bda9d6b083d9a480eb619e49ab02c109c4d3f3a0e1c16ef63087ead880ddc","description":"Sofia examines how social media influences attention, cognition, identity, and self-worth. It explores the role of dopamine and algorithms in encouraging continuous engagement and highlights the importance of three essential skills: directing attention, thinking critically, and developing self-awareness. Ultimately, it argues that conscious technology use is essential for maintaining personal freedom and identity. I am a high school senior in the chemical-biological program, interested in understanding how the world and society work and in creating positive change by leaving a meaningful impact. I enjoy reflecting, writing, traveling, exercising, and trying a bit of everything. Something people don’t usually know about me is that I’m good at dancing, writing, and sports. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":449,"id":"a1a1c201-76ee-49d0-a05f-ed1e9a9199b1","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:40:13+00:00","title":"Who Controls Who? Attention in Digital Age | Sofía Regina Marquez | TEDxPrepas La Salle Bajio Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"_U1T987_-8w"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"ef7f13bdb62028ebd28386001bbf9e1a4f43dfc77cf55cc406ec9856651682c3","description":"There is nothing wrong with how you think. Our mindset might just be right for a room and wrong for another. I am Susenna N, currently pursuing a B.Com at KG College of Arts and Science, Coimbatore. I'm building a base for my career in finance and data analytics through real-time work with Python, SQL, and Power BI. I'm a national-level artistic yoga artist and a zonal-level chess player as well, and I enjoy crocheting, video editing, and logo designing in my free time. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":612,"id":"a2f09e17-8270-4c22-a459-873a39d3e686","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:49:15+00:00","title":"Right Mind, Wrong Room | Susenna N | TEDxKGCAS","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"NvV4ICkdko8"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"a87ee7a44a0d2fe7b0dabf761f9713db5d991e6bc9c8cd76bf6eed80625c20bd","description":"What good is a vaccine people don't trust? Immunologist and TED Fellow Kizzmekia Corbett co-developed the first COVID-19 vaccine to enter clinical trials — but she says making the vaccine was never the hardest part. The real obstacle is the gap between scientists and the people who have honest questions about the shots they're being asked to take. She shares a refreshing approach for taking on vaccine inquisitiveness and ensuring that humanity is ready for the next pandemic. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/kizzmekiacorbett\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Vz9JMC18LFs\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Science #PublicHealth","duration_seconds":596,"id":"a3b395d7-c409-43a4-b252-7eba3c616e25","published_at":"2026-07-06T15:00:39+00:00","title":"Your Vaccine Questions Deserve Real Answers | Kizzmekia Corbett | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Vz9JMC18LFs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2f51be917cfde1ceaf0f781ec3ed18b84bf297320701574e82594ee36cfed146","description":"May Amorim apresenta a solidão como um problema de saúde pública relacionado à redução das conexões sociais e ao crescimento do uso de tecnologias. Ela observa que muitos jovens já mantêm interações afetivas contínuas com sistemas de inteligência artificial e alerta para um possível ciclo: quanto menos nos relacionamos com outras pessoas, mais perdemos nossa “musculatura social”, reduzindo a capacidade de criar vínculos e aumentando a sensação de isolamento.\n\nA palestrante, porém, evita tratar a tecnologia apenas como ameaça. A partir dos conceitos de tecnoafetividade, amor como aceitação do outro e afeto como aquilo que amplia ou limita nossa capacidade de agir, ela questiona se a inteligência artificial também poderia fortalecer relações humanas. Embora a ferramenta não experimente sentimentos, o vínculo percebido pelo usuário pode ser verdadeiro. Além disso, habilidades desenvolvidas nessas interações podem ser transferidas para relacionamentos entre pessoas.\n\nMay exemplifica essa possibilidade contando como utilizou um chatbot para compreender melhor a comunicação e as necessidades de uma pessoa autista com quem começava a se relacionar. A inteligência artificial não decidiu por ela, mas ajudou a construir repertório, empatia e segurança até que a experiência real tornasse esse apoio desnecessário. Sua conclusão é que precisamos educar, regulamentar e usar criticamente essas ferramentas, sem abandonar os encontros humanos: bem calibrada, a tecnologia pode deixar de substituir vínculos e passar a potencializar nossa capacidade de nos tornarmos mais humanos. Mayara Amorim is a Social Health Strategist, researcher of human relationships, and TEDx Speaker. Working at the intersection of behavior, culture, technology, and the future, she explores how rapid technological and social transformations are reshaping the way people, organizations, and communities connect.\nAs a business consultant, speaker, and facilitator, she translates her research into strategies and experiences that strengthen relationships, transform organizational cultures, and expand human adaptability in a rapidly changing world. Her work is guided by a central question: As technology advances exponentially, how can we sustain meaningful human relationships? This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":681,"id":"a405237c-a9a0-4eed-b38c-c0ff6fdd8bb7","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:45:29+00:00","title":"Tecnologia: um potencializador das relações humanas? | Mayara Amorim | TEDxGoiânia","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:54.793002+00:00","youtube_video_id":"fjJRaasGeXk"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d1e6b7bb2d31f52875998e5514c297772eb97f5b398f27298582f8386341af79","description":"When is the last time you did absolutely nothing for 10 whole minutes? Not texting, talking or even thinking? Mindfulness expert Andy Puddicombe describes the transformative power of doing just that: Refreshing your mind for 10 minutes a day, simply by being mindful and experiencing the present moment. (No need for incense or sitting in uncomfortable positions.)","duration_seconds":87,"id":"a5b85dff-340d-456d-9a2a-009848f42754","published_at":"2026-08-01T19:00:02+00:00","title":"Your thoughts can stay— you just stop wrestling them. #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"8W3_ifwVwKY"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"dec82b72eb7717536c4302863e3d92572ee55e6445c4b759b54e28db9313e50a","description":"When professional child actor Suki Hillier landed the title role in \"Matilda The Musical,\" she thought she would have to learn how to become someone else. Instead, she realized she had known the role since she was three, when she playfully tried to move objects with her mind the way Matilda does. In this joyful talk, she reveals the similarities between the stage and life — because imagination isn't about escaping the world. It’s a way of building one. (Recorded at Play@TED on May 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/sukihillier\n\nhttps://youtu.be/TeVE4TQPEwM\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Theater","duration_seconds":307,"id":"a70f70fa-4ef0-417a-848d-25d0ff387adc","published_at":"2026-08-18T15:30:28+00:00","title":"The Power of Imagination — Onstage and Off | Suki Hillier | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"TeVE4TQPEwM"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d9752c0c37634eea605685a897a78a7cc3e3846a0803cd1c5dcaeb9bbb34b711","description":"After finding himself alone in an unreported wildfire in the woodlands of California, John Mills decided to take matters into his own hands. Hear the incredible story of how he rallied fire survivors and retired first responders to create Watch Duty, the nonprofit emergency alert system that’s beating official government warnings and buying people precious minutes to escape danger.","duration_seconds":88,"id":"a71e492b-bba4-4ccf-bb54-1da760fd0480","published_at":"2026-08-05T22:00:01+00:00","title":"During a wildfire, every second counts #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"4eowPocR2Y8"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"25d4e342ba6ae5a5afcbdbeab2b04cc0d90074ff37b7b7950201e24718f0fc59","description":"When was your last unforgettable trip? Travel industry expert Suresh Subudhi believes joyful moments of connection are the reason we explore the world — but with vacation hotspots overrun and locals pushed to the brink, tourists have become unwelcome guests. Proposing a new way to use technology to stop loving our favorite destinations to death, he shows what travel could look like if we got it right. (Recorded at TED@BCG on October 23, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/sureshsubudhi\n\nhttps://youtu.be/jM5MIRDCL-8\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Travel","duration_seconds":697,"id":"a81308d3-7153-44db-b748-f11286e0d028","published_at":"2026-08-09T15:00:07+00:00","title":"Why Overtourism Could Ruin Your Next Vacation | Suresh Subudhi | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"jM5MIRDCL-8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"4cb0b36b355e3b74ddc9f08a14c3a1b32e123d743c76ff0a44e011cb03f406c3","description":"Scientists are warning that AI is the greatest threat to human survival since the invention of nuclear weapons. This talk will explain the reasons why the creation of superhuman AI would be detrimental and what PauseAI and others are trying to do about it. Joseph Miller a PhD student in Machine Learning at the University of Oxford, he is also the founder of Pause AI which advocates for leaders to pause unsafe AI development until real safeguards are put into place. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1075,"id":"a8ee400f-3535-48b0-9d29-1096ab1cbde3","published_at":"2026-09-02T16:04:29+00:00","title":"Why I'm trying to pause AI | Joseph Miller | TEDxUniversityofStAndrews","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"x8XiL5nhvGw"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e46e4d8225d312582f54369e98074ad109c2fe76709f95f6dec88a3adf232ca0","description":"Do you sometimes have your most creative ideas while folding laundry, washing dishes or doing nothing in particular? It's because when your body goes on autopilot, your brain gets busy forming new neural connections that connect ideas and solve problems. Learn to love being bored as Manoush Zomorodi explains the connection between spacing out and creativity.","duration_seconds":66,"id":"a9450e42-379e-4b75-931b-c4cd31ffe69d","published_at":"2026-08-11T19:00:00+00:00","title":"Let this be your last Short for the day #TEDTalk","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"gU_7tuSo7AU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"a27f03d78ce7bda5b56e8f4951dcefa3cbb489511f03dce7ea332105dc79c110","description":"We live in a world obsessed with building solutions—but what if the real breakthrough is not in building more, but in building together?\n\nIn this thought-provoking talk, Praveen Thiyagarajan explores how communities can become powerful catalysts for innovation, personal growth, and collective impact. Drawing from his journey as an entrepreneur and community builder, he challenges the conventional idea that meaningful change is created by individuals working alone.\n\nThrough the lens of the youth ecosystem, Praveen reveals how the right communities can connect people, ideas, opportunities, and experiences—turning individual potential into collective action.\n\nUnder the theme “Redesigning Reality,” he invites us to rethink what it means to build solutions: perhaps the most powerful thing we can build is not a solution, but a community capable of creating many solutions together. Praveen Thiyagarajan is a community builder, entrepreneur, and youth ecosystem enabler dedicated to bridging the gap between education and industry. As the Co-Founder of CampusX Community, he has built a thriving platform that empowers students and young professionals through mentorship, networking, career development, and innovation-driven opportunities.\n\nAt TEDxKanniyakumari 2026, under the theme \"Redesigning Reality,\" Praveen shares how communities have become one of the most powerful catalysts for personal growth, innovation, and collective impact. His talk explores why the future belongs not just to individuals, but to those who build and belong to communities that inspire action and unlock potential. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":627,"id":"aa74d05c-0c26-46db-aa03-6878ae041350","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:10:48+00:00","title":"Stop building solutions—do this instead | Praveen Thiyagarajan | TEDxKanniyakumari","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"H0uCta1TlVw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"d1d10b4147a46ba1329815003c2f03b92a25c21ec7a2ab75b19a1e5baa124f5c","description":"Vivi acredita que a forma como lideramos os outros começa pela maneira como conduzimos a nós mesmos. Ao longo de sua trajetória, desenvolveu uma visão de liderança que une estratégia, inteligência emocional e relações humanas para formar equipes mais autônomas e conscientes. Para ela, a verdadeira liderança nasce da coragem de olhar para si antes de conduzir qualquer transformação. Vivi Quiessi acredita que a forma como lideramos os outros começa pela maneira como conduzimos a nós mesmos. Ao longo de sua trajetória, desenvolveu uma visão de liderança que une estratégia, inteligência emocional e relações humanas para formar equipes mais autônomas e conscientes.\n\nCriadora do Método VOP (Você, Organização e Pessoas), construiu sua experiência em diferentes contextos organizacionais, incluindo uma passagem marcante como diretora de um cemitério — uma vivência que ampliou sua compreensão sobre propósito, vulnerabilidade e o valor das conexões humanas.\n\nHoje, dedica-se a apoiar líderes e organizações na construção de ambientes onde resultados e cuidado com as pessoas caminham juntos. Para Vivi, a verdadeira liderança nasce da coragem de olhar para si antes de conduzir qualquer transformação. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":733,"id":"aa8232af-b9a0-4f43-9014-22d760434db4","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:45:53+00:00","title":"Diretora de cemitério ensina sobre autoliderança | Vivi Quiessi | TEDxEloy Chaves Women","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"gDTNkpVEo0A"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"60012dd10e4458060b0a31ed52b36f25627ef957f56f31b3b61a0e911c152f26","description":"Philosopher Meghan Sullivan and space architect Ariel Ekblaw don't usually work at the same altitude ... but put them in a room together, and they'll answer the big questions. Drawing on wisdom from Aristotle to astronauts, they discuss what humans inherently owe each other, what it takes to live a good life and what a rulebook for a better world might look like. (This conversation is part of “TED Intersections,” a series featuring thought-provoking conversations between experts navigating the ideas shaping our world.)\n\n0:00 - TED Intersections\n0:39 - What is the common good? \n2:21 - What do we owe each other?\n4:02 - Designing a better world\n5:34 - The hope for humans\n7:18 - The barriers to thriving\n10:07 - Systems that influence good\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/tedintersections\n\nhttps://youtu.be/daLpHnD4b9A\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #philosophy #space","duration_seconds":763,"id":"aaaa187a-b2b2-4211-a76b-4ec7d1bb6087","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:00:07+00:00","title":"How to Live a Good Life — on Earth and in Space | TED Intersections","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"daLpHnD4b9A"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"4f72e20fc53a2a142e950a1161e9608b1e2986111977177c3b8c2cf2cc540be1","description":"What is justice when fairness and survival collide? \nWhen a single heart is available and multiple patients need it, what decision would be fair? \nIn this talk, Kai explores the complex world of organ allocation, revealing why justice is not always about equality and why the hardest decisions demand moral reasoning rather than simple rules. 生物伦理议题探索者 This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":800,"id":"ab13ab32-9e3e-4a37-aac9-082367a4c291","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:09:51+00:00","title":"Not All Fairness is Equal: A New Way to Understand Justice | Kai Huang | TEDxNanshan Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"V8szYUazJ-g"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"7ee2247912f141d91ac7a381313166a783413262e4d9d2162a2ddd87b6ba9696","description":"In South Asia, climate change is not only reshaping coastlines and crops. It is redefining childhood. When floods, droughts, and heatwaves disrupt livelihoods, families are forced into impossible calculations. For some, marrying a daughter early becomes a way to manage risk, secure support, or enable mobility. Not simply tradition, but survival shaped by shrinking choices. Masculinities, too, are being renegotiated, as work disappears and expectations remain. Drawing on stories and research from the Climate Brides project, this talk reveals how climate shocks remake family life, gender roles, and the bargains through which child marriage is recast as survival. Ultimately, climate justice is about whose futures are protected, and whose are negotiated away in a warming world. Dr Subramanian is a Senior Research associate and BBC- AHRC New Generation Thinker 2025 who works in the School of Global Development at the University of East Anglia. Her work focuses on climate change, gender and labour migration. She is the creator of the Climate Brides podcast. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1109,"id":"abb8451a-5f79-4ed0-8464-99e2f143ab18","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:51:19+00:00","title":"Climate Brides | Dr Reetika Revathy Subramanian | TEDxUniversityofStAndrews","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"7R7nAD0QRuM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9e63d4e7e53a223f24bd8b56a7a5c68f3d9b77ff1b7947cf2a2fcd864c7946a4","description":"In this heartfelt talk, Flora Claudia Shamiran reflects on her journey of learning to embrace free will, choice, and opportunity. From being afraid of making her own decisions to understanding the power of taking ownership of them, she explores how the choices we make—and the choices we allow others to make for us—can shape the course of our lives. Through personal experiences, she encourages us to step beyond fear, recognise the opportunities around us, and confidently take charge of our own path.\n\nAt its heart, the talk is a reminder that sometimes the hidden force shaping our lives is simply the courage to choose. Mrs. Flora Claudia Shamiran is an entrepreneur, philanthropist, and community leader. She is the First President of the Indo-UAE Economic Chamber of Commerce – National Women's Wing and Managing Director of Drizzle Hospitality Group and Gold and Light Company. She has been recognized among The Times of India's Top 10 Influential Women Entrepreneurs and received the Best Young Entrepreneur (Mayan Awards), Inspiring Entrepreneur (Mayan Vista Awards), Best Woman Entrepreneur (Women's League Association), and Golden Crown Award – Queen of Entrepreneurship & Role Model. She serves as Assistant Governor (2026–27) of Rotary District 3233 and President of the Rotary Club of Chennai Spotlight, leading 100+ Rotary projects, Global Grants, and CSR initiatives. A motivational speaker, singer, and stage performer, she has been featured by NDTV, Makkal TV, and leading print media, inspiring future entrepreneurs and leaders. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":589,"id":"ac04c817-6d89-4400-ab1c-9ccf7a69ad79","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:14:52+00:00","title":"Choosing’s Easy, Living With It Is Hard | Flora Claudia Shamiran | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Dp0fpPfQ4Ec"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e03f0df892b246367fdf01eff9216f9aca4198e222d7aef2a4164478f46aafd4","description":"If you want to live in a world where people read, novelist Ann Patchett has news for you: it's your job to help create that reality. Tracing her path from a chance airport encounter through a career writing iconic novels and opening a beloved independent bookstore, she makes the case that reading isn't a private pleasure but a civic act that builds empathy, sustains a \"long-format brain\" and pulls people out of isolation. Ready to lose yourself in a book?","duration_seconds":119,"id":"ae1eaddf-0016-4b4d-8064-6c337ac8f556","published_at":"2026-07-26T17:00:11+00:00","title":"If you want more people to read, read books yourself #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"BsqFxyU37HY"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9c354b13d185af66f296925782af45ef202ddfb705bc50e5039d0243b0f03cbc","description":"國岡　啓子　：　「価値あるもの」が「評価されるもの」になるまでの物語\n\n25年以上、在野の研究者として、子育てや家事をしながら、途方もない量のデータベースを作成してきた彼女。\nがんばって作っても、このデータベースは世に出ることはないかもしれない。\nいっそやめてしまおうと諦めそうになったこともあった。\nそんな彼女が辿り着いた、「価値あるもの」と「評価されるもの」との間にあるものとは。\n\nKUNIOKA Keiko　：　The Journey from What Has Value to What Is Valued\n\nFor more than 25 years, she has worked as an independent researcher,\nbuilding an enormous database while raising children and managing household responsibilities.\nNo matter how hard she worked on it, there were times when she feared that this database might never see the light of day.\nAt times, she came close to giving up altogether.\nWhat she eventually discovered was the crucial element that lies between something of value and something that is truly valued.\n\n\n After graduating from university, she studied bureaucratic personnel affairs in the Meiji era as a researcher out of power for a long time. The results of her  research are now being distributed by her university as a blue book DB and Kainin Nichiroku DB. Kainin Nichiroku  is the Appointment Records issued  within Japan's central bureaucracy during the early Meiji period. The number of people listed in both DBs exceeds 40,000, making it the largest bureaucratic data of the Meiji era at present, and the first open data source. Recently, she has been making efforts to disseminate the existence of Kainin Nichiroku, believing that it can make a significant contribution to the research community due to its elaborate contents. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":902,"id":"af00b559-2459-4232-8496-4f376f60ec63","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:47:38+00:00","title":"「価値あるもの」が「評価されるもの」になるまでの物語 | Keiko KUNIOKA | TEDxKumamoto","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"EQHihSi2H8U"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"1e3edf6b83eaf535a2fe088ef383f5f04e34a1e3ff1c7b75912712d6b154ce70","description":"Excessive and chronic rewards trigger dopamine floods and replace natural rewards. This ''high jacking\" of the reward circuit leads to interesting changes in motivation that will change how individuals prioritise everyday tasks, value relationships and their expectations of their immediate environments. What is the difference between a negative and a positive addiction? When does instant gratification and memory impairments become problematic? This TED x talk will explore some of these issues and help normalise the notion that addicted individuals were morally weak and/or genetically  predisposed to become dependent on psychoactive substances. Professor Baldacchino holds a variety of esteemed positions including Chair of the World Psychiatric Association (WPA) Addiction Faculty, Scientific Advisor for the European Union Drug Agency (EUDA) and World Health organisation (WHO). His main research interest lies in improving the lives of individuals with a history of substance use disorders and addictive behaviours. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1118,"id":"af691b8d-6aa6-46fe-a6d8-b515887b5635","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:51:56+00:00","title":"Brain changes in addiction | Professor Alexander Baldacchino | TEDxUniversityofStAndrews","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"jXf1_xzwKvw"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"f784ffe908f9df6897f9166c0629e86c5e5e10dd506b0d065c2ae918566a2de2","description":"When James G. Robinson's son Nadav was born with a rare heart defect, he feared the worst. What he didn't anticipate were the moments of unexpected wonder. In this profound talk, Robinson traces five years of impossible medical odds, a genetic discovery so strange it felt like fate ... and how grateful he is to have experienced it all. His story is a vivid reminder of what it means to be human — an inexplicable gift, however fleeting. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/jamesgrobinson\n\nhttps://youtu.be/o2PLCTzEo_E\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Family","duration_seconds":837,"id":"afe6c9a5-d55e-48dc-9743-fcfa997c1706","published_at":"2026-07-13T15:00:14+00:00","title":"The Unexpected Wonders of My Son’s Short Life | James G. Robinson | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"o2PLCTzEo_E"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d9d0b92d1d47baad1ebcd8c148754b0fe64472de497ae2a472bf65c3bcce8f00","description":"Social entrepreneur Van Jones believes a new human civilization is being born in real time — and that our technology is racing ahead of our wisdom. In this urgent and hopeful talk, he reframes the AI debate, showing why the real danger isn't the technology itself but rather the communities it leaves behind. His solution? A new deal between big tech and humanity, built on a different kind of \"AI\" that we desperately need. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 17, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/vanjones26\n\nhttps://youtu.be/_JlQOnnEwxc\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #AI","duration_seconds":960,"id":"b048c6dc-266b-4f96-81bc-80de899f8486","published_at":"2026-07-20T15:00:14+00:00","title":"The Kind of AI We Actually Need | Van Jones | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"_JlQOnnEwxc"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e871bcb4087d0e8db0b63dc90e6feceff34959fe101e63bf397928d2b228ba93","description":"Ci sono percorsi che guardano oltre i confini… letteralmente.  Annamaria Nassisi, laureata in Fisica e Geofisica presso l'Università La Sapienza di Roma, vanta oltre 35 anni di esperienza nel settore spaziale. Oggi è Manager Space Economy, Observation & Navigation di Thales Alenia Space, dove opera tra innovazione, strategia e cooperazione internazionale. Insignita della Stella al Merito del Lavoro nel 2022, è anche una figura di riferimento nella promozione della presenza femminile nelle discipline STEM. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1064,"id":"b0e13e5c-2fe8-4885-8a96-04e324515755","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:34:28+00:00","title":"Da spettatrici a protagoniste: il valore di farsi spazio | Annamaria Nassisi | TEDxLUISS","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"xKhD3EYXIgg"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5c87a27446dd40c36d034d33b33a8faf704a541f9047e21ec986e98024bf0ce9","description":"How can one little girl who cannot speak change the way we think about healthcare, education, government, leadership—and even artificial intelligence?\n\nAfter becoming the primary carer for her daughter with profound disabilities, Christine Stow discovered that the greatest barriers to progress are often not a lack of resources, but the assumptions hidden inside the systems we create. Through deeply personal stories and powerful real-world examples, she explores why lasting change begins by questioning the way we think—not simply building bigger systems. In an era where AI will increasingly shape our institutions, this talk is a timely reminder that technology can only amplify the quality of the thinking behind it. Christine Stow is a TEDx speaker and strategic thinker who helps people and organisations change the way they see so they can change what's possible.\n\nDrawing on her experience as a corporate executive, elected local government leader, and primary carer for her daughter with profound disabilities, Christine brings a unique perspective to leadership, systems thinking, and decision-making.\n\nHer TEDx talk challenges audiences to rethink the assumptions behind the systems we create and explores why the biggest breakthroughs don't happen when we work harder—they happen when we change our perspective.\n\nThrough compelling storytelling and practical insight, Christine inspires leaders, organisations, and communities to ask better questions, embrace new perspectives, and unlock possibilities they never thought existed. \n\nwww.christinestow.com.au\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/in/christinestow\nFacebook: https://www.facebook.com/christine.stow.1\nYouTube: https://www.youtube.com/@empoweredwomeninbusiness23 This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":508,"id":"b16cde89-d742-4f47-b02d-dbfecedc2e20","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:43:51+00:00","title":"What caring for my daughter taught me about broken systems | Christine Stow | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"6B6tWs6K1pw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9c1be1f1165d3b3cea7bf3c1871a57419c915404dc451abb13ca641c028daa4d","description":"This talk explores how the principles of ancient wisdom can be useful to very modern problems. Humans, by default, still start as a blank canvas and must relearn the same lessons about control, identity, ego, relationships, and resilience through burnout, rejection, ghosting, imposter syndrome, and conflict, even though thinkers like the Stoics and Aristotle articulated practical frameworks for these challenges thousands of years ago. The problem is not that the wisdom is outdated, but that the language and framing no longer fit how we live and work.\n\nWhat makes this talk new is its approach: it reframes ancient philosophical principles as a practical operating system for everyday modern problems, using humour, contemporary examples, and cultural touchpoints to make them accessible without dumbing them down. Rather than treating philosophy as abstract or academic, the talk presents it as a set of usable mental tools for navigating uncertainty, pressure, and human behaviour in the 21st century. \n\nThe core idea is that we don’t need new answers. We need better translations. Nick Murray is Director of TecTonic MVMT and an innovation programme designer who has spent over a decade building entrepreneurial and leadership programmes across the UK, Europe, the Middle East, and beyond - working with organisations including Techstars, Barclays Eagle Labs, and Startup Grind Scotland. Alongside that work, Nick has spent years asking why ancient thinkers like the Stoics and Aristotle already had practical answers to burnout, rejection, imposter syndrome, and conflict - and why we keep rediscovering them the hard way. His talk, Ancient Wisdom for Modern Problems, argues that humans still arrive as Version 0.1, forced to relearn the same lessons every generation - not because the wisdom is outdated, but because the translation is broken. Nick makes the case that we don't need new answers. We need better ones. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":609,"id":"b25793b0-b954-4120-845d-177ca03d960a","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:37:32+00:00","title":"Ancient wisdom for modern problems | Nick Murray | TEDxLeith","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"JlrloI3Msfw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"f5a50839129d69a6a2387e457db5e94012a964da39b65c3155b486eb69b65af2","description":"Our identity was never meant to be defined by the polished versions of ourselves we choose to show online...\nTEDxRGSV Youth - 404: The Forgotten Archive  - 2025 High school student from RGSV Year 13 student This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":652,"id":"b32b8fbb-0df3-41ac-91d8-6a8cea4b267b","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:45:54+00:00","title":"Rediscovering The Human Behind The Screen | Hong Anh Dang | TEDxRGSV Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"lqt5iDzxa9s"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"a79b4c81206350d9c3b6c150a9a1c63c5003ccf009abdfff299e5d4a90c3c262","description":"Have you ever built a life that looked absolutely perfect from the outside, only to realize it wasn’t actually yours?\n\nIn this moving and witty talk, business owner and US Marine veteran Bobbie Maloy exposes the \"Self-Betrayal Loop,\" a psychological trap where we constantly say yes to things that aren't us, effectively auditioning for everyone else's version of who we should be. Sharing her raw, personal journey through divorce, sudden identity shifts, and \"identity moratorium,\" Bobbie challenges the modern self-help industry’s obsession with finding ourselves through addition. Instead, she reveals a simple, unconventional 10-minute exercise to help you draw a final line in the sand, proving that we don’t discover who we are by figuring out who we are supposed to be, but by courageously confronting who we are not. Bobbie Maloy is an author, business owner, U.S. Marine Corps veteran, and creator of The Aligned Self Method. After spending years navigating drastic identity shifts—from military service to entrepreneurship, and enduring an unexpected divorce—Bobbie realized she had unconsciously built a life based entirely on other people's choices. This profound realization led her to study the psychological traps of identity moratorium and self-betrayal. Today, she works as a transformational coach, helping individuals build the unshakeable confidence needed to break free from chronic people-pleasing, reclaim their identity, and intentionally sculpt an authentic life. Bobbie is dedicated to empowering people to stop living for everyone else's expectations and instead draw a definitive line in the sand for their own lives.\n\nWebsite: www.alignedselfmethod.com\nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/alignedselfmethod\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@alignedselfmethod\nPublished Work: Author of \"Healing Your Inner Child\" available on Amazon, Audible, and major book retailers This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":647,"id":"b38f1aae-b593-480c-9c38-6e11a0db4bc6","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:39:31+00:00","title":"How the Self-Betrayal Loop Traps You | Bobbie Maloy | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"D1tn35wuhds"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e35487c418801d04deaa834a419e58fe1fa0deedb025b5a0fa561bfb2ab6b5b2","description":"How do you find common ground when people disagree? Opportunity builder Anuj Gupta and Visit Philadelphia CMO Neil Frauenglass discuss why democracy needs debate and why we all have more political power than we think. They explore whether society is divided or just disconnected, how local action makes a difference and what it takes to rebuild trust when we can't even agree on truth itself. (This conversation is part of “Idea Knock Down,” a series where two people with different perspectives take on big questions — one block at a time.) (Recorded on June 12, 2026) Visit: https://www.visitphilly.com/ted\n\n0:00 - Idea Knock Down\n0:57 - Is debate overrated?\n1:15 - Do people want to connect?\n2:04 - Are people divided or disconnected?\n2:51 - Can young people fix what's broken?\n3:34 - What brings people together\n4:20 - When is democracy working?\n5:31 - Where do people still have power?\n6:59 - Should voting be mandatory? \n7:13 - Is compromise a sign of weakness?\n7:37 - Do people really not care?\n8:35 - How to invite people to engage\n9:20 - Something worth rebuilding now\n9:50 - Is social media good for democracy?\n10:34 - Mystery challenge\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nhttps://www.ted.com/series/idea_knock_down\n\nhttps://youtu.be/S7tJbGWDis0\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Democracy","duration_seconds":727,"id":"b39c9710-c510-48d9-b259-03f9a041191c","published_at":"2026-07-21T15:00:36+00:00","title":"You Have More Power Than You Realize | Idea Knock Down | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"S7tJbGWDis0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"3288df7dccb864f36c029d3e9b9b5ac86d4426feb6bf67aa14d0c17e117470ba","description":"Our thoughts have the power to shape the way we experience life, influence our actions, and create the reality we move towards. Through a story of a shopkeeper, a king, and an unexpected act of kindness, this talk explores how our thoughts can become our karma and how positive thinking can transform difficult circumstances. It also looks at the importance of water, exploring ideas of vibration, energy, and the relationship between what we think, what we consume, and how we live. The talk invites us to become more conscious of our thoughts and recognise the power they hold in shaping our present and future.\n K. Jayaganesh is an officer of the Indian Revenue Service (IRS), 2008 batch, currently serving as the Commissioner of Income Tax (Departmental Representative) at the Income Tax Appellate Tribunal, Chennai. With a career spanning the private sector, the Income Tax Department, and the Enforcement Directorate, he brings extensive experience in taxation, investigations, intelligence, corporate assessments, infrastructure, exemptions, and regulatory enforcement.\nAn engineer, legal scholar, and management professional by qualification, Jayaganesh holds a Diploma and Bachelor's degree in Mechanical Engineering, a Bachelor of Academic Laws (BAL), and a Master's in Management Studies (MMS). His multidisciplinary background enables him to approach complex challenges with analytical thinking and practical insight. Through his professional journey and public engagements, he advocates the power of positive thinking, values-driven leadership to inspire meaningful personal and professional growth. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":566,"id":"b53f7d50-a45d-4b1b-bc46-914d7d193931","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:19:01+00:00","title":"The Power of Our Thoughts | Jayaganesh K | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"rR_IyiNmS5g"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"7b60c269fed89506e33bfdac2636b2cf186666016ca33ab08178509c5aa31661","description":"Author Gregg Ward spent decades grappling with guilt from a personal tragedy before recognizing a different path forward — where purpose replaced self-punishment. What he learned about transforming regret into positive change might make you rethink your own mistakes. (Recorded at TEDxSanDiego on February 20, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/greggward\n\nhttps://youtu.be/cGZwLEoAx0U\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #PersonalGrowth","duration_seconds":668,"id":"b6153108-12c9-49a1-bcec-6157e01d00d1","published_at":"2026-08-04T15:00:37+00:00","title":"How to Live with Your Biggest Regret | Gregg Ward | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"cGZwLEoAx0U"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2b856bdb8314c79813d9b6a9733a6b6e7621a88025203f69dffa38edce734dca","description":"Maryana com Y acredita que o humor é uma das mais poderosas ferramentas para fortalecer relações, ampliar a inteligência emocional e tornar a vida mais leve, sem perder a profundidade. Sua trajetória reúne experiências em diferentes países, estudos em felicidade, inteligência emocional e neurociência. Para Maryana, rir não é escapar da realidade. É encontrar uma forma mais inteligente e humana de atravessá-la. Maryana com Y acredita que o humor é uma das mais poderosas ferramentas para fortalecer relações, ampliar a inteligência emocional e tornar a vida mais leve — sem perder a profundidade.\n\nCriadora da Inteligência HUMORcional®, desenvolveu uma abordagem que une neurociência, comportamento humano e bem-estar para ajudar pessoas e organizações a enfrentarem desafios com mais consciência, conexão e criatividade. Sua trajetória reúne experiências em diferentes países, estudos em felicidade, inteligência emocional e neurociência, além de uma passagem marcante pela Disney, onde aprofundou seu olhar sobre o impacto das emoções na experiência humana.\n\nPara Maryana, rir não é escapar da realidade. É encontrar uma forma mais inteligente e humana de atravessá-la. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":732,"id":"b64bb5e0-c594-4d59-80a1-bb68379c4b77","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:48:57+00:00","title":"Legal sempre, disponível às vezes | Maryana com Y | TEDxEloy Chaves Women","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"2cejNCPD2Nw"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"8fbeed270b9d8070e0b7489afbfb45258b29f96794ab97a151cbdb1c6efaa52a","description":"Most of the technology powering life in Europe today — chatbots, the cloud, digital infrastructure — is owned by companies in the United States. Writer and curator Bruno Giussani makes the case that Europe's reliance on American technology has crossed the line from convenient to risky. His answer isn't to go it alone, but to build something the continent currently lacks: a real alternative, rooted in a different model. \"Technology that does not spy on you — that should be Europe's digital difference,” he says. (Recorded at TEDxPorto on March 28, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/brunogiussani\n\nhttps://youtu.be/IHqPcDaMtFg\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Technology #Democracy","duration_seconds":1142,"id":"b703357f-5c60-4b7d-95bc-8736c38089a0","published_at":"2026-08-23T15:00:09+00:00","title":"Why Big Tech Is Incompatible with Democracy | Bruno Giussani | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"IHqPcDaMtFg"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"136cb268803c6546da38a935060b9b1064475a23ba2f742be89701ff18aced96","description":"Take a graffiti tour through ancient Pompeii with Roman archaeologist Jacqueline DiBiasie-Sammons and discover what 2,000-year-old scribblings from antiquity can teach us about life in modern times. A fascinating reminder of what we leave behind for future generations.","duration_seconds":45,"id":"b721f0c6-e9d0-417a-88ed-08420ccbde31","published_at":"2026-08-18T18:00:11+00:00","title":"Group chats used to be written on the walls (literally). #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"yVJfjS1qtUQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"20d8413a35a5fc9509fb8eda23785719fa24260056d7e152746cb1caf95e9109","description":"For years, we've lived in an attention economy where followers, likes, and virality determined who was seen, trusted, and rewarded. But artificial intelligence is quietly changing the rules.\n\nIn this thought-provoking talk, physician-turned-entrepreneur Dr. Christopher H. Loo, MD-PhD argues that we're entering an era where authority—not attention—will become the most valuable currency. As AI-powered search and answer engines increasingly determine what information people discover and trust, long-term credibility, evidence, and a body of work matter more than ever.\n\nDrawing from his own journey of leaving medicine during the 2008 financial crisis, rebuilding his career through media and technology, and hosting more than 1,500 podcast conversations, Dr. Loo reveals why the future belongs to those who build trust instead of chasing trends.\n\nIf AI is rewriting the rules of influence, trust, and opportunity, what should we be building instead? Dr. Christopher H. Loo, MD-PhD, is a physician, entrepreneur, investor, and speaker whose work explores how artificial intelligence is reshaping authority, trust, and financial opportunity.\n\nAfter leaving medicine during the 2008 financial crisis, he achieved financial independence through strategic investing before reinventing himself again by building a top 5% globally ranked podcast into a six-figure media brand. Today, through more than 1,500 interviews with entrepreneurs, executives, and innovators, he examines what it takes to remain relevant in a world where AI is transforming how we discover expertise and build influence.\n\nA graduate of Rice University and Baylor College of Medicine, Dr. Loo believes the future belongs not to those who chase attention, but to those who build enduring authority. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":648,"id":"b8735ffa-c6a2-4cee-abaa-9bae76d102b2","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:47:35+00:00","title":"Why AI Will End the Attention Economy | Christopher Loo | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ieocPQJDxPM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"14f589eeaa82b195cff53e48f746a52e6a64b17287a40a637b4f01601c3b9e17","description":"Byron Adams examines how people understand and negotiate who they are across cultures, particularly in contexts of inclusion and marginalization, with the aim of translating research into more inclusive organizations and societies. Byron G. Adams is an Assistant Professor in Psychology at the University of Amsterdam, the Netherlands, and a Senior Research Associate in the Department of Industrial Psychology and People Management at the University of Johannesburg, South Africa. He is an identity psychologist whose work examines how people understand and negotiate who they are across cultures, particularly in contexts of inclusion and marginalization, with the aim of translating research into more inclusive organizations and societies. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":843,"id":"b89171ad-5bfa-4bb2-8a9f-fa264bd27994","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:03:29+00:00","title":"Who gets to define you? Identity in a world that classifies | Byron Adams | TEDxAU College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"0hoSGwGywQ0"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"fec761414d166ac484834543bad09254fd305445748ebc28bcca4dd7e2a73893","description":"Can you stand up for what you believe in without participating in politics? Anti-career coach Danielle Roberts and political rule breaker Katie Paris discuss how to rethink power, work and civic life on your own terms. They explore how rest, community and meaningful participation can help you stay engaged in the issues shaping your life. (This conversation is part of “Idea Knock Down,” a series where two people with different perspectives take on big questions — one block at a time.)","duration_seconds":45,"id":"b977dd90-640d-457e-8e6c-af210c2f6022","published_at":"2026-08-23T19:00:15+00:00","title":"Just one conversation can make a difference #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"fTehWwo-eJU"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"97d5fee705277742162cafb28c578d25283bcb12f19f1831c4634ae6e5ff4164","description":"Netflix cofounder and Anthropic board member Reed Hastings joins TED's Sal Khan to give a look inside the race to build AI. Hear his take on how the technology could accelerate education — including the possibility of an AI tutor for every student — and reshape the economy faster than anyone expects. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/reedhastings26\n\nhttps://youtu.be/5e37ZT3SQbk\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #AI #Netflix #Anthropic","duration_seconds":985,"id":"b9d44b2e-bbd7-4f08-98d6-5004d3b2fd14","published_at":"2026-07-28T15:00:06+00:00","title":"Why AI Is an Even Bigger Deal Than You Think | Reed Hastings | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"5e37ZT3SQbk"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"325ff6399bc8a635c0b383ed89c6d54513d2af377ec50ba737624f7cdab96473","description":"What if the most important map you’ll ever explore isn’t the world—but yourself?\n\nIn this thought-provoking talk, Dr. Gail Awan introduces J.O.G.I.™ (Journey • Origin • Geography • Identity), a framework that explores how history, ancestry, geography, and personal experience shape the stories we tell about ourselves and others. Drawing from science, history, culture, and storytelling, she challenges audiences to see identity as something richer than labels or DNA alone.\n\nThrough an unexpected conversation with Wilma Flintstone, Dr. Awan invites us to ask a timeless question: What kind of ancestor will you become? The result is a fresh perspective on empathy, belonging, and the legacy each of us has the power to create. Dr. Gail Awan is a nonprofit executive, cultural storyteller, author, and two-time TEDx speaker with more than 25 years of experience leading community-based initiatives. She is the creator of J.O.G.I.™ (Journey • Origin • Geography • Identity), a storytelling framework that invites people to explore how history, ancestry, geography, and identity shape who they are—and to consider what kind of ancestors they will become. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":628,"id":"ba175b1c-d6f7-4cd3-b1a9-9f98452e1699","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:46:39+00:00","title":"Coffee with Wilma Flintstone | Gail Awan PhD | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"BIso0wwEdbQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"f7c9a6082a2b11b666fa672171415b120b18149997137b47709a0110448008f6","description":"Through the expressive language of mudras and movement, the speaker brings the devotion of Meera Bai and her love for Krishna to life. Her performance transcends the boundaries of reality, immersing us in a world where every gesture becomes an emotion, and every movement tells a story. Divya Warier is a renowned Mohiniyattam soloist trained under Dr. Neena Prasad and a Doordarshan-graded artiste in Mohiniyattam and Bharatanatyam. She has performed at global stages like Khajuraho, Soorya Festival, Paris, and NMACC.\n\nFounder of Avyukt Arts and Trustee of the Warrier Foundation, she supports 3,000+ children through arts and education. From classical dance to cinema—including Ghoomar (Padmavat)—she blends tradition with contemporary expression, with 1,000+ performances worldwide. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1537,"id":"bc75de43-fcab-4fd0-a36f-cd0871f71bde","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:09:12+00:00","title":"When Dance Becomes Devotion | Divya Warier | TEDxSPCE","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"njkNFwEaiXA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"7510248316191793bdb6631c1aefc89f76dab13ef1e943be646dcb40592cef2d","description":"Democracy should not depend on courage; it should be designed by default.\n\nMost institutions rely on opt-in participation, which unintentionally rewards confidence and personality. The result is that the loudest voices dominate, even when everyone believes participation should be equal.\n\nWhat makes this idea new is the shift from seeing democracy as a moral or cultural issue to seeing it as a structural one. I introduce the concept of Democracy by Default, replacing opt-in voice with built-in voice. Rather than asking people to be brave enough to speak, participation is designed into the process so everyone contributes. This reframes democratic inequality as a design problem, not a motivation problem. Greg Sanderson is the founder of Smart School Councils, a charity that has spent over a decade redesigning how participation works in schools - supporting hundreds of thousands of young people across the UK to make student-led decision making part of everyday school life. A former Leith resident, Greg's thinking about participation and community was shaped in part by the neighbourhood itself. His talk introduces the concept of Democracy by Default - arguing that democratic inequality isn't a motivation problem, it's a design problem. When institutions rely on opt-in participation, the loudest voices win by default. Greg makes the case that healthier democracies begin not at the ballot box, but in how we structure participation long before anyone ever votes. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":980,"id":"bd7b21cd-1e98-4bb7-92cc-05bdc1ffa3fb","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:39:55+00:00","title":"Democracy by Default | Greg Sanderson | TEDxLeith","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"i7YfSSRMBHM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"12ae898fcb025eedc08c4a12e828abfea563c1f316e48d9e8ad5aa77c7dda923","description":"In un mondo in cui l'intelligenza artificiale è sempre più capace di imitare il nostro lavoro, cosa significa davvero essere presenti?\nJacopo Romei esplora il confine tra accesso e presenza, mostrando come la vera differenza non stia nella qualità tecnica di ciò che produciamo, ma nella responsabilità, nell'intenzione e nelle relazioni che siamo capaci di costruire.\nPerché la presenza autentica non può essere automatizzata: resta il tratto più umano e il valore più difficile da replicare. Ha scritto il primo codice nel 1984 su un Commodore 64 e ha iniziato a programmare per lavoro nel 1996. Entro il 2011, aveva già fondato tre aziende e ne aveva chiuso due. Ha introdotto i metodi agili in eBay, Siemens, FAO e molte altre corporation. Ha sempre creduto che parlare in pubblico fosse un'attività cruciale per diffondere il suo messaggio, validarlo e promuoverlo. \nOggi Jacopo aiuta aziende, startup e professionisti a costruire nuove imprese, minimizzando il loro rischio e massimizzando il loro ritorno sull'investimento, l'efficacia e il divertimento. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":916,"id":"bdb7bf7c-82f1-4ef7-8eed-dffe3fd048b9","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:40:52+00:00","title":"Il confine tra accesso e presenza | Jacopo Romei | TEDxMessina","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"t6JsyC7lSb8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5707f05c78af7890f68691f26730a8993b38acdf938e7106bedd74eefe9da942","description":"In \"No Such Thing as 'Fast',\" Alexus Grace (Rhone) examines how flawed social science research became cultural shorthand that mischaracterized Black girls as “fast” and families as “broken.” Tracing these labels from academic studies to everyday language, she reveals how data without context can harden into identity—shaping not only perception, but lived experience.\n\nBlending personal narrative, cultural analysis, and storytelling practice, Alexus introduces “re-storying” as a corrective approach—one that restores complexity, dignity, and agency to lives flattened by oversimplified conclusions. She challenges the assumption that research is neutral, reframing it instead as a powerful narrative force that must be handled with care.\n\nUltimately, this talk expands beyond one community to ask a universal question: What happens when people are defined before they are fully formed? In an era where more people than ever have the tools to shape stories, Alexus invites us to take responsibility for how we name, frame, and carry one another’s lives—recognizing that there is no such thing as “fast,” only human beings in the ongoing process of becoming. Alexus Grace (Rhone) is a writer, producer, and artistic theologian devoted to exploring the power of truth when ‘dressed’ in story.\n\nFounder of Truth Meet Story, Alexus tells stories about the sweetness of Black girls and the compassion of Black women on diverse platforms - on page, stage, screen, web, radio, and podcasts.\n\nShe entered the creative ecosystem publishing a YA novel series targeting reluctant readers. Premature Pleasures, Secret Shame, Backseats and Bleachers: A High School Love Story, and Cover The Coo are page-turning “mirrors” and love notes to her readers.\n\nOver the past 25 years, she expanded her storytelling repertoire to include stage productions “Call & Response,” “Ancient Of Ways: For Colored Girls Who’ve Considered Ifa When Jesus Came Up Short,” “Pitching My Tent Too Close To Sodom: Justice For Rosa Lee Ingram”; digital storytelling series’ “#TheBounceBackRDU,” “#Proverbs32,” and award-winning documentary “Religion, Philosophy & Mental Health in Black Americans.”\n\nDeeply impacted by the creative journey of Alice Childress, Alexus writes, directs, and produces original content for this context. The burden of this moment in history to counter the attempts to erase Black women’s contributions and Black girls’ value undergirds the framing and tone of her narratives.\n\nShe holds a Master of Arts in Theology from Fuller Theological Seminary in Pasadena, California, and a Bachelor of Journalism from the University of Texas at Austin. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":597,"id":"be4a560f-4048-4dde-b0c0-0ff15f5e5f59","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:48:00+00:00","title":"No Such Thing as 'Fast' | Alexus Grace Rhone | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"pBf_aErlDhM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5603e6b51519c34c531a1b20c4726851a5757b41321846f4faeb8769672856da","description":"Have you ever wanted to post online but felt like you'd be judged? Joe Gannon, founder of a social media agency, explains why the people with the most valuable experience are often the ones who stay silent and what it's costing them.\n\nIn this TEDx talk, Joe introduces the concept of \"visibility debt\", the widening gap between who you are and what the world sees when fear keeps you from sharing your ideas. Drawing on Jakob Nielsen's 90-9-1 rule (90% of people never post, 9% post occasionally, and just 1% create most of the content we consume), Joe explains why public speaking is our #1 human fear, why your biggest insecurity might be your greatest strength, and how preparation, not luck, is what turns a high-stakes moment into a life-changing opportunity. Joe Gannon is a personal brand strategist, founder of Amplify and creator of Personal Brand Mastery. He has spent over seven years helping entrepreneurs, founders and thought leaders turn their ideas into influence, with Amplify supporting global names including Ali Abdaal, Chris Williamson, Sahil Bloom, Jordan Peterson and Andreessen Horowitz. Drawing on experience in content strategy, online teaching and brand positioning, Joe helps people build personal brands that create trust, visibility and opportunity without chasing algorithms or burning out. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":570,"id":"bebacdf2-861b-4ebc-b121-fc139621e6da","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:48:18+00:00","title":"The Hidden Cost Of Not Posting Online | Joe Gannon | TEDxBlack Mountain","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ojudKJMncDo"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9c5b8acfd81e798c5379f8938cd1f2b4e33802b364670ccc9f37fb92a044504e","description":"In speaker Sheba Saeed's talk, she explores the complexity of begging in Lahore, Pakistan, delving into the typology, demographic, reasons, reality and fieldwork insights of the topic. Her talk exemplifies to the audience the importance of nuance in widely controversial topics, and to solidify the idea that change begins with discussion.  Dr. Sheba Saeed is an interdisciplinary academic whose journey spans history, law, journalism, and film. Beginning with a degree in East Mediterranean history, she later qualified as a solicitor in crime and children’s law before pursuing an MPhil in the History of Film and Television at the University of Birmingham, U.K. Her PhD research culminated in the book Begging, Street Politics and Power: Religious and Secular Laws (Routledge, 2023), exploring begging in India and Pakistan. Her debut documentary Beggars of Lahore (2010) was screened internationally and accompanied by UK exhibitions.\n\nDr. Saeed is currently completing a book about trans women in the context of Pakistan. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":774,"id":"bed04fc9-2921-4fb1-bd48-68cc9a5b7132","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:45:37+00:00","title":"Begging in Lahore: Seeking an Anatomy of Change | Sheba Saeed | TEDxISPH Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"RvvXrAkvtxk"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"f02b63e34a4c28288ff6362c8c16a32e9164ccd8035115198ae5dfbac8e97066","description":"Science communicator Saloni Dattani traces the quiet revolution of medical innovation that has slashed death rates from conditions like heart disease and malaria — and reveals the broken incentives that have delayed other lifesaving treatments for decades. \"Diseases are not a fact of life,\" she says. \"They're problems that we can solve.\" (Recorded at TED2026 on April 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/salonidattani\n\nhttps://youtu.be/6eYEi9tN6Og\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #PublicHealth","duration_seconds":676,"id":"bf5cc1de-72f5-4a58-9a37-08c7a69bac16","published_at":"2026-08-31T15:00:36+00:00","title":"We’ve Solved Many Medical Mysteries. Where Are the Cures? | Saloni Dattani | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"6eYEi9tN6Og"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"a83c1154393e56c1e712949513f97fbc37c086a54a8c6fab7963a9c214176cfe","description":"What if your passion could become more than a hobby -- what if it could become a career?\n\nTom Huynh shares how a winding journey from the nail industry to professional fishing taught him an unexpected lesson: great educators aren't defined by a classroom or a degree, but by their passion, expertise, and willingness to share what they know. His story challenges the idea that educators must stay at the bottom of the pay scale and offers a new way to think about turning knowledge into opportunity. I have been in the nail salon industry for over 20 years, and more recently took the professional walleye fishing world by storm by being one of the first to use the newest technology to win multiple pro events and championships.  I have taken what I have learned and developed the first ever online fishing curriculum to teach everything I am learning.  In one year, I have enrollees in more than 40 states and 20 countries. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":714,"id":"c0c93c38-7797-4e60-bc01-af399ce3f450","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:58:29+00:00","title":"Fingernails to Fishing | Tom Huynh | TEDxFargo","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"hbqPkXQwjJ4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"d654afd8cc8b2f9722603be260e6f5ce2da3799c3073063d21413e7d5c986897","description":"This talk explores the relationship between hope, perseverance, and human resilience through the famous rat experiment conducted by Curt Richter. It examines how expectations can influence our response to difficult situations and emphasizes that hope does not mean ignoring challenges, but recognizing that circumstances can change and continuing to move forward. I am a curious and determined student who is passionate about learning, growth, and pushing my own limits. I was born on September 28, 2008, and I have always been interested in understanding both the world and myself through challenges and new experiences.\n\nI’m passionate about sports, especially basketball and endurance-based activities. Through sports, I have learned the importance of discipline, resilience, and mental strength. For me, physical activity is not only about performance, but also about the constant dialogue between the mind and the body, and how we choose to respond when we are pushed to our limits. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":378,"id":"c3b0b880-0762-4881-b413-363264a2369a","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:40:02+00:00","title":"The Second Swim | Karla Estrella Padilla Servín | TEDxPrepas La Salle Bajio Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"PXvwk1BIitw"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"cd0fc146b1536a49a87c902e12da1a269c5371a6b4e13602aec198af83d21960","description":"Humans aren't just social — we're ultrasocial, wired like bees and ants for deep connection. So what happens when smartphones take over childhood, tablets replace textbooks and AI companies infiltrate our kids’ lives? Social psychologist Jonathan Haidt lays out three principles of technoskepticism — and explains why, two years after sounding the alarm in \"The Anxious Generation,\" he's more concerned (and hopeful) than ever before.","duration_seconds":95,"id":"c42384e5-a457-4bc3-8e2c-2cb53b3cca76","published_at":"2026-07-09T20:00:36+00:00","title":"Compliment a stranger or reach out to a loved one. You may do more good than you think #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Q-pnqCPpNFw"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"9bcaca55c9a8df9d6b3d642402b77a2029d9f71222d58e3228c2fab2d623e394","description":"Is authoritarianism winning, or is democracy just failing to deliver? Communications strategist Sally Kohn lays out how democracy has evolved before and why it must evolve again, into what she calls \"Democracy 3.0\" — a version that finally shares power and opportunity equally. \"We the people demand that our politics and our economy work way better for us,\" says Kohn. (Recorded at TED Democracy Philadelphia on June 13, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/sallykohn26\n\nhttps://youtu.be/Ip77mh3Of0I\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Democracy","duration_seconds":1069,"id":"c470186e-1a0e-4eae-a843-1bf0480c4f2d","published_at":"2026-08-17T15:00:38+00:00","title":"Why Economic Inequality Is the Biggest Threat to Democracy | Sally Kohn | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Ip77mh3Of0I"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"61eb6338e317585a90637d804e4895c02156fc23dca6a0def10add5ddc560cfe","description":"Nunca tivemos tanto conhecimento, dados e tecnologia para construir cidades melhores. Mas saber não chega: a verdadeira transformação acontece quando ciência, participação e mudança de comportamento se encontram.\n\n Carlos Ribeiro é Presidente Executivo do Laboratório da Paisagem, um centro de investigação ambiental e educação. Membro da Direção Executiva da Guimarães 26 – Capital Verde Europeia e copresidente da sua Comissão Científica. É docente coordenador no Instituto Superior de Saúde e investigador na área de governança, sustentabilidade e saúde. É doutorado em Ciências Biomédicas pelo Instituto de Ciências Biomédicas, da Universidade do Porto, mestre em Genética Molecular e licenciado em Biologia Aplicada pela Universidade do Minho. É também pós-graduado em Gestão e Organização do Futebol Profissional, pela Universidade Católica Portuguesa. Ao longo do seu percurso tem sido responsável pela orientação e coorientação de teses de mestrado e doutoramento, e gestão de vários projetos nacionais e internacionais, assim como autor de artigos científicos em revistas internacionais, revistas por pares, e de várias comunicações orais em congressos nacionais e internacionais. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":805,"id":"c5e249ea-9959-4d22-983d-c217b7d90015","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:57:16+00:00","title":"Sabemos. Então porque não mudamos? | Carlos Ribeiro | TEDxPortoSalon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"_LzYrj5hdls"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"a40e5ca44b7ab3aac4f91a2d88e51938862fd1112186e305259d0674bbe0d90f","description":"As the creator of a large chunk of the Marvel Universe, Stan Lee knows a thing or two about conjuring new superheroes from thin air. In this instructional and charmingly idiosyncratic talk, Lee shares a few lessons on how to write your own superpowered characters and deploy them in stories that connect with the largest possible audience.","duration_seconds":45,"id":"c771d193-36d9-4683-829c-95f475ba138e","published_at":"2026-08-08T17:00:04+00:00","title":"Spider-Man shoots webs + flies through NYC, but it’s his human traits that make him memorable #TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"JdujjVVzMj0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2f5b10143e7ff4a71518256614d0878fa3bad5340e63e8a6b4bdfd3c1d90e291","description":"NOTE FROM TED: This talk only represents the speaker's personal views and experiences in the military. TEDx events are independently organized by volunteers. The guidelines we give TEDx organizers are described in more detail here: http://storage.ted.com/tedx/manuals/tedx_content_guidelines.pdf\n\nSur la scène de TEDx, Nicolas transcende le mythe militaire pour révéler les véritables mécanismes de la motivation profonde et de la cohésion d'équipe. Il démontre avec force comment l'adversité, lorsqu'elle est partagée avec un but commun, devient le ciment d'un engagement indéfectible – une leçon universelle de fraternité et de résilience, applicable dans nos entreprises comme dans notre quotidien. Ancien légionnaire, entrepreneur & expert en leadership\n\nQu'est-ce qui pousse des hommes venus des quatre coins du monde, ne partageant ni la même langue ni la même culture, à risquer leur vie les uns pour les autres ? Ancien membre de la prestigieuse Légion étrangère, Nicolas Brault a forgé son leadership dans les conditions les plus extrêmes. Aujourd’hui entrepreneur et mentor, il traduit les codes de cette unité d'élite pour le monde civil et entrepreneurial. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":855,"id":"cadf5088-c469-4e33-9ea6-e95a3db845ac","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:51:02+00:00","title":"L’esprit de corps : Le vrai secret du lien | Nicolas Brault | TEDxBielBienne","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"C1cqkdhHktE"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"23e5e19709988becf1cdc48bc3f402beff1a452af127f7cccfc5ba4f31bd0a2a","description":"Can a five-minute walk change how you feel all day? Exercise scientist Keith Diaz shows how your body is built for the kind of movement that modern life has quietly erased — and suggests something refreshingly doable: making time for small doses of movement sprinkled throughout the day, as a way to boost your brain and body. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/keithdiaz\n\nhttps://youtu.be/rKgtm81yi94\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Health","duration_seconds":684,"id":"cb061b4a-1253-43db-96d2-a2fc0005c023","published_at":"2026-06-29T15:00:13+00:00","title":"What Sitting All Day Does to Your Brain and Body | Keith Diaz | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"rKgtm81yi94"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"8625f144c707088c1348c188409fccb9fa6899d230ef20c5032766522ed2e3a6","description":"There's a simple way to increase your cat's happiness, says animal behaviorist Nicky Trevorrow. She explains the importance of play for our feline friends — and for basically any species (looking at you, dog lovers!) — and shares specific, actionable strategies to boost your cat's mental and physical health.","duration_seconds":90,"id":"cb0a76fe-0a3e-45aa-826b-5dac1010b811","published_at":"2026-08-09T19:00:34+00:00","title":"Would your cat would give you a paw-sitive review?#TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"botYt9uElQs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"7198a6b8fed4b01e93c2eede8c6244116ef8ea3f9b011dc0a1b162eb997f336a","description":"NOTE FROM TED: This talk only represents the speaker’s personal views and experiences in the military. TEDx events are independently organized by volunteers. The guidelines we give TEDx organizers are described in more detail here: http://storage.ted.com/tedx/manuals/tedx_content_guidelines.pdf\n\nI am Lt Vamshi E (Retd). Based on my experiences throughout childhood poverty, pressure to succeed, confusion about careers, military training and service experience, entrepreneurship, mentoring, endurance sports and content creation, I have realized a few realities of life in our 20s, especially for the Indian youth. I am sharing those realizations in an easy to understand and implement format.  Ex-Army Officer, HR Professional, Mentor This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":761,"id":"cdcbf557-b1d2-4a52-ad00-00683643701e","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:49:15+00:00","title":"How to make the most of your 20s | Lt Vamshi E | TEDxSRIT","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Hj6VvQiFnEA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"71be8f0329c45a6787e43832ad148bc88fdadc76195afe6cd24f9842d2ff7fd2","description":"Inspired from different educational systems that Jonas has experienced, he seeks to critically assess the current education systems from a new perspective. Through his talk, Jonas hopes to propose a new system that creates a balanced learning environment.\n Jonas is a third year science major at AUC focusing on computer science and mathematics. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":683,"id":"ce191943-39ab-4544-b29b-1c22b689c600","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:02:30+00:00","title":"What chess can teach us about learning  | Jonas Dornieden | TEDxAU College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"SptG9xpBbdk"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"23776c5753b11465b9e24a46e01e4ff65ba95ec150775bccc426474a8ca42c04","description":"What does it take to stay in control of your life online? AI ethicist Jen Golbeck and TED Fellow Shalini Kantayya discuss how algorithms, data collection and AI shape the choices you make every day. They explore whether it’s possible to use the internet without giving up your privacy, how to push back against big tech and what AI should never be allowed to decide. \n\nIdea Knock Down features experts with different perspectives taking on big questions — one block at a time. Made possible with the support Visit Philadelphia.","duration_seconds":52,"id":"cee6d324-6019-458a-bd98-9b3ea563fd62","published_at":"2026-07-10T20:00:28+00:00","title":"Convenience may make your life easier. But what does it cost? #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Jlsag62r1TQ"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"d90e9282d96a0ea7314ecc74302eb9f29c5ea5c42a20634ad96260064511210e","description":"Neutrality can get you through a tough conversation, but it rarely gets you anywhere else. Joshua Johnson, actor/anchor for \"The Onion\" and former NPR host, makes the case instead for objectivity — parking your own opinions for a moment so you can focus on someone else's story. He shares three quick ways to turn your most frustrating conversations into real understanding. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 16, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/joshuajohnson\n\nhttps://youtu.be/ElYYD7Wx2WE\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Communication","duration_seconds":383,"id":"cfa4a6e7-7932-4aca-9116-55b4b2ee5e84","published_at":"2026-08-10T15:00:15+00:00","title":"Idiots Are Everywhere. Here’s How to Cope | Joshua Johnson | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ElYYD7Wx2WE"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"89d4d6b3dcb075de60ab806c8e3412a050f1f57afcce7ad79f36b5963bb94359","description":"As more parts of the world face a housing crisis, Lara explores the potential of intergenerational housing. Through case studies in Germany and the Netherlands she proposes a solution to this social crisis that equally benefits young and old generations. Lara Gleim is a 3rd year sciences major with a focus on neuroscientific research. Beyond that she likes connecting with people from different walks of life and meeting new people. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":724,"id":"cff0eefe-952b-4dfb-9d61-f33028c697e9","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:02:19+00:00","title":"Intergenerational living; rebuilding our village in the modern age | Lara Gleim | TEDxAU College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"1XAQ-D7PDvE"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"972db82924b43c368406b7b6010d06716e6a78e7dd5273370fe388e54455a847","description":"Diana confronts the universal confusions of adolescence — the growing pains — and shares her transformation from playing the role of the “perfect well‑behaved student” to bravely pursuing her desired major and embracing her uniqueness. G11 student from Jinrui School Shanghai This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":452,"id":"d0fce7bc-ca96-4090-8c88-5160329cf9da","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:16:08+00:00","title":"Reclaim self‑definition amid growing pains | Diana Bao | TEDxJRSS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"3nc_ObpmQ08"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"db8b875a97d431cba44b2daac3019def70a673dec497a8f9532f6459ddb05b3d","description":"This talk addresses how if our ancestors were to experience the amount of convenience we have in our day and time, they would be disappointed about how lazy we get and how much of opportunities we do not maximize.\nIt's a call to live life fully present and aware of the opportunities that come to us daily and take advantage of them. Favour Ehizibue is a virtual operations and tech assistant and founder of Favfrica Academy. A platform training Africans to earn in dollars through remote work and freelancing. Passionate about digital independence and youth empowerment, she has trained over one thousand Africans to land their first clients, with students going on to earn thousands of dollars individually. With Upwork Top Rated status and active clients across four continents, she built everything from scratch while still a university student.\n\nHer work sits at the intersection of digital skills, mindset, and economic freedom, with a mission to ensure that every young African who is waiting to feel ready understands exactly what that wait is costing them. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":698,"id":"d130a7ec-a47e-46bf-b9e6-ea74c32cd9e5","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:56:03+00:00","title":"My ancestors would be embarrassed by me. | Favour Ehizibue | TEDxSamaru","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"rKBlN0UYBvg"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"014bc7cc99f84a43954b3a3622be7c578fd2e195b51f146988447697ab034f50","description":"From dreaming of Bollywood stardom in 1998 to becoming an international oral implantologist and community leader, Dr. Rahul Chauhan shares his incredible journey.\n\nSpeaking under the event theme \"Lucid Maze,\" Dr. Rahul reflects on how giving 100% to unexpected detours transformed his career—leading to groundbreaking cosmetic surgeries, international dental practices in Dubai, and award-winning community transformation projects.\n\nKey Takeaways:\n- Why \"accidental\" detours often lead to your greatest breakthroughs\n- How 100% commitment transforms secondary choices into top-tier careers\n- The power of continuous learning in healthcare, tech, and beyond\n- How individual initiative can drive massive community change Dr. Rahul Chavan is a highly experienced Periodontist and Oral Implantologist, having completed his MDS from Government Dental College, Nagpur, Maharashtra in 1998. With over 25 years of clinical excellence, he is currently a full-time private practitioner and an international consultant in advanced dental care.\n\nHe has contributed extensively to dental education as a former teaching faculty at prestigious institutions including DY Patil Dental College (Navi Mumbai), Sharad Pawar Dental College (Wardha), MGM Dental College (Navi Mumbai), SMBT Dental College (Sangamner), Rungta Dental College (Bhilai), and Guru Govind Singh Dental College (Burhanpur).\n\nBeyond dentistry, Dr. Chavan is an active social worker and serves as the Secretary of a large housing society in Mumbai. He is widely credited for transforming a dilapidated residential complex into one of the most well-organized and iconic societies in Mumbai and Maharashtra through visionary leadership and execution. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1317,"id":"d1405f47-f016-472a-93bd-4f9000ff21ef","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:07:52+00:00","title":"Life is a Maze: Turning Unplanned Detours into Career Success | Dr. Rahul Chauhan | TEDxSFIT","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"pjjYa7x5I5o"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"3672c63c0a86c32ecdd63e27de7a7d15995ad26b9f0bda9c75ea04c35aeb327f","description":"In mapping and analyzing issues at work that impact media workers' mental health and well-being, a paradoxical conclusion emerges: what makes media work special is also what can make its people sick. Drawing on his research Mark Deuze explores the challenges of living a good life in media. \n Mark Deuze is Professor of Media Studies at the University of Amsterdam, author of 14 books (including \"Life in Media\" with The MIT Press), and bass player of the band Skinflower. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1089,"id":"d1abb29d-fdcd-4589-9e22-0df1a786db10","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:03:37+00:00","title":"How to live a good life in media | Mark Deuze | TEDxAU College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"fkpShGXvAKg"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"e6c0f544415e7ef44f5a0fad540d24379d5142b5d7a2debe60b76e364345f71c","description":"If you ate bread this week (or cooked with salt and oil), you probably benefited from something millions miss out on: food fortification. Social entrepreneur Felix Brooks-church is on a mission to solve \"hidden hunger\" — malnutrition caused by a lack of nutrients — by fortifying everyday foods for regions of the world hit hardest by food insecurity. Laying out a pragmatic vision to improve food systems globally, he reminds us that hunger doesn’t always look like an empty plate. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/felixbrookschurch\n\nhttps://youtu.be/-Z-Miko0nrQ\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Design","duration_seconds":696,"id":"d1d808aa-f626-4f1b-916d-1f72bb20c524","published_at":"2026-08-03T15:00:11+00:00","title":"How I Built a Machine That Feeds Millions | Felix Brooks-church | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"-Z-Miko0nrQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"499001250fe35ec1e42ede06315d5eeedf21777a9f8c1ba801edd2c1aed02387","description":"Nour Salem shares his journey from aspiring to become an actor to finding his way into the world of social media. Without connections or an easy path into the industry, he turned to content creation as a way to build his own platform and create opportunities for himself.\n\nConnection to “The Missing Part”:\nHis story reflects the idea that sometimes the missing part isn’t a person or a connection, but the courage to create your own path when the one you wanted seems out of reach. Nour Salem is an Egyptian digital creator and entertainer known for his presence on social media, particularly Instagram and TikTok. He creates lifestyle and entertainment-focused content and has built a growing audience of more than 60K followers on Instagram. His content reflects a youthful, trendy, and charismatic personality, with a strong focus on personal style, lifestyle, and engaging visual content. He describes himself as an Artist and Elite Entertainer, and his presence across social platforms continues to attract a young audience. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":705,"id":"d36e93d8-be6b-4a77-ae65-9eb5104aee54","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:58:31+00:00","title":"Creating Your Own Path | Nour Salem | TEDxMansoura National University","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"MRop-IigejU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"3b86a90b71930975c0693dc2b504fb570ba23f4ef4933a14a86e615a75e3773c","description":"Our human civilization and the science we have produced has stemmed from our imagination, curiosity, passion, compassion and courage, and AI won't change the importance of these human traits in pushing forward our species. \n\nThis talk features examples from history and how science has advanced with these human qualities. Lydia Kostopoulos is an emerging technology advisor who helps organisations imagine new competitive futures for themselves. She is also the author of Imagination Dilemma - a book arguing that in an era of AI-led disruption, our greatest competitive advantage is the one thing AI can't replicate. Her talk, Humanity's Moat Against AI, takes that argument head-on. Drawing on examples from across history and science, she makes the case that imagination, curiosity, passion, compassion, and courage aren't soft qualities to be nostalgic about - they are the defining human advantages of our time. Rather than asking how we compete with AI, she asks a better question: what becomes possible when we stop trying to match it, and start leaning into what only we can do? This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":527,"id":"d3e0ff10-a1f4-4fdf-aaf0-a64d9c9c2add","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:37:19+00:00","title":"Humanity's Moat Against AI | Lydia Kostopoulos | TEDxLeith","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"_agiAJx_PDA"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"1ba19a9dc66f02747817bbbccee60744636c769837d4668f05fa7ebe947d43f1","description":"Musician Wallis Bird performs three genre-bending songs with carefully crafted lyrics that weave themes of grief and societal struggle. The result is raw, roaring and deeply moving. (With singer Tracey Kelleher and followed by a note from TED guest curator Jacob Collier) (Recorded at TED2026 on April 15, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/wallisbird\n\nhttps://youtu.be/XJUcZV_F_T0\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Performance #Music","duration_seconds":610,"id":"d461b6b8-c45a-4f88-b610-d7dae405a11a","published_at":"2026-08-08T15:00:03+00:00","title":"“To My Bones” / “As the River Flows” / “In Dictum” | Wallis Bird | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"XJUcZV_F_T0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"81bbf21c6f95aef658982e79b50f12cf1da130fe92b46c4ea791770d2c8f4a6b","description":"“戏剧是什么？”这是戏剧导演赵易阳反复思考、并尝试回答的问题。\n在他看来， 戏剧不仅是一种表演艺术，更像是一把隐形的椅子，让那些无处安放的情绪坐下来，得以被看见。\n在这场演讲中，他以简单而真实的故事切入，从木河马的孤独到小山村的戏剧社，他让我们看到：在戏剧的世界里，没有对错，只有“尝试”；这是一种开放的拥抱，能帮助每个人看见自己，看见彼此。\n戏剧就像一座桥梁，在生活的洪流中，连接孤独与共鸣，安全地接住隐藏的情绪与故事，并将之缓缓导向治愈与希望。 深圳超级猴戏剧Club创始人\n戏剧导演 This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":611,"id":"d4782335-e883-45b5-8486-113e1ac92558","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:48:46+00:00","title":"戏剧不是生活的解药，但它能给你一把接住情绪的椅子 | 易阳 赵 | TEDxNanshan Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"RsI-8_Q-DkM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"51d1e93e742846535fd4a445963b042d5877c48982dc082e993f21fba7582dea","description":"#shorts #tedx #ted #truecrime #media","duration_seconds":80,"id":"d65b04ce-c818-4124-9e4f-cf11ec063750","published_at":"2026-09-02T17:00:28+00:00","title":"Why are we so obsessed with true crime media? #shorts #tedx","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"O_bL-3IJ2gs"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"5f6f785ef197a865280e9626a4e7944483f12ec640d9d39e621695096a26702b","description":"Zack Kass's talk challenges us to rethink the narrative around technological progress, innovation, and impact- through the lens of historical events. \n\nHis talk explores how we can become an active participant in the future that delivers more agency, dignity, and time. Zack Kass has spent more than sixteen years at the forefront of artificial intelligence.\n\nAs Head of Go-to-Market at OpenAI, he led the teams responsible for sales, partnerships, and customer success, turning the company’s cutting-edge research into real-world business solutions, and growing revenue from millions to billions. \n\nSince then he has advised more than 500 of the Global 5000, taught at University of Virginia and Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, and published his first book, an international bestseller, The Next RenAIssance: AI and the Expansion of Human Potential.\n\nHis mission is to ensure everyone can be an active participant in future that delivers more agency, dignity, and time. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":836,"id":"d69de9b8-add2-4983-9bcc-4bf7e594ceb2","published_at":"2026-09-01T16:35:14+00:00","title":"The Future of Living | Zack Kass | TEDxMiami","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"sIJAn32Mzc8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"fef2534ed63c583679f632b87c967b7adcaeeb82612046c1458b908862701aab","description":"Drawing on the common experience of homework burnout among students, Eason argues we often mislabel struggles as laziness. He breaks down core differences between extrinsic and intrinsic motivation and offers actionable strategies for building motivation. G12 student from Jinrui School Shanghai This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":759,"id":"d73f6778-db8a-479c-aecb-050218686f1f","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:18:04+00:00","title":"Demystify Motivation | Eason Yuan | TEDxJRSS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"cXzmI27R4Ug"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"38842015f58f024afdf35212900dfdf560bed628c73019233b811a5885abb4d1","description":"Niamh shares her personal thoughts about grief and offers a new perspective. Niamh is a student at Marist-Sion College. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":354,"id":"d7e2ec6d-ed02-493f-91dd-f29009599b75","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:46:23+00:00","title":"Grief is not just about death | Niamh Juster | TEDxMarist Sion College Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"df6zUCL6Q2s"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"7fe454d1c47ff6028fd65f976d350d94b2c0566c249d91313f132bd42efe3ac2","description":"What if the worst ideas turned out to be the most interesting? Mischief-making artist Gabe Whaley shares how he built a collective of artists whose seemingly bad ideas — like selling 5,000 keys to the same car — often go viral and sell for thousands of dollars. His playful experiments show why it’s worth leaning into the absurd … because you never know what might happen.","duration_seconds":101,"id":"d98dc9b1-cc33-4ba9-97f7-d59c95652f9f","published_at":"2026-06-24T14:00:02+00:00","title":"One person’s “bad idea” may be another’s new obsession #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"TEZPOfbWOCU"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"ac580a5b01c03209982e9678b8864aa7ea64bed362f310416bf9bb6dc1e7b611","description":"Artist Joe Whale used to get in trouble for doodling in class, until a local restaurant let him cover an entire wall with his artwork … and the internet took notice. He traces his unlikely beginning to a bigger story: your imagination is a muscle, and it weakens when you hand it off to AI. Through a live doodling demo, he shows in real time what it looks like to let a thought become a drawing, one line at a time. (Recorded at Play@TED on May 14, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/joewhale\n\nhttps://youtu.be/rIRon8zJyps\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Art","duration_seconds":388,"id":"d9eab422-943d-48f7-b3e1-015075ed41a7","published_at":"2026-08-30T15:00:02+00:00","title":"How Doodling Unlocks Your Imagination | Joe Whale | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"rIRon8zJyps"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"0d5e9f27224d0d477df7ab55706f8e65cb7bdcff71745d19acc7b70851292dbb","description":"In this ode to improvisation, musician and comedian Reggie Watts beatboxes, raps, loops his own rhythms and reflects upon the everyday power of turning the mundane into magic. After all, he says, we're all just making it up as we go along.","duration_seconds":105,"id":"da72a1b7-e73d-408d-b786-5a5662851906","published_at":"2026-07-23T22:15:15+00:00","title":"Turns out, your brain is freestyling daily #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"POySLLhF2_c"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"62f7b0c4a1c182b5562b7f071ae17c1fbc625399806c6e68c1207e039ec71b6e","description":"Great ideas are everywhere—but only a few manage to take over the world. What separates an idea that remains unnoticed from one that reaches millions?\n\nIn this talk, Vasanth Kumar explores “The Distribution Effect”—the often-overlooked force that determines how ideas, products, and brands move from a few people to the masses. Drawing from his experience working with startups, entrepreneurs, and growth-stage businesses, he reveals why creating something valuable is only half the journey.\n\nThe real advantage lies in knowing how to distribute, communicate, build trust, and create momentum around an idea.\n\nThrough practical insights and entrepreneurial experiences, Vasanth challenges us to rethink how we define innovation and growth—and shows why sometimes, the difference between a good idea and a world-changing one is simply how far it travels. Vasanth Kumar Rengan is the Founder & CEO of Brand Mindz Global Technology Pvt. Ltd., a business coach, and a National Public Speaking Champion. He works with startups, family businesses, and growth-stage companies to build brands, accelerate business growth, and create sustainable competitive advantage through strategy, marketing, and technology.\n\nHaving trained over 30,000 entrepreneurs, students, and professionals, Vasanth believes marketing should go beyond visibility to create measurable business impact. At TEDx, he shares practical insights from his entrepreneurial journey, leadership experiences, and the strategies that help businesses build trust, scale with purpose, and achieve long-term success. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":606,"id":"daadb3f4-85a5-46fa-ba7b-3c5cc1a9381e","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:39:41+00:00","title":"Why some ideas take over the world | Vasanth Kumar | TEDxKanniyakumari","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Wka_TIr6coU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"95eacae973a54a42c389d1eb75f32d0b95ad3ffec33b2a148d51859408a14855","description":"As artificial intelligence becomes increasingly capable of thinking, creating, predicting, and automating, a deeper question emerges: What becomes uniquely human when machines can do so much for us?\n\nIn this thought-provoking talk, Adithya Mangal explores the relationship between artificial intelligence and one of humanity’s most powerful yet least understood capabilities — intuition. Aditya Mangal leads strategic partnerships with early-stage startups at Microsoft, where his work sits at the intersection of enterprise scale and founder ambition. His focus is on India's dynamic startup ecosystem — identifying high-potential ventures, aligning cross-functional teams around them, and channelling Microsoft's technology and resources to accelerate their growth. With expertise spanning channel sales, partner relationship management, and customer acquisition, Aditya has helped founders shorten the distance between an idea and its market. He believes the next decade of Indian innovation will be defined less by what big companies build and more by who they choose to back. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":669,"id":"dc407aa5-b5f2-435f-adfa-19f815270cf9","published_at":"2026-09-02T14:57:37+00:00","title":"The Architecture of Intuition: How AI Frees Us to Be Human | Adithya Mangal | TEDxKanniyakumari","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"gnx2QpIcbs0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"ae3acaf122248190b08541eec64d9872aba13424b0e3435e8962217a4c2e2ff1","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\n\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\n\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\n\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\n\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology\n Anupam Rana Magar is an MYP Year 3 student and a dynamic young individual known for his eloquence, musical talent, and quick wit. An accomplished public speaker and event host, Anup is also a World Scholar’s Cup Global Qualifier and a participant in the 2026 FLAME University MUN and Knowledge Festival. Beyond his academic and public-speaking pursuits, he is a gifted flautist who brings creativity and expression to his music. His journey reflects a unique blend of confidence, versatility, talent, and engaging personality. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":458,"id":"dc444d8c-034e-46a8-8801-4b2109e17cea","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:11:58+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Era | Anupam Rana Magar Rana Magar | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ppFIxQAK0t4"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"ab3fa194dcb164d85637d361c17490e71d70d4dfb6a9efac3b2578441a37bab3","description":"Our brains are hardwired to crave community and belonging — a tribal instinct that drives politics in the United States, says political strategist Sarah Longwell. She shares what she learned trying to convince people to vote against their political party in a recent election and shows why telling a better story about democracy is key to bridging the ideological divide.","duration_seconds":65,"id":"dd0e743d-7d85-4c20-9cba-d37dfcd58193","published_at":"2026-07-07T18:30:31+00:00","title":"\"If we can tell a better story about democracy, we can tell a better story about America\" #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"BG7NCjrOfw4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"24e940526c10c901620f3ea5fa7c1541f779c7e53df38d3e2412db35a12f716c","description":"What happens when someone finally feels seen?\n\nIn a world where people are often measured by performance, titles, and results, “The Power of Being Seen” explores the transformative impact of recognition, empathy, and human connection. Karthika Murugeswaran shares insights from her journey in Human Resources to reveal why feeling valued and heard can unlock confidence, belonging, and untapped potential.\n\nThis talk invites us to look beyond roles and responsibilities—to truly see the people around us.\n\nBecause sometimes, being seen is all it takes to help someone believe in themselves. Karthika Murugeswaran is a seasoned Human Resources professional with over four years of experience across the full spectrum of HR, including Talent Acquisition, HR Operations, Employee Engagement, Payroll Management, and HR Strategy. Her career has been driven by a simple yet powerful belief: organizations thrive when people do.\n\nWhat began as a curiosity to understand people and workplace dynamics has evolved into a mission to create inclusive, people-first cultures where individuals feel valued, empowered, and inspired to perform at their best. Throughout her journey, Karthika has successfully partnered with organizations to strengthen HR processes, enhance employee experiences, and align people strategies with business goals.\n\n\n\nAt TEDx, Karthika brings her real-world experiences and people-centric perspective to inspire audiences to rethink the role of HR—not as a support function, but as a catalyst for meaningful organizational transformation. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":542,"id":"de38f78d-93c5-4bdc-8233-2d52f56fb1a3","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:10:00+00:00","title":"The Power of Being Seen | Karthika Murugeswaran | TEDxKanniyakumari","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"M63kMq75Arc"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"97c26782fc1eea27c329942c254023d1040a4138c1a15e401b99fe637186bd46","description":"Six months into a catastrophic war in Iran — and with no end in sight for the war in Ukraine — the world is embroiled in conflicts with no apparent exit plan. In this interview, political scientist and Eurasia Group founder Ian Bremmer breaks down what these wars are costing people around the world — and which one he's more worried might spiral out of control. (This interview, hosted by TED's Helen Walters, was recorded on July 28, 2026.)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/tedexplainstheworld\n\nhttps://youtu.be/DyzXwiyr1Fo\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Iran #Ukraine","duration_seconds":2296,"id":"e04cca64-e05e-48c1-bff9-55f36a6a876f","published_at":"2026-07-29T18:36:30+00:00","title":"Two Wars, No Clear Exit Plan. Now What? | TED Explains the World with Ian Bremmer","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"DyzXwiyr1Fo"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"caadc45464ffdb4ef0f1b9924048c5d4f36605a7a4c5016623115ee8d39e9b1a","description":"In this captivating talk, journey into the surprisingly colorful nights of the Amazon Rainforest, as artistic entomologist Leo Lanna and designer Lvcas Fiat introduce us to the shapeshifting wonder of a creature they've fallen in love with: the praying mantis. Using an innovative approach that fuses science, art and conservation, Lanna and Fiat uncover the unimaginable biodiversity in this natural kingdom, proving that the age of exploration on Earth is far from over.","duration_seconds":71,"id":"e061f925-b3ee-4a59-be84-9ac680d91a6d","published_at":"2026-07-16T19:00:33+00:00","title":"These praying mantises are unbe-leaf-able #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"pLVtKlyHKoQ"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"7a3e02acd5729c456d6c745230948f30fb9d75bd171746daec24aceb4487421e","description":"What does it look like when a city becomes a laboratory for innovation? His Excellency Khalfan Belhoul, CEO of the Dubai Future Foundation, explains why Dubai is cutting the bureaucratic red tape to experiment with big ideas in everything from AI and emerging tech to finance and climate solutions. The future, he says, depends on those willing to test it. (This conversation is hosted by TED's Whitney Pennington Rodgers.) (Recorded at TED@BCG on October 23, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/hekhalfanbelhoul\n\nhttps://youtu.be/EUKbVj2iiSE\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Technology","duration_seconds":749,"id":"e11663f9-c72e-4080-b25e-f9d486c181c1","published_at":"2026-07-31T15:00:26+00:00","title":"Inside Dubai’s Mission to Build the City of the Future | His Excellency Khalfan Belhoul | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"EUKbVj2iiSE"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"65f37d8095c8777ed476a34ff3b7164ab29b711f246f691db822c9462682ca8c","description":"Addiction is often seen simply as a problem caused by drugs, but the reality is far more complex. This talk explores the hidden forces that can make people vulnerable to addiction, including isolation, stress, loneliness, trauma, social pressure, and the environment around them. Through the Rat Park experiment, real-life stories, and examples from India and around the world, the talk examines how connection, purpose, compassion, and community can play a powerful role in prevention and recovery. It ultimately asks us to look beyond addiction itself and address the deeper reasons that make people seek an escape in the first place. Krishna Vijay is a proactive youth leader, published author, and community builder dedicated to fostering intergenerational solidarity and youth empowerment. Currently pursuing her Class XII studies in the Humanities at Sri Mutha School in Chennai, Krishna is\nthe founder of Bridging the Gap, a youth-led non-profit initiative designed to preserve cultural heritage and connect generations, she pioneered and hosted the landmark Tamil Nadu Legacy Summit. An accomplished writer, Krishna authored Grand Stories, Wise Lessons—a book of intergenerational profiles featuring a foreword by Dr. Shashi Tharoor —and is currently co- authoring a biographical work on Indian Paralympic medallists. She also serves as the Chief Student Ambassador for Tamil Nadu under a youth well-being initiative directed by the Narcotics Control Bureau and Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE). Combining her background in public speaking with training in Bharatanatyam and Western classical\nmusic. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":622,"id":"e298898d-e1cd-4485-a8b3-b5198838db0a","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:15:55+00:00","title":"Beyond the High: Hidden Forces Behind Addiction | Krishna Vijay | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"5c1SBxBgqc8"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"8ce179e513ce7e5256b7c1f68f12f9c2c3727d9dc713c0e93f72af5693d08fc6","description":"What's the most powerful tool in any classroom? In the age of AI, education advocate Matt Wu still believes it's the people. In this hopeful talk, he discusses the nonprofit he leads, Schoolhouse, which pairs students with peer tutors from across the globe, building the crucial human connection that every person needs to thrive. Peer tutoring isn't just teaching students how to learn concepts, says Wu. It's teaching them how to better understand one another. (Recorded at TED2026 on April 17, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/mattwu\n\nhttps://youtu.be/xHr18GEJqck\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Education","duration_seconds":700,"id":"e32ef5ac-9fdf-423f-bb8b-ac85e3aa2700","published_at":"2026-07-26T15:00:33+00:00","title":"Why AI Will Never Replace a Great Teacher | Matt Wu | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"xHr18GEJqck"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"fb3c05d12feb0998c4a48311df4bee27fbdbce2afe1046ea8beb1cef7a448ef5","description":"Sophia talks about feminism today and what it means for all of us. Sophia is a student at Marist-Sion College. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":372,"id":"e3482aab-86fa-4f02-905a-dd1d125249fc","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:46:07+00:00","title":"Feminism is still disregarded in the modern world | Sophia Phillips | TEDxMarist Sion College Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"VnV1e1LRWxw"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c65c6d451cf355e9c4fff942edebd3ab4382a3958b76736898cf6505e3319fc2","description":"Antonello Sidoti parte da una domanda semplice: e se il confine più difficile da superare fosse proprio il nostro Io?\nAttraverso la sua esperienza nella scuola di italiano per migranti Penny Wirton racconta come l'incontro con l'altro riesca a scardinare paure, pregiudizi e indifferenza. Le storie di ragazzi, famiglie e rifugiati mostrano che ogni relazione autentica è capace di trasformare non solo chi arriva, ma anche chi accoglie.\nIl suo è un invito a riconoscere i diritti fondamentali di ogni persona e a lasciare che l'Io si apra fino a diventare un \"noi\". Da 41 anni si occupa di dipendenze e disagio giovanile. Musicista e referente provinciale del programma “Nati per la Musica”. \nNel 2013 fonda l'associazione “Wind of Change”, la cui sede operativa è una biblioteca di quartiere, dentro la quale opera la scuola di italiano per stranieri “Penny Wirton”. \nCollabora con i dipartimenti Scipog e Cospecs dell'Università di Messina in qualità di Tutor per i tirocini formativi del CdS in Scienze del Servizio Sociale e co-ricercatore nel Tavolo dei Saperi Professionali. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":916,"id":"e417e2c1-08cb-4601-8332-a3b4d0e05ce0","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:40:06+00:00","title":"Io come confine del \"Noi\" | Antonello Sidoti | TEDxMessina","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"q73XI1l5lRA"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"a83c623ce5d34bd5851f651f089df6e449d371f75f8b0ce071685c5e44dc54c1","description":"He convinced us to let him do a TEDx Talk about Harambe. Though he really ended up giving us all insight into Gen-Z Culture. Alex Garfin, born September 23rd 2003, is an internationally acclaimed actor and youth activist who has dedicated his personal life to uniting people. His acting career spans over a decade with highlights including playing “Superboy” in “Superman and Lois,” “Linus” in “The Peanuts Movie” and a storied on-stage career in NYC. \n\nIn his advocacy career, he serves as the head of youth and board secretary of the UN ECOSOC NGO World Information Transfer leading a staff of ten young people at UNHQ. He is the founder of The Alethos Initiative, a nonprofit that gives influencers journalism training and access to experts to fight the spread of misinformation online. He says the roots of his advocacy work are in his travels engaging with people on a grassroots level around the world. He has been to 24 countries so far, including the Middle East, the far east, Africa and 30 American states. He plans to continue to engage in grassroots dialogue domestically and abroad. \n\nThis talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":684,"id":"e489f64f-4f2e-41cf-965e-ef1ba23a8b51","published_at":"2026-09-01T17:00:38+00:00","title":"How Harambe kickstarted Gen Z culture | Alex Garfin | TEDxBerlin","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"fqeGsMO50Gw"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"be3a8830d68476b9b2725fcf9b4740a409af6cd71de9bc9439940fd8c0e02a5a","description":"If you've ever been swept away by the worlds of \"Wicked,\" \"Hamilton\" or \"West Side Story,\" you've seen Paul Tazewell's breathtaking costumes. The Oscar-winning designer (whose work features in \"Wicked: For Good\") explores the subconscious language of clothing and how it shapes who we view as heroes — and who we view as villains.","duration_seconds":154,"id":"e4fe4588-5c61-4ea9-ab96-97327ea7ef17","published_at":"2026-07-03T18:00:23+00:00","title":"These costumes did not throw away their shot #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"kiHmIZHp9uI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"894db37ce7ebc92ed49d56a3152bc7e1bb5c9f6f1d73b19e4d057ee316cc7aa2","description":"Using his tennis training experience, Michael vividly shows the dual sides of boundaries. He recounts his journey from match losses to persistent hard work, earning breakthroughs and the role of school team captain. G12 student from Jinrui School Shanghai This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":444,"id":"e50c59e5-a4fe-4612-a079-eb5dea28edbf","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:17:27+00:00","title":"Comfort Zone — Shelter or Vision Limiter? | Michael Hu | TEDxJRSS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"4rbjPzZc2E0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"c41a8c547ef3311923c74dc16438d084b9e8f1ac04e7b50104f9ddd13389902c","description":"We are taught that marks, degrees, and a polished résumé are what open doors. But beyond the visible résumé lies another one, shaped by who knows you, who vouches for you, and how people remember you. Drawing from 15 years of experience on the hiring side, this talk uncovers the three hidden forces behind opportunity: sponsorship, weak ties, and reputation. Through practical and actionable insights, it shows how anyone can begin building their own invisible résumé and create opportunities that qualifications alone cannot.\n Gokulakannan Narasimhan is an HR leader with 14+ years in Talent Acquisition, Development, and Management. He scaled talent strategies for Cognizant, Amazon, Intuit, and ServiceNow, leading Global Emerging Talent hiring across APJ.\n\nHe hired thousands of early-career professionals, built high-performing teams, and created strategies balancing growth with candidate experience. His expertise spans campus recruitment, workforce planning, employer branding, and talent pipelines.\n\nPassionate about workforce, he delivers lectures and keynotes at universities and HR forums across India, blending industry insights with real-world experiences to help students transition to corporate life and equip HR professionals with modern strategies.\n\nToday, he contributes as a speaker, mentor, and thought leader on the future of work, AI in recruitment, and building world-class talent organizations. Recognized for storytelling and practical insights, he inspires the next generation of talent and HR leaders This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":784,"id":"e59c0303-76c6-42e7-94e8-316f2d214171","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:14:49+00:00","title":"The invisible resume | Gokulakannan Narasimhan | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"24jEnm7Z_1s"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"31c9379c322bb6964663ef483b98cb29f983faa8c8bb39ac0d1a57b7788bf109","description":"Generative AI hallucinates, creating a truth problem that science can't afford. Computer scientist Tudor Achim thinks a 400-year-old idea holds the fix: Leibniz's dream of a logical framework where errors are simply impossible. Learn about his idea for mathematical superintelligence that would ground AI in formal verification, turning unreliable chatbots into rigorous partners for scientific discovery. (Recorded at TEDAI San Francisco 2025 on October 21, 2025)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/tudorachim\n\nhttps://youtu.be/HSxytYCWVow\n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Technology","duration_seconds":772,"id":"e6ee0505-bdc1-4b26-9927-390e08b56229","published_at":"2026-08-11T16:00:39+00:00","title":"The Path to Mathematical Superintelligence | Tudor Achim | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"HSxytYCWVow"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"1ec632629978683dd000709045381539650c12cd888dff923c1b275ccae98116","description":"Burnout shouldn’t be the price of success, but setting boundaries at work is easier said than done. Tarveen Forrester, who oversees workplace culture at Kickstarter, shares practical strategies for protecting your time and cultivating “sustainable ambition,” so you can crush your goals — without letting them crush you.","duration_seconds":80,"id":"e76cef3e-431b-425a-ae6b-62bd02b045b0","published_at":"2026-08-25T19:00:13+00:00","title":"Burnout shouldn’t be the price of your success #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"JOcnvPZSt8E"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e24f21ae15b42825697bd1c9993ddd10a214611b71684932b41cd5ac7332649b","description":"山田 理 : アフターインターネットの社会で、起業の大衆化がGAME CHANGERになる\n人生の大半は、仕事をすることに時間を費やす。\nでは、会社に入ると幸せな人生を得られるのか。\n山田は人が幸せになるために「複業で起業」という選択肢を持とう、と叫ぶ。\nそのためには「2匹のモンスター」を倒すことが大事である。\nそれはいったいどういうことなのか。\n\n\nYAMADA Osamu : \nPost-Internet Society:Democratizing Entrepreneurship Changes the Game\n\nMost of our lives are spent working — but does corporate life really bring happiness? \nYamada argues for a new path: entrepreneurship without quitting your job. \nThe catch? Two monsters stand in the way. \nWhat are they, and how do we defeat them?\n\n\n He Joined the Industrial Bank of Japan in 1992.\nHe made a career move  to Cybozu during its founding phase in 2000. He served as Vice President overseeing finance, human resources, and legal affairs.\nHe established the foundation for Cybozu's organization, realizing its vision of 100 ways of working for 100 people, emphasizing transparency.\nHe transferred to Silicon Valley in 2014 to establish the U.S. corporation. He pressed ahead with building the organizational structure supporting Cybozu's global expansion across the U.S., Asia, and so on.\nIn 2024, he left Cybozu to start his own business and founded KT Game Changers Inc. As an organizational culture consultant, he works to spread  culture where 100 people can thrive in 100 different ways.\n\n\nMajor  books include The Lightest Management (Cybozu-shiki Books) and The Corporate Employee's Guide (Daiwa Shobo). This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":908,"id":"e9de8be3-782e-44c3-b65c-044c1d049a6c","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:15:25+00:00","title":"アフターインターネットの社会で、起業の大衆化がGAME CHANGERになる | Osamu YAMADA | TEDxKumamoto","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"5t0wDGJtOrU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"3c2214e9003dfdb3ac3e422d223413400e0d954a1bc22d149c85900fc38d87b7","description":"Annie Téllez, a third-grade primary school student at Gimnasio El Hontanar, shares an inspiring message about the creativity and potential of children. In her talk, “Children Can Be Inventors Too!”, she explores how curiosity, imagination, and simple ideas can become meaningful inventions. Annie encourages other children to believe in their ideas, think creatively, and recognize that you don’t have to be an adult to invent something that can make a difference. Annie is a vibrant and enthusiastic 3rd-grade student at Gimnasio El Hontanar who stands out for her confidence and passion for public speaking. A natural and expressive communicator, Annie enjoys sharing her ideas, connecting with her audience, and using her voice to inspire others. Her enthusiasm, creativity, and growing confidence make her a passionate young speaker with a bright future ahead. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":261,"id":"e9f963f0-3051-4218-a7e5-724d5801c9c6","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:40:17+00:00","title":"Children can be inventors too! | Annie Gabriela Téllez Buitrago | TEDxGimnasio El Hontanar Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"afY-3xW447o"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"41865584d00342ae6de5beb1f84e700b172de17d47cfbefbd0d851673bcd6b33","description":"What if the biggest thing holding you back isn't a lack of talent or opportunity—but waiting until everything feels \"perfect\"?\n\nIn this inspiring TEDxSFIT Talk, tech entrepreneur, blockchain pioneer, and SFIT alumnus Jetso Analin shares how embracing uncertainty transformed his career. From struggling to understand the Bitcoin whitepaper on his first read to building a global Blockchain & AI company, his journey proves that success doesn't come from having all the answers—it comes from taking action despite the unknown.\n\nIntroducing the idea of the \"Lucid Maze,\" Jetso draws a powerful parallel between lucid dreaming and entrepreneurship: the goal isn't to escape uncertainty, but to learn how to navigate it with confidence.\n\n Jetso Analin is a multi-vertical entrepreneur and business strategist with a strong focus on blockchain, AI, and Web3 technologies. He leads Block Intelligence, a global blockchain and AI development firm with clients spanning government institutions, enterprises, and Web3 startups across LATAM, India, Middle East, Europe , and beyond.\n\nBeyond tech, Jetso manages an influencer marketing agency, a news and media operation, and is actively building into luxury and hospitality\n\nKnown for identifying market gaps and turning them into structured business systems, Jetso operates at the intersection of technology, digital media, and entrepreneurship. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1186,"id":"eafd56ec-4fe6-49d4-ace3-5e8d400538c6","published_at":"2026-09-04T16:07:34+00:00","title":"Stop Waiting for Clarity: Take the First Step | Jetso Analin | TEDxSFIT","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"UObw_uRWLUQ"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"080d6f0888b11bd18ba729fed5060e21e9873cb878147870cc5128c80fed3398","description":"Behind every successful journey are people, relationships, and opportunities that may never be visible to the outside world. Drawing from over four decades in cinema and his experiences as a filmmaker, actor, entrepreneur, and social innovator, Suresh Chandra Menon explores the unseen influences that have shaped his life and career. Through stories from his journey in the film industry, he reflects on the power of networking, meaningful connections, and the people who open doors, create opportunities, and influence the paths we take. The talk invites us to recognise how the relationships we build can quietly shape our choices, careers, and lives.\n Suresh Chandra Menon is a National Award-winning filmmaker, actor, entrepreneur, and social innovator with over 48 years of experience in the Indian film industry. He has built a distinguished career as a cinematographer, writer, director, producer, executive producer, and actor, making significant contributions to both cinema and television. He directed the acclaimed film Pudhiya Mugam and produced the National Award-winning English feature film Mitr, My Friend. As the founder of Telephoto Entertainments Limited, he pioneered technological advancements in Indian filmmaking and produced over 1,000 television episodes and 150 advertising and corporate films. Beyond entertainment, he has contributed to public infrastructure as a Traffic Planning Consultant to the Chennai Police, implemented practical traffic solutions, established successful hospitality ventures, and is an avid bird photographer. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":863,"id":"eb36b307-ee0c-43fc-8440-21d15d8cbb21","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:19:46+00:00","title":"Unseen Influences | Suresh Chandra Menon | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"GivoIKTalhI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"a6956ecdf79a839770606ce794b35bb0969b1171616273cf983c727674c994a6","description":"¿Y si el verdadero precio de tu celular no se midiera en dinero, sino en horas reales de tu vida? En esta charla reveladora, Paulina Yazigi cuestiona la famosa máxima de Benjamin Franklin de que \"el tiempo es dinero\", demostrando que el tiempo es una moneda mucho más valiosa y no renovable que impulsa nuestras decisiones personales, el sistema bancario y la macroeconomía. A medida que la tecnología y la inteligencia artificial transforman nuestro mundo hacia la abundancia absoluta, ella nos insta a repensar críticamente cómo invertimos nuestro recurso más escaso y precioso. Paulina Yazigi Salamanca es Ingeniera Comercial de la Pontificia Universidad Católica de Chile, Magíster en Economía Aplicada de la misma casa de estudios y Máster en Matemáticas en Finanzas de la Universidad de Nueva York. Actualmente se desempeña como vicepresidenta de BancoEstado, cargo que asumió en marzo de 2026. Previamente, entre 2023 y comienzos de 2026, presidió la Asociación de AFP de Chile, y entre 2021 y 2022 lideró la División de Mercados Financieros del Banco Central de Chile. Ha dedicado su carrera profesional a la macroeconomía y los mercados financieros, tanto en el sector público como privado, ocupando cargos de inversión y research en instituciones como BBVA, Compass Group, Credicorp Capital e IM Trust, en Chile y Nueva York. Ha sido además directora de Redbanc y de la Bolsa Electrónica de Chile, y fue reconocida entre las 100 Mujeres Líderes de Chile en 2018, 2023, 2024 y 2025. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1076,"id":"eb5b6d17-bf0e-4fdc-af74-2dce259c07fb","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:10:40+00:00","title":"\"Es tiempo, no dinero\" | Paulina Yazigi | TEDxPUC de Chile","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:06.493895+00:00","youtube_video_id":"gr_oJjElQ50"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"65a1410576e3e264b3732aa2b66a2ddd3efc141913d0d8261e61a35f2fda31b5","description":"Streaming media gives us access to everything instantly — but at what cost? Music professor Tom Rizzuto traces the history of physical media — from CDs and vinyl to bone music (Soviet-era records pressed onto discarded X-rays) and the near-loss of “Nosferatu” — making the case that art shouldn't just live in the cloud. (Recorded at TEDxMolloy University on ebruary 28, 2026)\n\nJoin us in person at a TED conference: https://tedtalks.social/events\nBecome a TED Member to support our mission: https://ted.com/membership\nSubscribe to a TED newsletter: https://ted.com/newsletters\n\nFollow TED! \nInstagram: https://www.instagram.com/ted\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/ted-conferences\nTikTok: https://www.tiktok.com/@tedtoks\nFacebook: https://facebook.com/TED\nX: https://www.twitter.com/TEDTalks\n\nThe TED Talks channel features the best talks and performances from the TED Conference, where the world's leading thinkers and doers give the talk of their lives in 18 minutes (or less) — plus originals, podcasts and exclusive content. Look for videos on Technology, Entertainment and Design as well as science, business, global issues, the arts and more. Visit https://TED.com for our entire library, transcripts, translations and personalized recommendations.\n\nWatch more: https://go.ted.com/tomrizzuto\n\nhttps://youtu.be/yUzvXA5HmtE \n\nTED videos may be used for non-commercial purposes under a Creative Commons License, Attribution–Non Commercial–No Derivatives (or the CC BY – NC – ND 4.0 International) and in accordance with the TED Talks Usage Policy: https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy. For more information on using TED for commercial purposes (e.g. employee learning, in a film or online course), submit a request at https://media-requests.ted.com\n\n#TED #TEDTalks #Technology","duration_seconds":505,"id":"ec98afb8-268f-4be5-9ef2-7100d52c2f8c","published_at":"2026-07-05T15:00:29+00:00","title":"The Problem with Streaming — and the Case for Physical Media | Tom Rizzuto | TED","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"yUzvXA5HmtE"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"9d44cc5901c6c312c125856d6f5d10125c2e0a23ee8e50db1b7fe0c284e38250","description":"Where does real innovation come from? Is it simply a result of smart people, big budgets, artificial intelligence, or hackathons? This talk challenges the common belief that innovation can be created by simply putting the right ingredients together. Through the experience of bringing strangers together to solve a problem in 48 hours, it explores the hidden forces that make innovation possible, including diversity of thought, recognising the right problem, collaboration, and culture. The talk reveals that innovation is not just about having the best technology or the smartest minds, but about creating the environment where new ideas can emerge, evolve, and create meaningful impact.\n Vinod Natakala Madhavan is an Engineering Leader at Genesys, where he leads global engineering initiatives focused on AI, cloud technologies, and large-scale data platforms. Over the past decade, he has built and mentored high-performing engineering teams while driving innovation across products and platforms.\n\nBeyond his technical role, Vinod is passionate about fostering a culture of innovation. He has participated in, mentored, judged, and organized numerous hackathons, transforming them from coding competitions into platforms that inspire collaboration, creativity, and continuous learning. His work has helped hundreds of engineers turn ideas into impactful solutions by creating environments where innovation can thrive. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":815,"id":"ef62fd94-cebe-4320-b8ab-da26e5e72245","published_at":"2026-09-02T15:20:08+00:00","title":"The invisible Engine of Innovation | Vinod Natakala Madhavan | TEDxPanimalar Engineering College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:02.256451+00:00","youtube_video_id":"3VHSQ-0E-DM"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"f762a9ecae0353b8a160436361bb04408df92dd6df50d13d705140d9d6d657da","description":"作为一名ICU医生，余一生的工作是在危急之中为患者守住生的希望。这看似是与死神的“拔河”，其实背后是身体的自发抗争、家属艰难的抉择与人性之中巨大的心理冲突。\n在这场演讲中，她带我们走进ICU，思考那些肉眼无法看见却至关重要的东西——那些来自细胞的奇迹，来自抉择的勇敢，和来自人性的温暖。\n她通过16年积累的故事分享，尝试回答这样的问题：在死亡与生存的夹缝中，是什么支撑了最终的奇迹？每一次抉择的背后，都有哪些看不见却无比重要的细节？ 南京鼓楼医院重症医学科副主任医师、医疗组长\n非虚构文学《只有ICU医生知道》作者 This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":574,"id":"f0a9c227-1f57-44bd-8a26-1bc1a68dd6eb","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:46:33+00:00","title":"看不见的生命力：ICU里的韧性与抉择 | 一生 余 | TEDxNanshan Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"wvva9tOcFYI"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e74e0fc4811c630161e4b96659468634a5ab7149ba3e58a2487cf8a949ca15c8","description":"Taya shares her personal experience of being an athlete but being so much more.  Taya is a student at Marist-Sion College. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":203,"id":"f0b58de8-4a86-42ae-9ab1-12213dbe0cca","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:46:20+00:00","title":"Athletes, what else are you? | Taya Smith | TEDxMarist Sion College Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"xHFeB5Br2I8"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"aced1196f1ae802d99a9de3675a30e61905af50f8890f1d73520735b5724d7fd","description":"NOTE FROM TED: This talk contains a discussion of combat, warfare and injury. TEDx events are independently organized by volunteers. The guidelines we give TEDx organizers are described in more detail here: http://storage.ted.com/tedx/manuals/tedx_content_guidelines.pdf\n\nWhy do we wait for certainty before taking action? Michael Allison explores how adversity, uncertainty, and the fear of making the wrong decision can lead to analysis paralysis. Through lessons from combat, leadership, and life, he reveals why responsible action creates feedback, learning, confidence, and ultimately clarity. Michael W. Allison is a leadership theorist, advisor, and best-selling author whose work focuses on identity, meaning, and decision-making under pressure. A U.S. Marine Corps Purple Heart veteran, he studies how individuals interpret adversity and how those interpretations shape long-term behavior, resilience, and well-being. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":597,"id":"f0ceb60c-4bdb-4340-a5ec-4f209603801f","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:07:02+00:00","title":"Why Action Creates Clarity | Michael M Allison | TEDxRaleigh","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Dkk72bHvXho"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"b2a4ca5ef486ef623b7616e88fcf5e36c38eca69123b11c10aab05c68ae82a15","description":"What would it take to build a democracy that works for everyone? Communications strategist Sally Kohn and entrepreneur Jeff Maurer discuss the systems they think need rebuilding — and what people will need to truly thrive within them. They debate whether politicians should face a wealth limit, what success should mean for those in office and whether the next generation will have more opportunities than the last.\n \nIdea Knock Down features experts with different perspectives taking on big questions — one block at a time. Made possible with the support Visit Philadelphia.","duration_seconds":55,"id":"f17be3ee-68ab-4f7c-8a1c-16bba09d268b","published_at":"2026-07-04T18:00:36+00:00","title":"What does success look like for young people? #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"z5Vj9TUlYdQ"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"5548c732daa867e161f0c486a6965e83bd093aefd7a861ccf79d3b372969ed6d","description":"How do you find common ground when people disagree? Opportunity builder Anuj Gupta and Visit Philadelphia CMO Neil Frauenglass discuss why democracy needs debate and why we all have more political power than we think. They explore whether society is divided or just disconnected, how local action makes a difference and what it takes to rebuild trust when we can't even agree on truth itself. \n\nIdea Knock Down features experts with different perspectives taking on big questions — one block at a time. Made possible with the support Visit Philadelphia.","duration_seconds":64,"id":"f1b48b7a-7072-471b-a51e-b10a3e6dee97","published_at":"2026-07-25T18:00:37+00:00","title":"Want to make a difference? Try this #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"nNIKazY6094"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"577828e229668a7a426118f2811cfe778d4060aa8f2e5b83fa692d16ec7cdb04","description":"Is Our Early Intervention System Failing Children Who Need Support Most? is a compelling TED Talk by Dr. Essence Presley that challenges the way society responds to young children with developmental delays. Drawing from decades of experience in early childhood and special education, Dr. Presley exposes the growing gap between children who qualify for early intervention services and the limited workforce available to serve them. She argues that children are not simply \"falling through the cracks\"—they are being left behind by a system that cannot keep pace with increasing demand during the most critical years of brain development.\n\nRather than focusing solely on the problem, Dr. Presley presents an innovative, workforce-based solution that expands the circle of support beyond licensed specialists. She advocates for equipping parents, childcare providers, educators, and community members with foundational skills to recognize developmental delays and provide meaningful early support. Through practical examples and a call to rethink who can be trained to help, this talk inspires educators, policymakers, healthcare professionals, and families to build a stronger, more accessible early intervention system where every trained adult becomes another opportunity for a child to thrive. I am an experienced early childhood educator and advocate for children with developmental needs. My work focuses on transforming early childhood education by integrating a broader range of professionals into the field to support children and families earlier in their developmental journey. As the founder of a Career and Technical School and a pioneer of a Registered Apprenticeship program for Special Education Paraprofessionals, I have dedicated my career to improving educational outcomes for young children. My leadership in the Education Institute for Early Intervention has given me firsthand insights into effective programs, policy needs, and the importance of a community-based approach to child development. I am passionate about empowering communities to play a more active role in early intervention and advocating for systemic changes to better support children with developmental challenges. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":611,"id":"f1b7ce63-9b3f-4b8f-a7ff-3f9f8d3845aa","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:45:03+00:00","title":"A new approach to early intervention for children | Essence Presley | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Pp9K0PB61Es"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"68bc066357a47dc0ad8028ecd8f7f848845b4d951810a3919090a3971b324aa3","description":"Racism isn't always depicted by blatant beliefs, thoughts, and actions. It is often found in subtle mannerisms and deeds that may be considered commonplace. However, blatant or subtle, racism is wrong and must be first confronted within oneself in judgement. All people are to be celebrated and diversity makes the world beautiful. Karla F. West has always been a staunch supporter and advocate of peace and respect for all people by actively increasing awareness and celebration of all people as a Minister, Mediator and conflict resolution specialist, a Life Coach and Divorce Coach. She holds a BS in Human Services Management and a MA in Christian Ministry and is a TEDx ThirdWard speaker. She also serves as a Women’s Pastor and is a member of several organizations promoting peace and civil rights. She is the proud mother of three adult children and a doting grandmother. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":731,"id":"f21ba7fa-07a9-4f38-9715-7170d7c0a85e","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:45:46+00:00","title":"You Might Be A Racist | Karla West | TEDxThird Ward Salon","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"O6DzeGME52Q"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"48e986f1e580e3567e3b954a571270f4d0a254fcc79da18cf0a16174b74aefe9","description":"Through the story of his grandmother’s kindness, John Cooper explores childhood trauma, healing, success, and the power we each have to break harmful cycles and choose what we pass on to others. John Cooper is the Founder and CEO of Cooper Tacia General Contracting Company, a multimillion-dollar commercial, industrial, and municipal construction firm based in Raleigh. After moving to North Carolina to attend NC State University, he grew the business from performing small kitchen and bathroom remodels to exceeding $100M in revenue. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":715,"id":"f221c6aa-0236-4618-8a59-c6c83d2a3ccc","published_at":"2026-09-01T16:17:47+00:00","title":"The Kindness We Pass On | John Cooper | TEDxRaleigh","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"4e1ZtuNGBPU"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"1528bf11cb6479f1cf92b7a7fd5057535dc4784b250870a0b8cd26dfad50eb59","description":"Alana explores the importance of being curious and to remain curious as we get older. Alana is a student at Marist-Sion College. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":214,"id":"f2269759-5c58-4a27-aefe-efea48d36781","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:45:50+00:00","title":"Curiosity is a superpower | Alana Lucas | TEDxMarist Sion College Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"z50Vq9Kb4DM"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"2c8202a4cd56c7fa4a2b952e59ba0d767da957332bb6a7c578c1e75c4dec3c42","description":"Malala Yousafzai has spent her life advocating for girls' education — surviving an assassination attempt at 15, meeting with world leaders and then watching hard-won progress collapse when Afghanistan fell to the Taliban in 2021. That moment of despair forced her to completely rethink what it means to create change, and what she discovered replaced her shattered optimism with something more powerful and more honest. Hear how to keep fighting for the future you want, even when hope feels lost.","duration_seconds":104,"id":"f34a9f3e-51c3-4779-bea1-72060b0710fa","published_at":"2026-06-26T17:30:23+00:00","title":"When Afghanistan fell again, here’s how Malala Yousafzai rediscovered hope #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"HaTL8icSZzk"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"a845b1c18f9e58fafdc493460fb575ed66f9aa43955731494a779eeea04db5eb","description":"We grow, we change, we excel, but one of the hardest things to do is to make amends with the person we used to be...\nTEDxRGSV Youth - 404: The Forgotten Archive  - 2025 High school student from RGSV Year 13 Student This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":567,"id":"f433d4e0-86c5-437b-9571-b743d65eaa6d","published_at":"2026-09-01T15:45:46+00:00","title":"From Something I Hid to Something I Own | Ngan Kim Ngoc Bach | TEDxRGSV Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-03T14:28:01.932329+00:00","youtube_video_id":"f4NRO_fyOv8"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"79442614e09b96a3caf99c29b2b18a06db28f67ea2c676ae4ca0186c72eb8da2","description":"Malala Yousafzai has spent her life advocating for girls' education — surviving an assassination attempt at 15, meeting with world leaders and then watching hard-won progress collapse when Afghanistan fell to the Taliban in 2021. That moment of despair forced her to completely rethink what it means to create change, and what she discovered replaced her shattered optimism with something more powerful and more honest. Hear how to keep fighting for the future you want, even when hope feels lost.","duration_seconds":48,"id":"f5a7addf-3a58-40b1-88c7-82d69c859a59","published_at":"2026-08-28T19:00:34+00:00","title":"No one changes history alone, but someone has to start #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T14:31:00.750765+00:00","youtube_video_id":"lHlhHOAuPy4"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"cfca4caf8df743299a4129c33a9d65938cfbb7abad3f8bdb74d303e014e09043","description":"Drawing on her award winning research, Joyeeta Gupta proposes the development of a global constitution. Through her talk she outlines her vision for a novel multilateral agreement that seeks to address the pressing challenges of today. Joyeeta Gupta is Professor of Environment and Development in the Global South at the University of Amsterdam and Professor at IHE-Delft Institute for Water Education. She received much of prestigious academic recognition, such as the Spinoza prize in 2023 (the highest academic recognition in the Netherlans), the Amsterdam Impact Prize in 2025 and the 2022 Piers Sellers Prize for climate research. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":996,"id":"f5ee4cc3-03f5-4af7-9bf4-af86cd44fca6","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:03:16+00:00","title":"It’s time for a global constitution | Joyeeta Gupta | TEDxAU College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"20p1WKy9lT4"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"345f58b7853429a2d9ec7296aad17abbb9ac286865e13abe195bc4ab49c4618d","description":"What was your favorite toy when you were a kid? Your answer might reveal more about you than you expect, says toy historian Chris Byrne. From Matchbox cars and Barbie to Rubik's Cube and Squishmallows, he explores how playthings reflect cultural values and influence who you become as an adult.","duration_seconds":109,"id":"f71a0a67-3b6f-43b7-a7d8-ad16b94bf643","published_at":"2026-06-28T16:00:30+00:00","title":"What was your favorite toy growing up? #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"ZmZkQ-YvM1g"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"8518de1ecc550550628a73c517205b956023a215cf733c2b55c61e7528926c85","description":"Symbols of morality are often cheaper than responsibility. Opening with China's sky-high bride price, where a ritual of respect can quietly stand in for the conditions of actual respect, this talk follows a single pattern through performative wokeness, its anti-woke reflection, nationalism that leans on borrowed glory, and even the debate over whether mahjong is heritage or gambling. Drawing on a friendship that unravelled over these very questions and on the classical ideal of \"unifying knowledge and action\", it argues that the real opposite of moral performance is responsibility, and leaves the audience with one uncomfortable question: are we trying to understand, to repair, or simply to look good? Ziyue Frank Huang is a multilingual student, writer, musician, activist, and professional mahjong player whose work moves across politics, philosophy, language, and art. He self-identifies as liberal, yet his political convictions are shaped by an intense inner conflict: a refusal to mistake ideological slogans for justice, and a sharp wariness toward hypocrisy in any political camp. He frequently brings political debate into his music, treating composition not only as artistic expression but also as argument and critique. In his home city of Nanchang, he directly organized legal advancement efforts that led to changes in municipal policy and law. Beyond activism, Frank is passionate about spreading philosophical thought through literature, ancient linguistics, and constructed languages, which remain among his deepest intellectual hobbies. He is also one of the first Chinese professional mahjong players in Japan’s Saikouisen Professional Mahjong League. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":1149,"id":"fa3ac142-e61a-4007-b0d3-0e86a225cae7","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:15:12+00:00","title":"Is It Wokeness, Compassion, Hypocrisy, or Egotism? | Ziyue Huang | TEDxGCGS Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"RmAiirt9ZMk"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"79d64a01abf43756d908dd6e078df9fe211eb5055c6fa9c3d049b39106b6fdea","description":"How does social media shape the way young people see themselves?\nIn a world where likes, comments, followers, views, and online approval can influence how we feel about ourselves, our digital lives are increasingly becoming part of our identity. For today’s digital natives, social media is not simply a platform for communication—it can shape self-image, confidence, belonging, comparison, and self-worth.\nThis talk explores the psychology of digital identity and the impact of online validation on young people’s emotional well-being. It examines why we seek approval in digital spaces, how social comparison affects self-esteem, and what happens when our online identity begins to influence how we understand our real-world identity.\nDrawing on psychological research and insights from digital well-being, education, and youth development, the talk offers practical, evidence-based strategies for developing a healthier relationship with social media and technology.\n\nThe goal is not to reject the digital world, but to help young people navigate it with greater self-awareness, emotional resilience, and agency.\nBecause in a world where we are constantly being seen online, an important question remains:\nWho are we when we stop seeking validation from others?\nKeywords: Digital Identity, Psychology of Social Media, Social Media and Mental Health, Youth Mental Health, Digital Wellbeing, Online Validation, Self-Worth, Social Comparison, Self-Esteem, Digital Literacy, Emotional Resilience, Teen Mental Health, Psychology of Technology\n Aaron Bhandari is an A-Level student at Xavier International College and a dynamic young individual with diverse interests and accomplishments. A competitive swimmer who has represented Nepal internationally, Aaron is also a musician, an enthusiastic public speaker, and the former Student Council President of Xavier International School. His journey reflects a strong blend of discipline, creativity, confidence, and youth leadership. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":668,"id":"fa9db778-2494-4be3-b6bd-e847afdd6bf4","published_at":"2026-09-03T15:12:20+00:00","title":"The Psychology of Digital Identity | Aaron Bhandari | TEDxXavier International School Youth","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"NROlkrls3ig"},{"channel_name":"TED","content_fingerprint":"5371c56c3d7f3b54136472618942974eb028cc1c2de350537033a1c8cc68ab4d","description":"Our lives, our cultures, are composed of many overlapping stories. Novelist Chimamanda Adichie tells the story of how she found her authentic cultural voice -- and warns that if we hear only a single story about another person or country, we risk a critical misunderstanding.","duration_seconds":99,"id":"fd1c782b-814d-41c7-86ce-28ede535b73c","published_at":"2026-08-15T19:00:20+00:00","title":"No culture or country is only one thing #TEDTalks","updated_at":"2026-09-04T08:30:45.89357+00:00","youtube_video_id":"Z2oIPjnlK2I"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"eb7047e32f8efe54cf2e953977bda662cb298a9f91ced6be412541a11b0614d9","description":"Using various mathematical and computational methods, coupled with experiments and observations, Vitor delves into the intricacies of systems involving humans, resources, and governing institutions. Vítor V. Vasconcelos is an Associate Professor at the University of Amsterdam, working at the Computational Science Lab at the Informatics Institute (IvI), collaborating with a great local team and many great fellows worldwide. In this era of Complexity, I develop new models of human collective behavior to anticipate the intended and unintended consequences of interventions on complex social systems. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":788,"id":"fe096e43-6e15-40a9-8af9-7aaf8d463829","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:02:22+00:00","title":"Unravelling complexity | Vítor Vasconcelos | TEDxAU College","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"_QOsiGDrWCE"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"e034af3f19cd7fb0ce646a9544c7e18bf6e2517b9520ac7dfb2044551ec08993","description":"Bir gün size en iyi olduğunuz şeyi bırakmanızı söyleseler kim olurdunuz?\n\nBirkan Batuk, basketbol sahalarından başlayan ve bugünkü yolculuğuna uzanan hikâyesinden yola çıkarak dönüşümün hayatımızdaki yerini anlatıyor. Birkan Batuk, 18 yıl boyunca Türk ve Avrupa basketbolunun en üst seviyelerinde mücadele etmiş eski profesyonel basketbolcudur.\n\nTürkiye Millî Takımı formasını 162 kez giyen Batuk, Anadolu Efes ile altı EuroLeague sezonunda mücadele etti. 2015 ve 2018 yıllarında Türkiye Kupası şampiyonluğu yaşarken, 2015 yılında Cumhurbaşkanlığı Kupası Finali’nin MVP’si seçildi. Ayrıca EuroCup finalinde mücadele etti; kariyeri boyunca Darüşşafaka, Pınar Karşıyaka ve Beşiktaş gibi önemli kulüplerde forma giydi.\n\n2006 yılında U16 Avrupa Şampiyonluğu yaşayan Batuk, millî takım kariyerine genç yaşta başladı.\n\n2024 yılında profesyonel basketbol kariyerini noktalayan Batuk, Türkiye Basketbol Federasyonu bünyesinde TB2L ve EBBL Lig Direktörü olarak görev yapmaya başladı. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":583,"id":"ff274150-71a5-4880-a0d2-c9368a377b9a","published_at":"2026-09-04T15:38:13+00:00","title":"Bildiğiniz Hayat Bittiğinde | Birkan Batuk | TEDxMarmara University","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"sgi9GpXA_b0"},{"channel_name":"TEDx Talks","content_fingerprint":"2589a8a82bfec88b1c719c66bbd583019a887430bd154f3006a5bd4da3c6b465","description":"Nesta palestra, Emilio Sanhueza compartilha sua trajetória pessoal como filho de pais de diferentes nacionalidades e sua experiência de migração para o Brasil, refletindo sobre como a globalização conecta pessoas, culturas e histórias. Apesar de sua formação na área de engenharia, ele mostra que a arte e a música não são caminhos opostos à ciência, mas expressões complementares da experiência humana.\n\nAo revisitar sua própria relação com a música, iniciada ainda na infância, Emilio demonstra como essa linguagem atravessou fronteiras culturais e se tornou um elo fundamental entre os membros de sua família. Para ele, a música vai além das palavras, permitindo comunicar emoções, memórias e sentimentos que muitas vezes não podem ser plenamente expressos pela linguagem verbal.\n\nAcompanhado de seu violoncelo, o palestrante apresenta obras de Johann Sebastian Bach e destaca como a música funciona como uma linguagem universal capaz de conectar pessoas de diferentes origens. Ao explorar as características do violoncelo e sua proximidade com a voz humana, Emilio convida o público a refletir sobre o poder transformador da arte e sobre a capacidade da música de permanecer viva na memória e no coração das pessoas ao longo da vida. Emilio Augusto da Mota Sanhueza is a Spanish-Chilean Computer Engineering student at the Federal University of Goiás (UFG), with interests in offensive security, artificial intelligence, systems programming in C, and DevOps. Alongside his technical studies, he is also a musician and composer with formal training in piano, music theory, and cello. He has performed with youth and symphonic orchestras in Goiás and taken part in national and international tours, including the Young Euro Classic in Berlin and concerts in Salzburg, Prague, Budapest, and Vienna. His trajectory brings together engineering, computing, and music, connecting analytical thinking, creativity, and multidisciplinary practice. This talk was given at a TEDx event using the TED conference format but independently organized by a local community. Learn more at https://www.ted.com/tedx","duration_seconds":917,"id":"ffd91767-dc72-4ab6-9edc-f11668226434","published_at":"2026-09-03T16:00:46+00:00","title":"Além das Palavras: O Idioma Universal da Música | Emilio Augusto da Mota Sanhueza | TEDxGoiânia","updated_at":"2026-09-04T20:31:37.049342+00:00","youtube_video_id":"OC9bMv4y9jw"}]}$dataset$::jsonb;
    seed_snapshot UUID := (dataset->>'snapshot_id')::uuid;
BEGIN
    LOCK TABLE annotation_pair_scores, annotation_labels IN SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE MODE;

    -- Fail on identity collisions rather than overwriting an existing source record.
    IF EXISTS (
        SELECT 1 FROM jsonb_to_recordset(dataset->'videos') AS item(id uuid, youtube_video_id text)
        JOIN videos existing ON existing.id = item.id OR existing.youtube_video_id = item.youtube_video_id
        WHERE existing.id <> item.id OR existing.youtube_video_id <> item.youtube_video_id
    ) THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Match Lab seed video identity conflicts with existing data';
    END IF;

    INSERT INTO videos (
        id, youtube_video_id, channel_name, title, youtube_url, thumbnail_url,
        description, published_at, duration_seconds, updated_at, content_fingerprint
    )
    SELECT id, youtube_video_id, channel_name, title,
           'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=' || youtube_video_id,
           'https://i.ytimg.com/vi/' || youtube_video_id || '/hqdefault.jpg',
           description, published_at, duration_seconds, updated_at, content_fingerprint
    FROM jsonb_to_recordset(dataset->'videos') AS item(
        id uuid, youtube_video_id text, channel_name text, title text,
        description text, published_at timestamptz, duration_seconds integer, updated_at timestamptz,
        content_fingerprint text
    )
    ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

    INSERT INTO annotation_profiles (id, summary, topics, source)
    SELECT id, summary, topics, 'synthetic'
    FROM jsonb_to_recordset(dataset->'profiles') AS item(id uuid, summary text, topics text[])
    ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

    -- Existing profile versions and video snapshots remain untouched.
    INSERT INTO annotation_videos (video_id, title, description, channel_name, source_updated_at)
    SELECT id, title, description, channel_name, updated_at
    FROM jsonb_to_recordset(dataset->'videos') AS item(
        id uuid, title text, description text, channel_name text, updated_at timestamptz
    )
    ON CONFLICT (video_id) DO NOTHING;

    INSERT INTO annotation_snapshots (
        id, snapshot_sha256, source_commit, source_migrations,
        profile_count, video_count, pair_count, provenance
    ) VALUES (
        seed_snapshot, dataset->>'snapshot_sha256', dataset->>'source_commit',
        ARRAY(SELECT jsonb_array_elements_text(dataset->'source_migrations')),
        100, 296, 29600,
        jsonb_build_object(
            'dataset', 'match-lab-v2', 'extracted_at', dataset->>'extracted_at',
            'curation_method', dataset->>'curation_method', 'selected_count', 200,
            'category_counts', '{"strong_match":50,"close_call":60,"near_miss":40,"hard_negative":50}'::jsonb,
            'hash_contract', 'SHA256 of canonical source and curation artifact, excluding migration provenance',
            'existing_snapshots', 'Preserved; source extraction is recorded in source-snapshot.json',
            'scores', 'Assistant judgment estimates, not calibrated probabilities'
        )
    ) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

    INSERT INTO annotation_pair_scores (
        profile_id, video_id, relevance_score, difficulty_score, scoring_model,
        curated, predicted_fit, close_call, selection_rationale, decision_summary,
        scoring_model_version, snapshot_id, snapshot_provenance
    )
    SELECT item.profile_id, item.video_id, relevance_score, difficulty_score,
           dataset->>'scoring_model', TRUE, predicted_fit, close_call,
           selection_rationale, decision_summary, dataset->>'scoring_model_version', seed_snapshot,
           jsonb_build_object(
               'stable_id', item.profile_id::text || ':' || item.video_id::text,
               'category', category, 'topic_area', profile.topics[1],
               'snapshot_sha256', dataset->>'snapshot_sha256',
               'assessment_source', 'assistant', 'human_adjudicated', false
           )
    FROM jsonb_to_recordset(dataset->'pairs') AS item(
        profile_id uuid, video_id uuid, relevance_score double precision,
        difficulty_score double precision, predicted_fit text, close_call boolean,
        selection_rationale text, decision_summary text, category text
    )
    JOIN annotation_profiles profile ON profile.id = item.profile_id
    ON CONFLICT (profile_id, video_id) DO NOTHING;

    IF (SELECT count(*) FROM annotation_pair_scores WHERE snapshot_id = seed_snapshot) <> 200 THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Match Lab seed collides with existing pair assessments';
    END IF;

    -- Retain every existing reviewer judgment and close any already-reviewed seed pair.
    WITH counts AS (
        SELECT labels.profile_id, labels.video_id, labels.label, count(*) AS votes
        FROM annotation_labels labels
        JOIN annotation_pair_scores score USING (profile_id, video_id)
        WHERE score.snapshot_id = seed_snapshot
        GROUP BY labels.profile_id, labels.video_id, labels.label
    ), outcomes AS (
        SELECT profile_id, video_id, sum(votes) AS total,
               (array_agg(label ORDER BY votes DESC, label) FILTER (WHERE votes >= 2))[1] AS consensus
        FROM counts GROUP BY profile_id, video_id
    )
    UPDATE annotation_pair_scores score
    SET queue_status = CASE WHEN outcomes.consensus IS NOT NULL THEN 'consensus'
                            WHEN outcomes.total >= 3 THEN 'escalated' ELSE 'open' END,
        consensus_label = outcomes.consensus
    FROM outcomes
    WHERE score.profile_id = outcomes.profile_id AND score.video_id = outcomes.video_id
      AND score.snapshot_id = seed_snapshot;
END
$seed$;
